// 190 chapter review questions extracted from the uploaded 2026 study guide.
// The guide's 180-question full-length mock already exists in QDATA.pmpsims;
// it is normalized below and added without duplicating its text in this file.
const BOOK_REVIEW_BANK=[{"n":1,"q":"A company is developing a new mobile banking app with a fixed budget and timeline, but stakeholders acknowledge that customer preferences are unknown and expected to shift significantly during development. The feature set will be refined based on user feedback gathered throughout the project. Which development approach is most appropriate for this project?","opts":{"A":"Predictive, because the budget and timeline are fixed and must be carefully controlled.","B":"Predictive, because the project has a defined start and end date.","C":"Adaptive, because requirements are unclear and expected to evolve based on stakeholder feedback.","D":"Hybrid, because the project involves both software development and stakeholder engagement."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Adaptive, because requirements are unclear and expected to evolve based on stakeholder feedback. Explanation: The development approach is chosen based on how well requirements are understood, not on whether time and cost are fixed. Fixed time and cost with evolving scope is actually a hallmark of adaptive projects. When customer preferences are unknown and expected to shift, adaptive allows the team to deliver incrementally, gather feedback, and adjust as they go. Why the other options are incorrect: Fixed budget and timeline do not indicate a predictive approach. Adaptive projects typically operate within fixed time and cost constraints while allowing scope to evolve. Having a defined start and end date is true of all projects by definition and has no bearing on the development approach. Stakeholder engagement and software development exist in all approaches. Hybrid is appropriate when different parts of the same project have different levels of certainty. The entire project here has unclear, evolving requirements, pointing clearly to adaptive.","id":"book-terms-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Hybrid"},{"n":2,"q":"A construction company is building a new 20-story office tower. The project was planned in full detail before breaking ground, with a fixed scope, schedule, and budget. To generate revenue sooner, the company plans to open and lease floors 1 through 5 to tenants while the remaining floors are still being built. What type of development approach is this project using?","opts":{"A":"Predictive, because the full scope was defined upfront and the project follows a detailed plan.","B":"Adaptive, because the project is delivering value incrementally to stakeholders.","C":"Hybrid, because the project delivers work in increments while maintaining a fixed budget.","D":"Adaptive, because tenants are providing feedback that will shape the remaining floors."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Predictive, because the full scope was defined upfront and the project follows a detailed plan. Explanation: Incremental delivery means releasing portions of the product before the whole is complete, but this does not make a project adaptive. What defines the approach is how scope is managed. This project had its full scope, schedule, and budget defined upfront with no indication that requirements are evolving based on feedback. Delivering floors incrementally is a delivery strategy, not a methodology. Why the other options are incorrect: Delivering value in increments does not necessarily indicate an adaptive approach. Adaptive projects are defined by evolving scope and iterative feedback cycles, not simply by phased delivery of a pre-planned product. Hybrid combines predictive and adaptive elements based on different levels of certainty across parts of the project. There is no uncertainty described here. There is no indication in the scenario that tenant feedback is being gathered and used to change the design of remaining floors. This option introduces an assumption not supported by the scenario.","id":"book-terms-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Hybrid"},{"n":3,"q":"A project manager with extensive agile experience is assigned to lead a pharmaceutical company’s effort to build a new drug manufacturing facility. The facility must comply with strict government regulations, construction permits must be approved, and engineering requirements are fully documented. The project manager recommends using an Agile approach. Is this the right recommendation?","opts":{"A":"Yes, because adaptive approaches allow the team to respond to unexpected construction challenges.","B":"Yes, because all projects benefit from iterative delivery and frequent stakeholder feedback.","C":"No, because pharmaceutical projects must always use a hybrid approach to satisfy regulatory requirements.","D":"No, because the requirements are stable and well-defined, making a predictive approach the better fit."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: No, because the requirements are stable and well-defined, making a predictive approach the better fit. Explanation: The development approach should be chosen based on the stability and clarity of requirements, not on assumptions about flexibility or universal best practices. When requirements are clear, stable, and heavily regulated, a predictive approach (or sometimes hybrid) is the appropriate choice. Why the other options are incorrect: Flexibility is not the primary criterion for selecting an approach. Stable, well-documented requirements call for predictive regardless of what construction challenges may arise. No single approach benefits all projects equally. PMI expects project managers to match the approach to the specific context. Hybrid is appropriate when different parts of the same project have different levels of certainty. The entire project here has stable, well-defined requirements with no adaptive element needed.","id":"book-terms-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Hybrid"},{"n":4,"q":"A project team has just completed the final deliverable for a software implementation project. The project manager is unsure who has the authority to formally accept the deliverable and approve a scope change that was submitted earlier in the week. Who has the authority to do both?","opts":{"A":"The project manager, because they are responsible for the overall project outcome.","B":"The functional manager, because they control the resources that produced the deliverable.","C":"The sponsor, because they provide resources and support and are authorized to approve deliverables and changes.","D":"The PMO, because it approves scope changes and deliverables across the organization."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The sponsor, because they provide resources and support and are authorized to approve deliverables and changes. Explanation: The sponsor is the individual who funds the project and holds the authority to approve deliverables and authorize changes. While other stakeholders play important roles, formal acceptance and change authorization belong to the sponsor unless explicitly delegated to the customer. Why the other options are incorrect: The project manager is responsible for managing the project and delivering outcomes, but does not have authority to formally accept deliverables or approve scope changes. Functional managers control resources within their departments but have no formal authority over project deliverables or change approval. The PMO often plays a role in change control processes depending on its type, but it does not have authority to formally accept project deliverables.","id":"book-terms-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project manager is leading an agile software development project. During a sprint review, a team member asks the project manager to assign tasks for the next sprint and resolve a technical dispute between two developers. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Assign the tasks and resolve the dispute, because the project manager is responsible for the project outcome.","B":"Facilitate the team in self-organizing to assign tasks and resolve the dispute themselves.","C":"Escalate both issues to the sponsor, because decisions on agile projects require senior authorization.","D":"Defer both issues to the product owner, because the product owner manages all team activities on agile projects."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Facilitate the team in self-organizing to assign tasks and resolve the dispute themselves. Explanation: On agile projects, the project manager operates as a servant leader, supporting and enabling the team rather than directing them. Agile teams are self-organizing, meaning they should be assigning their own work and resolving their own conflicts. The project manager’s role is to remove obstacles, facilitate collaboration, and create conditions for the team to succeed. Why the other options are incorrect: Directly assigning tasks and resolving disputes reflects a traditional, command-and-control management style. On agile projects, this undermines team autonomy and self-organization, which are core principles of adaptive project management. Escalating routine team activities to the sponsor is inappropriate. The sponsor is not involved in day-to-day team operations on any project type. The product owner is responsible for prioritizing the product backlog, not managing team activities or resolving interpersonal disputes. These are outside the product owner’s role.","id":"book-terms-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"An agile software development team is midway through a project when three new feature requests come in from different stakeholders. The project manager is unsure how to handle the competing requests and asks who should decide which features get added to the next sprint. Who is responsible for making this decision?","opts":{"A":"The project manager, because they are accountable for the overall project outcome.","B":"The project team, because agile teams are self-organizing and decide their own work.","C":"The product owner, because they are responsible for prioritizing the product backlog.","D":"The sponsor, because they fund the project and have final authority over scope decisions."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The product owner, because they are responsible for prioritizing the product backlog. Explanation: On agile projects, the product owner owns and prioritizes the product backlog, which is the list of work items to be completed. When competing requests come in, it is the product owner’s responsibility to evaluate and rank them based on business value, stakeholder needs, and project goals. Why the other options are incorrect: The project manager on an agile project plays a servant leadership role, facilitating and supporting the team. Prioritizing features is not within the project manager’s scope on an agile project. While agile teams can contribute to prioritization decisions, the product owner remains ultimately responsible for deciding which features to build and in what order. The sponsor provides funding and high-level oversight but is not involved in day-to-day backlog prioritization decisions.","id":"book-terms-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is leading a large infrastructure project that is currently in its third phase. During execution, the team discovers a much more efficient method for completing a recurring task that will be performed several more times before the project closes. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Document the finding in the lessons learned register and apply the improved method to the remaining work.","B":"Wait until the project is complete before documenting lessons learned to ensure all findings are captured together.","C":"Present the finding to the sponsor at project closure so it can be considered for future projects.","D":"Note the finding informally and remind the team to include it in the final project report."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Document the finding in the lessons learned register and apply the improved method to the remaining work. Explanation: Lessons learned are recorded throughout the project, not just at the end. When a better approach is discovered mid-project, it should be documented immediately and applied to remaining work so the project benefits from the improvement. Why the other options are incorrect: Waiting until project closure to document lessons learned misses the opportunity to apply improvements to the current project. Lessons learned are captured continuously, not just at the end. Presenting findings only at closure limits their value to future projects and eliminates any benefit to the work still remaining on the current project. Informal documentation does not meet the standard for capturing lessons learned. The lessons learned register is a formal project document that ensures findings are preserved, accessible, and usable on future projects.","id":"book-terms-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A construction project to build a highway overpass was completed on time, within budget, and met all quality standards. Two years later, the overpass was demolished because it could no longer accommodate the volume of traffic in the area. How should this project be evaluated?","opts":{"A":"Successful, because it was delivered on time, within budget, and met quality standards.","B":"Unsuccessful, because the project failed to deliver expected long term value.","C":"Partially successful, because it was managed well but failed to deliver lasting value.","D":"Successful, because the project was completed in accordance with all requirements."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Partially successful, because it was managed well but failed to deliver lasting value. Explanation: Project success has two dimensions: the success of project management processes and the success of project outcomes. This project performed well on the first dimension by meeting scope, schedule, and cost constraints. However, it failed on the second dimension by not delivering lasting value. A truly successful project must satisfy both dimensions. Why the other options are incorrect: Meeting time, budget, and quality standards reflects strong project management but represents only one dimension of success. A project that is demolished shortly after completion cannot be considered fully successful regardless of how well it was managed. While the project did fail to deliver long term value, labeling it entirely unsuccessful ignores that it was well managed and met all of its process constraints. PMI recognizes both dimensions independently. Completing a project according to its requirements addresses only the process dimension of success. PMI expects project managers to consider whether the outcome delivered meaningful, lasting value, not just whether the defined requirements were met.","id":"book-terms-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project team has finished developing and deploying a new customer portal for a retail company. The portal was delivered on time and within budget, and all requirements were met. However, six months after launch, adoption rates are near zero because customers find the interface confusing and continue to use phone support instead. What does this scenario best illustrate?","opts":{"A":"The project failed because it was not delivered according to the sponsor’s requirements.","B":"The project succeeded because it met all defined scope, schedule, and cost constraints.","C":"A deliverable alone does not create value unless it enables the intended outcome.","D":"The project team should have included more detailed requirements during planning."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: A deliverable alone does not create value unless it enables the intended outcome. Explanation: Value is not created by completing a deliverable. It is created when that deliverable enables the intended outcome, in this case, customers successfully using the portal to reduce reliance on phone support. The portal was built and deployed, but it did not achieve its intended outcome. Every project is an investment, and the deliverable must generate benefits that justify that investment. Why the other options are incorrect: The scenario does not indicate the project failed to meet the sponsor’s requirements. The issue is not one of scope compliance but of value realization. Meeting requirements and delivering value are not the same thing. Meeting scope, schedule, and cost constraints reflects a strong project management process but does not guarantee value. A deliverable that no one uses does not justify the investment regardless of how efficiently it was produced. Adding more detailed requirements would not have addressed the root issue, which was that the end product did not meet the needs of its users. Value is driven by outcomes.","id":"book-terms-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A company has just hired a new project manager. When she arrives, she finds that the PMO provides her with a library of templates, process guides, and training resources, but does not require her to use any specific framework or approve her project plans. What type of PMO is this?","opts":{"A":"Directive, because the PMO is providing the project manager with the tools she needs to succeed.","B":"Controlling, because the PMO is actively supporting the project manager’s work.","C":"Balanced, because the PMO shares authority between the project manager and the organization.","D":"Supportive, because the PMO offers resources and guidance without imposing requirements."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Supportive, because the PMO offers resources and guidance without imposing requirements. Explanation: A supportive PMO provides templates, training, and guidance but does not mandate their use. It has low authority over projects and exists primarily to assist project managers rather than govern them. The key indicator in this scenario is that the PMO offers resources without requiring the project manager to follow a specific framework or seek approval. Why the other options are incorrect: A directive PMO assigns project managers and directs their work with high authority. Simply providing tools and resources does not make a PMO directive. A controlling PMO requires project managers to follow specific frameworks and use mandated templates. The PMO in this scenario imposes no such requirements. Balanced is not a PMO type. The three PMO types are supportive, controlling, and directive.","id":"book-terms-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager is overseeing an office renovation project with a scope baseline of painting and carpeting three floors, a schedule baseline of four weeks, and a cost baseline of $45,000. Midway through the project, the sponsor requests that a fourth floor be added to the scope. The project manager assesses the impact and determines the change will require one additional week and $12,000. After the sponsor formally approves the change request, what are the new baselines?","opts":{"A":"Scope: four floors, Schedule: four weeks, Cost: $45,000 until the phase is closed and updated.","B":"Scope: four floors, Schedule: five weeks, Cost: $57,000.","C":"Scope: four floors, Schedule: four weeks, Cost: $57,000 because schedule changes require a separate approval.","D":"The baselines remain unchanged until the project is complete and final costs are reconciled."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Scope: four floors, Schedule: five weeks, Cost: $57,000. Explanation: A baseline is the original plan plus all approved changes. Once a change request is formally approved, all affected baselines must be updated to reflect the new plan. In this case, the scope, schedule, and cost are all impacted and must all be updated together. Why the other options are incorrect: Baselines are updated as soon as a change is approved, not at the end of a phase. All three baselines are affected by this change. Scope, schedule, and cost are interconnected constraints. Approving a scope change without updating the schedule baseline would produce an inaccurate picture of project performance. Baselines are living documents updated throughout the project as changes are approved. Waiting until project completion to reconcile them defeats their entire purpose as a performance measurement tool.","id":"book-terms-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A technology company wants to become the leading global provider of cybersecurity software within five years. To achieve this, they are simultaneously developing a new product line, training their global sales force, and launching a worldwide marketing campaign. Each effort has its own project manager, timeline, and budget. What best describes how these efforts are organized?","opts":{"A":"A program, because all three efforts share a common goal and are being managed together.","B":"A portfolio, because the three efforts are aligned to a long term strategic business goal and include multiple projects.","C":"A project, because the overall effort has a defined start and end date and will create unique value.","D":"A program within a portfolio, because the sales training and marketing campaigns are subprojects of the product development effort."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: A portfolio, because the three efforts are aligned to a long term strategic business goal and include multiple projects. Explanation: A portfolio is a collection of projects and programs managed together to achieve a strategic business goal. Each individual effort is its own project, and together they form a portfolio aligned to that goal. Why the other options are incorrect: A program is a collection of projects with a common goal, but programs are generally used when the projects are interdependent. A portfolio is the more accurate fit here because the efforts are aligned to a strategic business objective rather than a single unified deliverable. While the overall effort does have a defined end goal and will create value, it consists of multiple independent projects managed separately. The sales training and marketing campaigns are not subprojects of the product development effort. They are independent projects aligned to the same strategic goal, which is the defining characteristic of a portfolio.","id":"book-terms-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager is planning a six month software deployment project with four distinct phases. A junior project manager on the team suggests that the planning activities should only occur during the planning phase and that monitoring and controlling should be saved for later in the project. What should the project manager tell her?","opts":{"A":"Each phase of the project contains all five focus areas, including planning and monitoring and controlling.","B":"She is correct that planning should be completed before execution begins to ensure a solid foundation.","C":"Monitoring and controlling is only necessary during the executing phase when the most work is being performed.","D":"Focus areas are performed sequentially, so planning must be fully completed before monitoring begins."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Each phase of the project contains all five focus areas, including planning and monitoring and controlling. Explanation: Focus areas are not phases and are not performed sequentially. Every phase of a project contains all five focus areas. Planning happens continuously throughout the project, and monitoring and controlling begins as soon as work starts, not after execution is underway. Why the other options are incorrect: Completing all planning before execution is a common misconception. While predictive projects emphasize upfront planning, planning is an ongoing activity that occurs throughout every phase of a project, not just during the dedicated planning phase. Monitoring and controlling is not limited to the executing phase. It runs in parallel with all other focus areas across every phase of the project to ensure performance stays aligned with the plan. Focus areas are not sequential. They overlap and recur throughout the project. Treating them as a linear sequence would cause critical activities like planning and monitoring to be neglected during phases where they are still very much needed.","id":"book-terms-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"A project manager is overseeing a software development project when the customer requests a minor expansion of the application’s reporting features. The project manager reviews the request and determines it is small enough that it will not affect the project schedule or budget. She approves the change and updates the scope baseline without submitting a formal change request for the other constraints. Three weeks later, the development team informs her that the additional reporting features require integration with a third party data system, pushing the project two weeks behind schedule and $15,000 over budget. What does this scenario best illustrate?","opts":{"A":"The project manager should have rejected the change since all scope changes carry risk.","B":"The customer should have communicated the full technical requirements of the request upfront.","C":"Minor scope changes do not require formal change control if the project manager approves them.","D":"Constraints are interconnected, and even a small scope change can have an unexpected impact on schedule and cost."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Constraints are interconnected, and even a small scope change can have an unexpected impact on schedule and cost. Explanation: No scope change exists in isolation. The project manager’s error was assuming the change was self-contained without fully assessing its downstream impact on all constraints before approving it. Why the other options are incorrect: Changes are a normal part of projects and should be evaluated and managed through proper change control, not avoided entirely. While clearer communication from the customer would have helped, the project manager is responsible for assessing the full impact of any change before approving it. No scope change is too minor to bypass impact assessment. Formal change control exists precisely to surface hidden dependencies and protect all project baselines, not just scope.","id":"book-terms-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A hospital has recently implemented a new electronic health records system. The implementation is complete and the system is now live. The IT department is now responsible for maintaining the system, resolving daily user issues, and pushing routine software updates on an ongoing basis. Which of the following best describes the current state of this work?","opts":{"A":"A project, because the IT department is still working with the same system that was implemented.","B":"Operations, because the work is ongoing with no defined end date and the output is not unique.","C":"A project, because the work requires specialized IT skills and impacts the entire organization.","D":"Operations, because the IT department is following a standardized process to manage the system."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Operations, because the work is ongoing with no defined end date and the output is not unique. Explanation: Once implementation closed, the work transitioned from a project to operations. Operations are continuous, have no defined end date, and produce repeated non-unique outputs. Working with the same system as the original project does not make ongoing maintenance a project. Why the other options are incorrect: A project is temporary and produces a unique output. Routine maintenance and support meet neither criterion. Specialized skills and organizational impact apply to both projects and operations and do not determine which one this is. Following a standardized process is also true of both. The defining characteristics of operations are that they are ongoing and non-unique, not that they follow a process.","id":"book-terms-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":16,"q":"A project manager is leading a network infrastructure upgrade. She works on the project full time and has been given moderate to high authority over the resources assigned to her project. Functional managers still exist in the organization but have limited influence over project decisions. Which organizational structure does this best describe?","opts":{"A":"Balanced matrix, because the project manager and functional managers share authority equally.","B":"Functional, because functional managers still exist in the organization.","C":"Strong matrix, because the project manager works full time and has moderate to high authority over resources.","D":"Projectized, because the project manager has primary authority over resources."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Strong matrix, because the project manager works full time and has moderate to high authority over resources. Explanation: A strong matrix gives the project manager full time status and moderate to high authority over resources while functional managers still exist but with limited influence. This is also the default organizational structure assumed on the PMP exam when none is specified. Why the other options are incorrect: In a balanced matrix, the project manager and functional manager share roughly equal authority over resources. The scenario describes the project manager having the clear authority advantage. The presence of functional managers alone does not indicate a functional organization. Functional managers exist across multiple structure types including all matrix variations. In a projectized structure, the project manager has full control over resources and functional managers typically do not exist. The scenario describes functional managers with limited but existing influence, which points to strong matrix rather than projectized.","id":"book-terms-16","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":17,"q":"A manufacturing company is considering a project to retrofit its facilities with cleaner production equipment. The project will reduce toxic waste output and improve working conditions for employees, but the financial projections show only a modest return on investment over ten years. The CFO argues the project cannot be approved because the financial return does not meet the company’s standard threshold. How should this project be evaluated?","opts":{"A":"The CFO is correct because all projects must meet the company’s financial return threshold to be approved.","B":"The project’s value should be assessed by weighing its financial return alongside its impact on employees and the environment.","C":"The project should be deferred until a version can be developed that meets the company’s financial return threshold.","D":"The project cannot be evaluated fairly because intangible benefits like worker safety are too difficult to quantify."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The project’s value should be assessed by weighing its financial return alongside its impact on employees and the environment. Explanation: The triple bottom line evaluates projects across the dimensions of profit, people, and the planet. Financial return is still a legitimate and important dimension, but it is not the only one. Why the other options are incorrect: Financial thresholds are one tool for evaluating projects, but the triple bottom line framework specifically exists to ensure decisions are not made on financial grounds alone. Deferring the project until it meets a financial threshold treats financial return as the only valid criterion, ignoring the social and environmental value the project delivers. Intangible benefits are a recognized and legitimate part of project value. PMI explicitly includes social impact, employee wellbeing, and environmental outcomes as valid measures of success even when they are difficult to quantify precisely.","id":"book-terms-17","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":18,"q":"A project manager is overseeing a construction project for a new medical office building. Her team has identified two sets of guidelines that apply to the project. The first was issued by the local government and specifies mandatory fire safety requirements. The second was published by a professional engineering association and outlines recommended best practices for building design. Which statement best describes how the project manager should treat these two sets of guidelines?","opts":{"A":"The fire safety requirements must be followed, while the engineering best practices are recommended but not legally required.","B":"Both sets of guidelines are mandatory because they both apply directly to the project.","C":"The engineering best practices take priority because they are issued by a recognized professional body.","D":"Neither set of guidelines is binding until the project sponsor formally incorporates them into the project plan."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: The fire safety requirements must be followed, while the engineering best practices are recommended but not legally required. Explanation: Regulations are mandatory requirements imposed by law or government authority. Failure to comply can result in fines or penalties. Standards, on the other hand, are guidelines approved by recognized bodies that represent industry best practices. Compliance with standards is optional, though following them is generally advisable. Why the other options are incorrect: Not all project guidelines carry the same weight. The source and legal standing of a guideline determines whether it is mandatory or optional. Standards are optional regardless of the credibility of the issuing organization. Regulations apply to a project by law and do not require sponsor approval to be binding. A sponsor cannot opt out of legal requirements by excluding them from the project plan.","id":"book-terms-18","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":19,"q":"A project manager has just been assigned to lead a large urban transit system overhaul. The project is in its earliest stages and the team has developed only a high level estimate of costs and a general sense of the work involved. A stakeholder asks why the project plan is not more detailed and pushes for a fully defined scope and precise budget before any work begins. How should the project manager respond?","opts":{"A":"The project manager should pause work until a detailed scope and budget can be established to avoid cost overruns.","B":"The project manager should escalate to the sponsor to get approval for a more detailed planning effort before proceeding.","C":"The project manager should hire a consultant to develop a detailed project plan since the team lacks the information needed.","D":"The project manager should explain that details will emerge progressively as the project moves forward and that high level estimates are appropriate at this stage."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: The project manager should explain that details will emerge progressively as the project moves forward and that high level estimates are appropriate at this stage. Explanation: Progressive elaboration is the process by which project details become clearer and more refined as the project advances. At the start of a project, high level estimates and general scope definitions are expected and appropriate. The key is knowing what information is needed and when. Why the other options are incorrect: Pausing work until all details are defined is unnecessary. Full detail at project initiation is rarely possible and not required to begin productive work. Escalating to the sponsor is not warranted here. The lack of detail at this stage is expected and normal, not a problem requiring senior intervention. Hiring a consultant to force detailed planning prematurely does not solve the underlying issue. Detail emerges through the natural progression of the project, not by bringing in outside resources to manufacture it artificially.","id":"book-terms-19","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":20,"q":"A software company launched a new project management application two years ago. Since then, the company has completed a project to add AI-powered scheduling features, is currently running a project to redesign the mobile interface, and is planning a future project to eventually retire the application and migrate users to a newer platform. What does this scenario best illustrate?","opts":{"A":"A program, because all the efforts are related to the same application and share a common goal.","B":"Project management, because each effort has a defined scope, schedule, and budget.","C":"Product management, because multiple projects are being coordinated across the full lifecycle of the product.","D":"A portfolio, because the projects are aligned to a long term strategic business goal."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Product management, because multiple projects are being coordinated across the full lifecycle of the product. Explanation: Product management focuses on the entire lifecycle of a product, from creation through enhancement to eventual retirement. Each stage of that lifecycle may involve one or more projects, but the overarching coordination of those efforts across the product’s life is product management, not project management. Project management governs individual temporary efforts within that lifecycle. Why the other options are incorrect: A program is a collection of related projects managed together to achieve a common goal. While these projects are related, the scenario describes coordination across a product lifecycle, which is the defining characteristic of product management. Project management governs individual temporary efforts. Each project described here is an example of project management, but the scenario as a whole, spanning creation, enhancement, and retirement, describes product management. A portfolio aligns projects and programs to a strategic business goal. The scenario focuses on the lifecycle of a single product rather than a collection of efforts supporting a broader organizational strategy.","id":"book-terms-20","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Key Project Management Terms","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A project is on schedule and within budget. A key vendor has been delivering work consistently, though always at the last possible moment within the agreed window. The project manager is reviewing the status report and preparing for the next phase, which has significantly less schedule buffer than the current one. Which TWO actions should the project manager take? (Choose 2)","opts":{"A":"Take no action since the vendor is meeting contractual obligations and the project is on track.","B":"Log the vendor’s delivery pattern as a risk in the risk register.","C":"Add internal schedule buffer to absorb potential delays.","D":"Meet with the vendor to discuss the delivery pattern.","E":"Log this as an issue and close it once the next delivery arrives on time."},"ans":["B","D"],"exp":"B: Log the vendor’s delivery pattern as a risk in the risk register. D: Meet with the vendor to discuss the delivery pattern. Explanation: A proactive mindset means treating observable warning signs as risks before they become problems. The vendor’s pattern of last-minute delivery has not caused harm yet, but the reduced buffer in the next phase makes the same behavior a real threat. Logging it as a risk ensures it is tracked and managed through formal risk management. Meeting with the vendor is a proactive, collaborative risk response: engage the vendor, clarify expectations, and seek earlier and more predictable deliveries. Why the other options are incorrect: Meeting contractual obligations does not mean a risk does not exist. A proactive project manager looks beyond current status and considers what the same pattern could mean under different conditions. Adding buffer without engaging the vendor treats the symptom rather than the root cause. It also reduces transparency with stakeholders by obscuring a known risk behind padding. This is a recurring behavioral pattern, not a single incident. Logging it as an issue and closing it when the next delivery arrives on time misclassifies the risk and leaves the team without a standing response plan going into a lower-buffer phase.","id":"book-principles-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive","type":"multi","pick":2},{"n":2,"q":"Two developers who are considered the most technically skilled members of the team have become visibly cold toward each other following a disagreement that appears to have started outside of work. No deliverables have been affected. They are scheduled to co-lead a critical module in the next phase that no one else on the team is qualified to handle. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Take no action since both developers are meeting their deliverables and the conflict has not affected the project.","B":"Escalate the situation to the sponsor and recommend that one of the developers be replaced before the next phase begins.","C":"Keep an eye on the situation and have a discussion with the developers only if the tension starts to affect their work on the critical module.","D":"Meet privately with each developer to understand the situation and discuss how they can collaborate effectively on the upcoming critical module."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Meet privately with each developer to understand the situation and discuss how they can collaborate effectively on the upcoming critical module. Explanation: A proactive mindset means acting on early warning signs before they become problems. Addressing the situation privately now is proportionate and pragmatic, giving the project manager the best chance of resolving the tension before it becomes a risk to the critical phase. Why the other options are incorrect: Current deliverable performance does not mean a risk does not exist. The real threat is what happens when close collaboration is required in the next phase, not now. Escalating to the sponsor and recommending replacement is a disproportionate response to a conflict that has not yet affected the project. Proactive action should be proportionate to the signal observed. Waiting until the tension affects the critical module means the problem has already materialized at the worst possible time. A conditional discussion is reactive, not proactive, and leaves the project manager responding to damage rather than preventing it.","id":"book-principles-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A software project is two weeks behind schedule due to unexpected technical complexity. The project manager reviews the project plan and identifies that shortening the performance testing phase from two weeks to three days would recover the lost time and bring the project back on track. The testing environment is already configured, the core features are working, and no major issues have surfaced during development. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Shorten the performance testing phase to recover the schedule.","B":"Raise a change request to shorten the performance testing phase.","C":"Analyze the impact of shortening performance testing.","D":"Consult the sponsor about whether to shorten the performance testing phase."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Analyze the impact of shortening performance testing. Explanation: Shortening performance testing looks like a clean schedule fix, but testing phases have dependencies that are easy to underestimate. Reduced testing time could mask performance issues that only surface under load, create rework cycles that cost more time than was saved, or affect stakeholders who depend on performance benchmarks. Thinking holistically means examining how a change in one area affects the rest of the system before any decision is made. Why the other options are incorrect: The absence of major issues during development does not guarantee that performance testing will be uneventful. Proceeding without evaluating the downstream impact is exactly the kind of narrow decision that leads to larger problems later. Submitting a change request is an important process step, but it should follow the impact analysis, not replace it. Moving straight to change control without first understanding the full effect of the change puts process ahead of judgment. Consulting the sponsor before completing the analysis means escalating a problem without a recommendation. The project manager should assess the full impact first and bring a well-informed proposal, not just a question.","id":"book-principles-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager is leading a large ERP implementation. Midway through the project, a senior vice president requests that her department’s reporting module be prioritized and expanded beyond the original scope. The VP has significant influence over project funding and has been a strong supporter of the initiative. The development team has the capacity to build the expanded module, but doing so would consume the schedule buffer reserved to manage risks in two other modules that are equally critical to the success of the ERP rollout for other departments. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Assess the impact of the request on the other modules and overall project objectives before responding.","B":"Approve the VP’s request to protect the project’s funding and maintain her support.","C":"Decline the request since the project scope has already been defined and cannot be changed.","D":"Escalate the request to the sponsor since a senior executive is involved."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Assess the impact of the request on the other modules and overall project objectives before responding. Explanation: The VP’s seniority and influence make her request feel urgent and politically difficult to push back on. But approving it without analysis means optimizing for one stakeholder at the expense of others. Thinking holistically means stepping back and evaluating how the change affects the entire system before making any commitment. Why the other options are incorrect: Approving the request to protect political capital prioritizes one stakeholder relationship over the project’s broader commitments and objectives. A holistic view requires considering all affected parties, not just the most influential one. Declining without analysis is equally narrow. The request may be legitimate and worth accommodating if the impact is manageable. Reflexive refusal is not a substitute for thoughtful evaluation. Escalating to the sponsor before completing an impact assessment means bringing a problem without a recommendation. The project manager should evaluate the full impact first and present a well-informed position.","id":"book-principles-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"You are midway through a critical phase of a project, and the team has been progressing well. One of the key features has just been completed: it meets all the documented functional requirements and passes the current round of testing, but the user interface is somewhat unintuitive and may cause confusion for some users. Addressing these usability concerns now would require two days of rework and cause a minor delay to this milestone. What should the project manager do next?","opts":{"A":"Accept the feature as it is and record the usability issue in the backlog to avoid delaying the current milestone.","B":"Work with the team to address the usability concerns now, even if it causes a minor delay to the current milestone.","C":"Ask the product owner or sponsor to decide whether to fix the usability issue now or defer it to a later release.","D":"Wait for user feedback after release to see whether the usability issue becomes a real problem before taking any action."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Work with the team to address the usability concerns now, even if it causes a minor delay to the current milestone. Explanation: A feature that works technically but frustrates users has not met quality standards. Deferring the fix feels efficient in the moment, but usability issues compound; the longer they go unaddressed, the more expensive and disruptive they become to fix. Embedding quality means resolving issues as they surface, not accumulating them for a later review that will arrive under even more pressure. Why the other options are incorrect: Recording the issue in the backlog trades a minor delay now for a potentially larger one later. Quality issues do not get easier or cheaper to fix as the project progresses. This decision is within the project manager’s authority to manage. Escalating it upward before attempting to resolve it at the team level is premature and adds unnecessary delay. Waiting for user feedback after release means knowingly shipping a feature that may frustrate users. Post-release fixes are the most expensive and disruptive form of rework and damage stakeholder trust.","id":"book-principles-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager is presenting a monthly status update to the sponsor and key stakeholders. The project is three weeks behind schedule after a third-party vendor failed to deliver a critical component on time. The vendor has since acknowledged the delay and provided a revised delivery date. The sponsor asks the project manager for an update on where things stand. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Explain that the delay was caused by the vendor’s failure to meet their contractual obligations and share the vendor’s revised delivery date.","B":"Apologize for the delay, outline the steps being taken to recover the schedule, and commit to keeping stakeholders informed as the situation develops.","C":"Assure the sponsor that the vendor has been formally notified and that contractual penalties will be applied if the revised date is missed.","D":"Request additional time to investigate the root cause before committing to a recovery plan."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Apologize for the delay, outline the steps being taken to recover the schedule, and commit to keeping stakeholders informed as the situation develops. Explanation: Regardless of what caused the delay, the project manager is responsible for the project outcome. Leading with a recovery plan and a commitment to transparency keeps the focus on moving forward rather than assigning fault. Why the other options are incorrect: Pointing to the vendor’s failure may be accurate, but leading with blame shifts focus away from solutions and signals that the project manager sees the problem as someone else’s responsibility. Citing contractual penalties addresses consequences for the vendor but says nothing about how the project will recover. The sponsor’s primary concern is the project outcome, not vendor enforcement. Requesting more time to investigate before committing to a plan leaves the sponsor without a path forward. The project manager should come to the status meeting prepared to respond, not to defer.","id":"book-principles-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"Early in a software project, a vendor missed the contractually agreed date for delivering their technical requirements. Rather than delay the schedule, the project manager made the call to begin development on the core features and integrate the third-party requirements once they arrived. Three months later, the requirements are delivered and reveal several conflicts with decisions already made in the codebase. Rework is now required, and the project will miss a key delivery milestone. The project manager is meeting with the sponsor to discuss the delay. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Explain that the third party delivered their requirements late and that the conflicts in the codebase were a direct result of that delay.","B":"Recommend that the third party be held accountable for the rework costs since their late documentation caused the conflict.","C":"Propose a revised schedule and present it to the sponsor without discussing the circumstances that led to the delay.","D":"Acknowledge that the decision to proceed without the third-party requirements contributed to the situation and present a plan to address it."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Acknowledge that the decision to proceed without the third-party requirements contributed to the situation and present a plan to address it. Explanation: The third party did miss their contractual deadline, but the decision to proceed without the requirements rather than flag it as a risk or adjust the plan was a judgment call that contributed to the current outcome. Presenting a recovery plan while being transparent about that decision maintains trust and keeps the focus on moving forward. Why the other options are incorrect: The third party’s late delivery is a legitimate contributing factor, but framing the entire delay as their fault omits the project manager’s decision to proceed. Pursuing cost recovery may be a valid contractual matter to explore separately, but raising it in this meeting deflects from the project manager’s own role in the outcome. Presenting a revised schedule without context leaves the sponsor without the full picture needed to make informed decisions. Transparency about what happened is as important as the plan to fix it.","id":"book-principles-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project manager is overseeing the launch of a new financial software product. Two weeks before release, testing identifies a defect that causes incorrect calculations under specific conditions. The defect is difficult to reproduce and may affect only a small percentage of users. The VP of Sales pressures the project manager to launch as scheduled, arguing that a delay will cost the company a major contract and the defect can be patched later. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Proceed with the launch since the defect is difficult to reproduce and affects only a small percentage of users.","B":"Proceed with the launch but document the defect and prioritize the patch in the next release cycle.","C":"Escalate the defect to the appropriate stakeholders and recommend delaying the launch until it is resolved.","D":"Defer to the VP of Sales since the business impact of the delay outweighs the risk of a minor defect."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Escalate the defect to the appropriate stakeholders and recommend delaying the launch until it is resolved. Explanation: A known defect that causes incorrect financial calculations puts users at risk regardless of how rarely it occurs. An accountable leader makes recommendations based on what is right for stakeholders, even when that recommendation is unwelcome to senior management. Why the other options are incorrect: The difficulty of reproducing the defect does not eliminate the risk. Shipping software with a known financial calculation error is an integrity issue, not just a technical one. Deferring the fix to a future release knowingly ships a flawed product. A promised patch does not eliminate harm that could occur in the meantime. Seniority does not make an ethically questionable decision the right one. Deferring to business pressure is exactly the kind of situation where integrity must take precedence.","id":"book-principles-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A senior network engineer approaches the project manager with a technical architecture decision that needs to be made before the team can proceed with a project phase. The project manager has been personally approving all technical decisions to make sure nothing important is missed. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Make the decision after consulting with the engineer to ensure nothing important is overlooked.","B":"Escalate the decision to the sponsor since technical architecture decisions carry significant project risk.","C":"Ask the engineer for a recommendation, evaluate it, and approve or reject it before the team proceeds.","D":"Ask the engineer to make the decision, move forward, and brief the project manager on the outcome."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Ask the engineer to make the decision, move forward, and brief the project manager on the outcome. Explanation: The engineer has the expertise to make this decision. Routing it through the project manager adds no value and creates delay. Building an empowered culture means trusting team members to operate within their area of expertise and reserving the project manager’s involvement for decisions that genuinely require it. Why the other options are incorrect: Consulting the engineer but retaining the final decision keeps authority unnecessarily centralized. The concern about missing something important is better addressed by trusting qualified team members than by bottlenecking decisions through the project manager. Escalating a routine technical decision to the sponsor is disproportionate and signals a lack of confidence in the team’s ability to manage their own domain. Asking the engineer for a recommendation but retaining approval authority maintains the same bottleneck. It adds a layer of process without meaningfully empowering the team.","id":"book-principles-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project team is six months into a twelve-month software development project and is on schedule and within budget. Midway through development, market research reveals that customer preferences have shifted significantly and several features being built are unlikely to be used. Changing course now would require reworking two months of completed development and pushing the delivery date back by six weeks. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Continue with the current plan since the project is on track and changing course would introduce significant cost and schedule risk.","B":"Raise the findings with the sponsor and recommend adjusting the scope to align with current customer needs, even if it impacts the schedule.","C":"Complete the current features as planned and incorporate the new customer preferences into a future project phase.","D":"Ask the team to accelerate delivery of the current features to minimize the window of misalignment before the next phase begins."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Raise the findings with the sponsor and recommend adjusting the scope to align with current customer needs, even if it impacts the schedule. Explanation: A value-driven mindset means continuously evaluating whether the project is still delivering what it set out to deliver. Being on schedule and within budget only matters if the work being done will actually create value. When evidence emerges that the current approach is misaligned with customer needs, the project manager’s responsibility is to surface that information and recommend a course correction, in spite of short-term costs. Why the other options are incorrect: Staying on course because the project is on track prioritizes process metrics over outcomes. Delivering on time and on budget has no value if the product does not meet customer needs. Deferring the adjustment to a future phase means knowingly completing work that is unlikely to deliver value. The cost of rework now is likely lower than the cost of delivering something customers will not use. Accelerating delivery does not address the misalignment. Finishing faster still results in features that customers are unlikely to use.","id":"book-principles-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager is selecting a cloud hosting vendor for a new customer-facing application. Two vendors have been shortlisted. Vendor A meets all technical requirements and comes in $40,000 under budget. Vendor B meets the same technical requirements but costs $40,000 more. However, they have a significantly stronger track record for uptime reliability and customer support responsiveness. The project sponsor is leaning toward Vendor A to stay under budget. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Select Vendor A since it meets all technical requirements and keeps the project under budget.","B":"Select Vendor B since a customer-facing application makes reliability and support responsiveness critical factors that justify the additional cost.","C":"Select Vendor A since the $40,000 in savings can be held as contingency to address any reliability issues that arise.","D":"Select Vendor A and negotiate a stronger service level agreement to offset the reliability and support gap."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Select Vendor B since a customer-facing application makes reliability and support responsiveness critical factors that justify the additional cost. Explanation: A value-driven approach means evaluating decisions against the full picture of value, not just the budget line. For a customer-facing application, uptime and support responsiveness directly affect the user experience and the organization’s reputation. The $40,000 difference is a known, manageable cost. The downstream impact of reliability issues is harder to predict and potentially far more expensive. Why the other options are incorrect: Meeting technical requirements and staying under budget are important but incomplete criteria. Cost alone does not capture the full value picture, particularly when customer experience is at stake. Holding savings as contingency assumes reliability issues will be minor and manageable. A vendor with a weaker track record may produce problems that exceed what contingency can absorb, and the damage to customer trust cannot be funded away. A stronger service level agreement can set expectations and define remedies, but it cannot substitute for a proven track record. Contractual penalties do not prevent outages or slow support response times from affecting customers.","id":"book-principles-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is leading a website redesign for a client. Based on experience with similar clients and projects, the project manager develops a detailed plan focused on visual refresh and page load speed. Midway through the project, the client reveals that their primary concern was actually improving the checkout conversion rate, which had never come up in initial discussions. What does this scenario best illustrate?","opts":{"A":"The project manager should have conducted a benchmarking study of similar projects to better anticipate client needs.","B":"The client failed to communicate their priorities clearly and should have been more involved in planning.","C":"The project manager should have followed industry best practices for website redesigns to avoid misalignment.","D":"The project manager should have validated what value meant to this client before committing to a plan."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: The project manager should have validated what value meant to this client before committing to a plan. Explanation: Value is subjective and different stakeholders define it differently. Prior experience with similar clients is a useful starting point, but it cannot reveal what this specific client values on this specific project. The misalignment here was not inevitable; it was the result of assuming rather than asking. Why the other options are incorrect: Benchmarking similar projects provides useful context but cannot substitute for direct engagement with the client. Pattern recognition across projects is not the same as understanding this client’s specific priorities. The client’s lack of explicit communication is a contributing factor, but the project manager is responsible for asking the right questions early. Stakeholder engagement is not passive. Industry best practices reflect common approaches, not individual client priorities. Following them may produce a technically sound deliverable that still fails to deliver what the client actually values.","id":"book-principles-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager is overseeing the development of a patient portal for a regional medical group. During a cost review, a senior executive notices that the data privacy and compliance infrastructure is one of the largest line items in the budget and instructs the project manager to find a cheaper alternative. The executive points to a simpler storage solution used successfully on the organization’s internal scheduling tool. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Implement the cheaper solution since the executive has approved it and a previous project used it successfully.","B":"Submit a change request to formally reduce the data privacy budget in line with the executive’s instructions.","C":"Explain to the executive that the data privacy infrastructure protects patients and the organization from regulatory and reputational risk, and recommend maintaining the original approach.","D":"Immediately begin researching cheaper alternatives to demonstrate responsiveness to the executive’s cost concerns."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Explain to the executive that the data privacy infrastructure protects patients and the organization from regulatory and reputational risk, and recommend maintaining the original approach. Explanation: The executive’s suggestion is not unreasonable on the surface; the cheaper solution worked before. But a patient portal operates under a very different risk profile than an internal scheduling tool. The project manager’s responsibility is to ensure decision-makers understand the full implications of cost-cutting decisions, particularly when they affect patient privacy and regulatory compliance. Short-term savings that create long-term legal and reputational exposure are not sustainable. Why the other options are incorrect: Executive approval and prior precedent do not make a decision sound. The previous project had a fundamentally different risk profile. Implementing the cheaper solution without raising the implications would be a failure of accountability. Submitting a change request treats this as a routine budget adjustment. It follows process without questioning whether the change is appropriate, and does nothing to ensure the executive understands the risk being introduced. Jumping straight into researching alternatives prioritizes cost reduction over stakeholder protection. Before exploring options, the executive needs to understand the regulatory and reputational risks.","id":"book-principles-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"A project manager is leading a mixed team of senior engineers and recent graduates on a complex infrastructure project. One of the recent graduates has been assigned a critical task for the first time and has the necessary technical training but no prior experience with this type of work. How should the project manager engage with this team member?","opts":{"A":"Provide clear guidance and closer support while the graduate builds experience on this type of work.","B":"Assign a senior engineer to take over the task to reduce the risk of delays on a critical deliverable.","C":"Apply the same hands-off approach used with the senior engineers to build the graduate’s confidence and independence.","D":"Escalate the assignment to the sponsor since placing an inexperienced team member on a critical task introduces unnecessary project risk."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Provide clear guidance and closer support while the graduate builds experience on this type of work. Explanation: Accountable leadership means adapting your approach to the person and the situation, not applying a uniform style across the team. A recent graduate tackling a critical task for the first time needs more structure and support than an experienced engineer, providing that support is not micromanagement; it is the appropriate leadership response for this context. Why the other options are incorrect: Reassigning the task avoids developing the team member and may not be necessary. With appropriate support, the graduate may be fully capable of completing the work successfully. A hands-off approach builds autonomy in team members who are already capable of operating independently. Applying it to someone with no prior experience on this type of work sets them up to struggle rather than succeed. A single inexperienced team member on a critical task is a manageable risk, not one that warrants sponsor involvement. The project manager has the tools to address this directly.","id":"book-principles-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A six-month software project was completed on time and within budget. The scope was stable throughout, stakeholders were engaged and satisfied, and the final product passed all acceptance criteria on the first review. Looking back, however, the team spent nearly 40% of the project timeline fixing bugs and rewriting code that had already been reviewed and approved in earlier phases. What most likely caused this issue?","opts":{"A":"The project’s acceptance criteria were not rigorous enough to catch issues earlier in the development cycle.","B":"The team was understaffed, causing quality to suffer under delivery pressure.","C":"Quality checks were treated as milestones rather than being embedded continuously throughout the development process.","D":"The project manager did not identify and escalate defects quickly enough during execution."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Quality checks were treated as milestones rather than being embedded continuously throughout the development process. Explanation: The project delivered a good product, met its constraints, and satisfied stakeholders. But the volume of rework on previously approved work points to a process quality problem. When quality is checked at discrete points rather than built into every step, issues pass through early gates and surface later when they are more expensive and disruptive to fix. A good final product does not mean the process that produced it was efficient or high quality. Why the other options are incorrect: The final product passed acceptance criteria on the first review, which suggests the criteria were adequate. Weak acceptance criteria would more likely result in a failed final review, not rework during development. There is no indication in the scenario that the team was understaffed. The project finished on time and within budget, which argues against a resourcing problem as the root cause. The project manager’s defect escalation speed does not explain why previously approved work required rework. The issue is not how fast problems were raised but why they were not caught before approval in the first place.","id":"book-principles-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Principles and Mindset","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A product owner on a software project tells the agile project manager that all items in the product backlog are equally important and should all be treated as top priority. The team is about to begin sprint planning. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Prioritize the product backlog on behalf of the product owner to keep the project moving.","B":"Allow the team to prioritize the backlog during sprint planning since they have the best understanding of the work.","C":"Educate the product owner on the importance of prioritization and work with them to establish relative value across the backlog items.","D":"Escalate the issue to senior management since the product owner is not fulfilling their role."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Educate the product owner on the importance of prioritization and work with them to establish relative value across the backlog items. Explanation: Only the product owner can prioritize the product backlog. When a product owner resists prioritization, the agile project manager’s first response should be to educate, not to take over or escalate. Declaring everything equally important is not a prioritization strategy. It undermines the core agile principle of delivering the highest value work first. Why the other options are incorrect: Prioritizing the backlog on behalf of the product owner cuts out the customer advocate from the process. The product owner represents the customer’s perspective and priorities. Without that input, the team risks building the most technically convenient features rather than the most valuable ones. The team and the agile project manager may assist the product owner by providing input and effort estimates, but handing prioritization to either of them has the same problem. Without the product owner’s customer perspective driving the order, value delivery is compromised. Escalating to senior management is premature. The agile project manager has not yet attempted to educate the product owner, which is always the appropriate first step.","id":"book-agile-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":2,"q":"An agile team has just completed their third sprint and delivered five new features to the customer. During the sprint review, the customer expresses frustration that none of the delivered features address their most pressing business needs, while several critical items they flagged early in the project are still sitting in the product backlog unstarted. What most likely caused this situation?","opts":{"A":"The team selected the wrong items from the product backlog during sprint planning.","B":"The product backlog was not properly prioritized based on customer value before sprint planning began.","C":"The sprint review meeting was not conducted frequently enough to catch the misalignment earlier.","D":"The agile project manager failed to communicate the customer’s priorities to the development team."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The product backlog was not properly prioritized based on customer value before sprint planning began. Explanation: Agile delivers value by working from the top of a prioritized product backlog. If high-value items are sitting unstarted after three sprints while lower-value items have been completed, the most likely cause is that the backlog was not prioritized based on customer value. The product owner is responsible for ensuring the backlog reflects what matters most to the customer. Why the other options are incorrect: The team selects items from the top of the product backlog during sprint planning. If those items did not reflect customer priorities, the problem was in how the backlog was ordered, not in how the team selected from it. Sprint reviews occur at the end of every sprint and provide exactly the kind of feedback described here. The frequency of reviews is not the issue; the issue is that the backlog driving the work was not aligned with customer value. The agile project manager’s role is to facilitate and remove impediments, not to relay customer priorities to the team. Customer priorities should be reflected directly in the product backlog, which is the product owner’s responsibility to maintain.","id":"book-agile-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":3,"q":"An agile team has just completed a sprint. The delivered increment has been presented and the customer has provided feedback. The team is now meeting internally to discuss what slowed them down during the sprint, identify what practices worked well, and agree on improvements for the next sprint. What meeting is the team conducting?","opts":{"A":"Sprint planning meeting","B":"Daily standup","C":"Sprint review","D":"Retrospective"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Retrospective Explanation: The retrospective occurs after the sprint review and focuses inward on the team’s process rather than on the product. It is where the team identifies what went well, what did not, and what to improve in the next sprint. Why the other options are incorrect: Sprint planning comes after the retrospective, not before it. The team has not yet returned to the product backlog to select work for the next sprint. The daily standup is a brief check-in held during the sprint, not a meeting conducted after it ends. The sprint review already took place when the customer evaluated the increment and provided feedback. The meeting described here is the internal team session that follows it.","id":"book-agile-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":4,"q":"An agile team is gathering to kick off a new sprint. After reviewing the highest priority items available, the team discusses how much work they can realistically complete and commits to a set of items to deliver by the end of the sprint. What is the primary output of this meeting?","opts":{"A":"Sprint backlog","B":"Product backlog","C":"Sprint plan","D":"Retrospective report"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Sprint backlog Explanation: The sprint planning meeting results in the sprint backlog, which contains the items the team has selected from the top of the prioritized product backlog and committed to completing in the upcoming sprint. The sprint planning meeting produces the sprint-specific subset of work the team will execute. Why the other options are incorrect: The product backlog is created and maintained by the product owner throughout the project. It is an input to sprint planning, not an output of it. A sprint plan is not a recognized agile artifact. The retrospective report is produced at the end of a sprint, not at the beginning. It reflects on the previous sprint rather than planning the next one.","id":"book-agile-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":5,"q":"A senior executive sponsoring an agile software project attended a sprint planning meeting and observed the team openly debating how much work they could commit to, negotiating scope amongst themselves, and selecting their own tasks. Afterward, he pulls the agile project manager aside and expresses concern that no one appears to be in charge, there is no detailed project plan, and the project manager does not seem to be directing anyone. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Begin assigning tasks to team members and producing a detailed project plan to reassure the executive that the project is under control.","B":"Defer to the executive’s concerns and adjust the team’s approach since he is a senior leader whose preferences should be accommodated.","C":"Explain to the executive that the team’s self-organization is intentional and schedule a sprint review so he can see the value being delivered.","D":"Ask the sponsor to speak directly with the executive and explain how agile works, since the sponsor has more organizational authority."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Explain to the executive that the team’s self-organization is intentional and schedule a sprint review so he can see the value being delivered. Explanation: A servant leader protects the team’s ability to self-organize while managing stakeholder expectations. The executive’s concern is understandable but stems from unfamiliarity with agile practices. Rather than abandoning the approach or dismissing the concern, the agile project manager should address it by educating the sponsor and redirecting his attention to what actually matters: the value being delivered. Why the other options are incorrect: Producing a detailed project plan and assigning tasks would undermine the team’s self-organization and reintroduce the command-and-control dynamic that agile is specifically designed to avoid. Executive satisfaction does not override sound agile practices. Escalating the executive’s concern to the sponsor is disproportionate and bypasses the agile project manager’s responsibility to manage stakeholder relationships directly.","id":"book-agile-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"An agile software project is approaching its final sprint. A senior stakeholder reviews the product backlog and notices that several planned features will not be completed before the release date. He demands that either the deadline be extended or additional developers be brought on to ensure all features are delivered. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Agree with the stakeholder and submit a change request to formally expand the project scope and timeline.","B":"Bring on additional developers to help the team complete the remaining features before the release date.","C":"Escalate the stakeholder’s concerns to the product owner since backlog management is their responsibility.","D":"Explain to the stakeholder that on an agile project, time and cost are fixed while scope is flexible, and that the highest value features have already been delivered within the agreed constraints."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Explain to the stakeholder that on an agile project, time and cost are fixed while scope is flexible, and that the highest value features have already been delivered within the agreed constraints. Explanation: Agile inverts the traditional project triangle. Rather than fixing scope and allowing time and cost to flex, agile fixes time and cost while allowing scope to vary. By prioritizing the product backlog based on value, the most important features are delivered first, meaning the project has already delivered its highest value work within the agreed constraints. Why the other options are incorrect: Submitting a change request to expand scope and timeline applies traditional change control to an agile project. Adding developers mid-project introduces risk, disrupts team dynamics, and does not reflect the agile model. The product owner manages and prioritizes the backlog, but stakeholder communication is the agile project manager’s responsibility. Routing the conversation to the product owner avoids the PM’s own obligation to educate and engage stakeholders.","id":"book-agile-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":7,"q":"An agile project manager notices midway through a sprint that the team is behind on several of their sprint backlog items. The project manager is concerned that the sprint goal may not be met. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Reassign some of the delayed items to the most experienced team members","B":"Raise the concern at the next standup, ask the team how they plan to address it, and offer support if needed.","C":"Escalate the delay to the product owner so they can reprioritize the sprint backlog accordingly.","D":"Extend the sprint by a few days to give the team enough time to complete their commitments."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Raise the concern at the next standup, ask the team how they plan to address it, and offer support if needed. Explanation: Agile teams are self-organizing and self-managing. The agile project manager’s role is to support the team, not to direct their work. Raising the concern in the standup and asking how the team plans to respond respects their autonomy while keeping the PM informed and available to remove any impediments. Why the other options are incorrect: Reassigning work undermines the team’s self-organization. The team committed to the sprint backlog together and should manage their own response to delays. The PM directing individual assignments is a command-and-control approach that contradicts agile principles. The sprint backlog is the team’s commitment for the current sprint. Involving the product owner to reprioritize mid-sprint introduces unnecessary disruption. The product owner manages the product backlog, not the sprint backlog. Sprint length is fixed and should not be extended to accommodate delays. If the team cannot complete all sprint backlog items, unfinished work returns to the product backlog for consideration in the next sprint.","id":"book-agile-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":8,"q":"An agile team is midway through a sprint when the only developer with database expertise falls ill and is unable to work for several days. Three other sprint backlog items are blocked because no one else on the team has the skills to complete them. The sprint goal is at risk and the team has no way to redistribute the blocked work. What could have prevented this situation?","opts":{"A":"Building a larger team to ensure more resources are available when individual members are unavailable.","B":"Assigning the database work to an external contractor who could be brought in on short notice.","C":"Building the team with overlapping skills so that no critical task relies on a single person.","D":"Reserving a portion of the sprint backlog as a buffer for unexpected absences."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Building the team with overlapping skills so that no critical task relies on a single person. Explanation: Agile teams should be composed of generalizing specialists, meaning team members have complementary and overlapping skills. When critical knowledge is concentrated in a single person, the team becomes vulnerable to exactly this kind of bottleneck. Overlapping skills allow the team to redistribute work fluidly and maintain their pace without external intervention. Why the other options are incorrect: A larger team does not solve the bottleneck problem if the additional members still lack the relevant skills. Team size also impacts cost. Bringing in an external contractor introduces someone unfamiliar with the team’s ways of working mid-sprint, which creates its own disruptions and does not address the underlying skill gap within the team. Reserving buffer in the sprint backlog manages the symptom rather than the cause. The goal is to build a team that can absorb disruptions, not to plan around them by doing less work.","id":"book-agile-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":9,"q":"Which THREE of the following are recognized Agile principles? (Choose 3)","opts":{"A":"Define all requirements completely before development begins.","B":"Welcome changing requirements, even late in development.","C":"Assign tasks to team members based on their individual areas of expertise.","D":"Deliver value to the customer frequently, in weeks rather than months.","E":"Document all project decisions thoroughly for future reference.","F":"The best architectures and designs emerge from self-organizing teams."},"ans":["B","D","F"],"exp":"B: Welcome changing requirements, even late in development. D: Deliver value to the customer frequently, in weeks rather than months. F: The best architectures and designs emerge from self-organizing teams. Explanation: Agile principles are centered on flexibility, frequent delivery, collaboration, and self-organization. Welcoming late changes, delivering value in short cycles, and trusting self-organizing teams to produce the best solutions are all core to agile thinking. Why the other options are incorrect: Defining all requirements before development begins describes the traditional predictive approach. Agile explicitly rejects this in favor of evolving requirements throughout the project. Assigning tasks based on individual expertise describes a directed, top-down approach. Agile teams are self-organizing and decide amongst themselves who does what. Thorough documentation of all decisions reflects a traditional, documentation-heavy approach. Agile values working software over comprehensive documentation.","id":"book-agile-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile","type":"multi","pick":3},{"n":10,"q":"An agile project manager is facilitating the daily standup when two team members begin a detailed technical discussion about how to solve a complex problem. The rest of the team is engaged and the conversation seems productive. The meeting has already exceeded 15 minutes and shows no sign of wrapping up. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Note the discussion as an impediment and add it to the next retrospective agenda to improve future standups.","B":"Let the two team members finish since interrupting a productive technical discussion undermines team collaboration.","C":"Intervene, close the standup, and suggest the two team members continue the discussion separately after the meeting.","D":"Let the discussion finish but shorten tomorrow’s standup to compensate for the extra time spent today."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Intervene, close the standup, and suggest the two team members continue the discussion separately after the meeting. Explanation: The daily standup is a timebox event limited to 15 minutes. Its purpose is to synchronize the team, not to solve problems. When a discussion exceeds the timebox, the agile project manager should intervene to protect the format, even if the conversation seems valuable. Detailed problem-solving should happen in a separate session with only the relevant participants. Why the other options are incorrect: Adding this to the retrospective defers a problem that needs to be addressed immediately and also miscategorizes discussion as an impediment. The agile project manager’s role as facilitator includes protecting the team’s processes and timeboxes. Deferring to the team in this situation is not empowerment; it is a failure to fulfill the facilitation responsibility. Timeboxes are not transferable. Each standup should be 15 minutes regardless of what happened in previous meetings. Compensating tomorrow does not restore the time lost today or protect the format going forward.","id":"book-agile-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager with extensive agile experience is assigned to lead a large infrastructure project for a financial services company. The project involves two distinct phases: a software configuration phase with evolving requirements and frequent stakeholder feedback, and a data migration phase with strict regulatory requirements, fully documented specifications, and zero tolerance for errors. The project manager proposes using agile for both phases since it has worked well on previous projects. Is this the right approach?","opts":{"A":"Yes, because Agile’s flexibility and frequent feedback loops will benefit both phases of the project.","B":"Yes, because the project manager’s experience with agile makes it the most reliable choice for the entire project.","C":"No, because financial services projects must always use a predictive approach to satisfy regulatory requirements.","D":"No, because the data migration phase has stable, well-defined requirements that call for a predictive approach, making hybrid the better fit."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: No, because the data migration phase has stable, well-defined requirements that call for a predictive approach, making hybrid the better fit. Explanation: The development approach should be selected based on the characteristics of each phase, not the project manager’s personal preference or track record. The software configuration phase has evolving requirements that suit an agile approach, while the data migration phase has fixed, regulatory requirements that suit a predictive approach. Applying agile wholesale ignores these differences and introduces unnecessary risk into a phase that requires precision and control. Why the other options are incorrect: Agile’s flexibility is a strength in uncertain environments, but it is not universally beneficial. Applying it to a phase with fixed, regulatory requirements introduces variability where none is needed or wanted. Familiarity with an approach is not a valid reason to apply it universally. The approach should be chosen based on what the project needs, not what the PM is most comfortable with. Regulatory requirements do not automatically mandate a fully predictive approach for the entire project. They do require a controlled, auditable process for the phases they govern, which is why hybrid is appropriate here rather than pure predictive.","id":"book-agile-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Hybrid"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is planning a migration of a highly complex legacy mainframe system to a new cloud architecture. The migration is technically demanding, but the requirements are fully documented and well understood by the team. Which development approach is most appropriate for this project?","opts":{"A":"Agile, because the technical complexity of the migration introduces too much uncertainty for a predictive approach.","B":"Agile, because cloud migrations involve emerging technology that requires frequent feedback and adaptation.","C":"Predictive, because the requirements are stable and well understood.","D":"Hybrid, because the technical complexity of some components will require flexibility while others can be planned upfront."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Predictive, because the requirements are stable and well understood. Explanation: The development approach is determined by the stability of requirements, not the complexity of the work. Despite the technical demands of the migration, the requirements are fully documented and well understood, which are the conditions that favor a predictive approach. Complexity is a distractor; requirement stability is the deciding factor. Why the other options are incorrect: Technical complexity introduces execution risk but does not make requirements unclear. Agile is appropriate when requirements are uncertain or expected to evolve, not simply because the work is difficult. Emerging technology can introduce uncertainty, but the scenario explicitly states that requirements are fully documented and well understood. The technology context does not override what is known about the requirements. Hybrid is appropriate when different parts of a project have different levels of requirement certainty. The scenario describes a single phase with stable, well-understood requirements throughout, which does not call for a hybrid approach.","id":"book-agile-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Hybrid"},{"n":13,"q":"An agile team has completed three sprints and has produced a working version of a new customer portal that covers the core functionality. The product owner is ready to release it to a small group of users to gather feedback. A senior executive reviews the portal and objects to the release, stating that the product is incomplete and that releasing an unfinished product would be embarrassing to the organization. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Agree with the executive and continue development until all features are complete before releasing to users.","B":"Explain to the executive that the portal meets the definition of an MVP and that early release allows the team to gather real user feedback to guide future development.","C":"Ask the product owner to add more features to the next sprint to address the executive’s concerns before releasing.","D":"Release the portal without informing the executive since the product owner has already approved it."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Explain to the executive that the portal meets the definition of an MVP and that early release allows the team to gather real user feedback to guide future development. Explanation: A minimum viable product is not an unfinished product; it is a usable product that delivers value without requiring all planned features. Releasing an MVP early is a deliberate agile strategy that allows customers to realize value sooner and provides the team with real feedback to guide future development. Waiting for a perfect product delays value and eliminates the feedback loop that makes agile effective. Why the other options are incorrect: Waiting until all features are complete contradicts the agile principle of early and frequent value delivery and eliminates the opportunity to gather user feedback during development. Adding more features to satisfy the executive’s perception of completeness prioritizes appearance over value delivery. The product owner is responsible for determining when the product is ready based on its value to the customer. Releasing without informing the executive is not appropriate. The agile project manager should manage stakeholder expectations transparently, not work around them.","id":"book-agile-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":14,"q":"An agile project manager is setting up the workspace for a new co-located development team. She plans to implement a sophisticated project management software with real-time dashboards, automated notifications, and digital kanban boards accessible from any device. A senior executive stops by and suggests the team simply use a whiteboard with sticky notes on the wall instead since that’s what they always used back when they were managing projects. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Proceed with the software since it offers more features and visibility than a physical whiteboard.","B":"Use both the software and the whiteboard to get the best of both worlds.","C":"Agree with the executive’s suggestion and use the whiteboard.","D":"Politely decline the executive’s suggestion since tool selection is the project manager’s decision and the software has already been planned."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Agree with the executive’s suggestion and use the whiteboard. Explanation: Agile promotes low-tech, high-touch tools because they encourage collaboration, are immediately visible to anyone in the space, and reduce barriers to participation. For a co-located team, a physical whiteboard is an information radiator that passively communicates project status to everyone who walks by. Sophisticated software, while useful in some contexts, can reduce spontaneous interaction and create dependency on devices. Why the other options are incorrect: More features do not equal more value in an agile context. A physical board that the whole team can see and interact with instantly often provides more practical visibility than a dashboard that requires logging in and navigating software. Using both tools adds unnecessary overhead and splits the team’s attention. Agile favors simplicity; maintaining two parallel systems creates duplication without added benefit. Tool selection should be based on what best serves the team and agile principles, not on who has authority over the decision. Dismissing a good suggestion because it came from outside the PM’s role contradicts the collaborative spirit of agile.","id":"book-agile-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":15,"q":"Midway through a sprint, a key stakeholder contacts the agile project manager with an urgent feature request. The stakeholder insists the feature is critical to an upcoming client demo and must be included in the current sprint. What should the agile project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Add the feature to the product backlog and work with the product owner to prioritize it for the next sprint.","B":"Ask the team to assess whether they can fit the feature into the current sprint without affecting their other commitments.","C":"Add the feature to the current sprint immediately since the client demo represents significant business value.","D":"Cancel the current sprint and restart planning to incorporate the urgent feature."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Add the feature to the product backlog and work with the product owner to prioritize it for the next sprint. Explanation: Changes are welcome on agile projects, but not mid-sprint. Once a sprint has started, the team’s commitments should be protected so they can work at a sustainable and predictable pace. The correct response is to add the request to the product backlog, where the product owner can prioritize it based on value. If it is truly urgent, it will rise to the top and be addressed in the next sprint. Why the other options are incorrect: Business value does not justify disrupting an active sprint. Introducing changes mid-sprint undermines the team’s ability to deliver on their commitments and sets a precedent that sprint boundaries can be ignored under pressure. Asking the team to assess capacity puts the burden of managing stakeholder pressure on the team. The sprint backlog should not change mid-sprint regardless of whether the team thinks they can absorb it. A good PM shields the Agile team from this kind of distraction. Cancelling a sprint is an extreme measure reserved for situations where the sprint goal becomes obsolete. A single urgent feature request does not justify abandoning the entire sprint.","id":"book-agile-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Agile and Hybrid","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":1,"q":"A project manager has just been assigned to a brand-new initiative. At this stage, the sponsor is clarifying the project’s purpose, securing initial funding approval, and identifying the key stakeholders who will be involved. Detailed scope and schedule have not yet been developed. Which focus area is the project currently in?","opts":{"A":"Planning","B":"Executing","C":"Monitoring and Controlling","D":"Initiating"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Initiating Explanation: The Initiating Focus Area is about defining and authorizing a new project or phase. This is where the project’s purpose is clarified, stakeholders are identified, initial scope is discussed, and initial funding is approved. The goal is alignment, not detail, so authorization, business need, and stakeholder identification are the signals of Initiating. Why the other options are incorrect: Planning is where the team decides how the project will be accomplished, including defining scope and estimating cost and schedule. The scenario states that detailed scope and schedule have not yet been developed, so the project has not reached Planning. Executing is where the work actually happens and deliverables are produced. No work is being produced yet. Monitoring and Controlling involves comparing actual results to the plan. There is no plan or performance to track at this stage.","id":"book-process-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager is about to create the project management plan for a new project. To save time, she retrieves a plan template and a completed plan from a similar project her organization delivered last year, along with the cost estimates that project produced. What is she using?","opts":{"A":"Organizational process assets","B":"Enterprise environmental factors","C":"Work performance information","D":"A change request"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Organizational process assets Explanation: OPAs are the plans, processes, policies, procedures, templates, and knowledge bases an organization has built up and can reuse. Templates, previous project plans, and estimates from past projects are classic OPAs that help a project manager manage a new project more easily. If you are talking about standard ways of working and stored knowledge you can reuse, it is an OPA. Why the other options are incorrect: Enterprise environmental factors are the contextual conditions the project must operate within, such as regulations, organizational culture, or market conditions. They are constraints to plan around, not reusable assets. Work performance information is analyzed data showing how current work compares to the plan. It is an output produced during the project, not a reusable asset from a past project. A change request is a request to modify a component of the project management plan. Reusing a template is not a request to change anything.","id":"book-process-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A painter reports that a room has been painted with flat white paint, that the work took two days, and that it cost $500. No comparison to the plan, schedule, or budget has been made yet. What does this BEST represent?","opts":{"A":"Work performance information","B":"A work performance report","C":"Work performance data","D":"A change request"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Work performance data Explanation: Work performance data is raw status information about the work that has been done, before any analysis has been applied. By itself it is not very useful, because it cannot answer questions like “Are we on schedule?” or “Are we on budget?” until it is compared against the plan. The painter’s raw figures are a textbook example of work performance data. Why the other options are incorrect: Work performance information is data that has been analyzed against the project management plan, such as recognizing that the $500 cost is over the planned budget. No such comparison has been made here. A work performance report compiles all the work performance information into one comprehensive status document. This is a single raw data point, not a compiled report. A change request is a request to modify a component of the project management plan. The painter is simply reporting status, not requesting a change.","id":"book-process-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project’s final deliverables have been verified and formally accepted by the customer. The team is now closing out contracts, documenting lessons learned, and transitioning the product to the operations team. Which focus area is the project in?","opts":{"A":"Executing","B":"Closing","C":"Monitoring and Controlling","D":"Planning"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Closing Explanation: The Closing Focus Area is about formally completing a project, phase, or contract. This includes verifying deliverables, obtaining final acceptance, closing contracts, documenting lessons learned, and transitioning work to operations. Acceptance, handoff, and formal completion all signal Closing. Why the other options are incorrect: Executing is where deliverables are produced. The deliverables here are already complete and accepted, so the work is finished rather than ongoing. Monitoring and Controlling tracks performance against the plan and evaluates changes. The project is being finalized, not tracked or adjusted. Planning is where the approach is decided at the start of the work. This is the opposite end of the life cycle.","id":"book-process-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project is currently on schedule and within budget. After noticing that a similar past project was delayed by late vendor deliveries, the project manager adds an early-warning checkpoint and builds in a buffer around a key handoff so the same problem does not occur on this project. What does this BEST describe?","opts":{"A":"A corrective action","B":"A defect repair","C":"Work performance information","D":"A preventive action"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: A preventive action Explanation: A preventive action is something you put in place to ensure the project stays on track. The project is not currently off track; the project manager is acting ahead of a potential problem to keep it that way. That forward-looking, keep-it-on-track intent is what distinguishes a preventive action. Why the other options are incorrect: A corrective action is taken to bring a project that has already gone off track back on track. The project here is on schedule and within budget, so there is nothing to correct yet. A defect repair is done to fix a broken component on a project, such as a failed network switch. No deliverable is broken here. Work performance information is analyzed status data, not an action taken on the project.","id":"book-process-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"Midway through execution, a project manager compares the project’s actual spending against the cost baseline, identifies a variance, and evaluates whether a change is needed to bring performance back in line with the plan. Which focus area best describes this work?","opts":{"A":"Monitoring and Controlling","B":"Executing","C":"Planning","D":"Closing"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Monitoring and Controlling Explanation: The Monitoring and Controlling Focus Area involves tracking performance, comparing actual results to the plan, identifying variances, and deciding whether changes are needed. Variance analysis, change evaluation, and performance reviews all point to Monitoring and Controlling. Why the other options are incorrect: Executing is where the work is produced. Here the project manager is measuring and evaluating the work, not producing it. Planning is where cost and schedule are estimated and the baseline is created. The baseline already exists and is being used as the comparison point. Closing is about formal completion and acceptance, which is not happening mid-execution.","id":"book-process-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is installing a phone system in four phases: select, install, test, and train. A new team member assumes that “Initiating” happens only once at the very start of the whole project and “Closing” only once at the very end. How should the project manager correct this understanding?","opts":{"A":"Initiating and Closing occur once per project, while the middle focus areas repeat each phase.","B":"Focus areas and phases are the same thing, so each phase is a single focus area.","C":"Each phase of the project includes all five focus areas, including initiating and closing that phase.","D":"Focus areas only apply to predictive projects, so phases do not contain them."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Each phase of the project includes all five focus areas, including initiating and closing that phase. Explanation: Each phase of a project includes all five focus areas. To complete a phase, the team initiates, plans, executes, monitors and controls, and closes that phase, producing a deliverable as its output. Focus areas are not the same as phases, and they are not limited to a single occurrence at the very start or very end of the project. Why the other options are incorrect: Saying initiating and closing happen only once per project is incorrect; every phase is initiated and closed. In the phone-system example, each of the four phases goes through all five focus areas. Treating focus areas and phases as the same thing confuses the two. A project may have phases like design, build, and test, but each phase still involves initiating, planning, executing, monitoring and controlling, and closing actions. PMI deliberately mixes phase and focus-area language to test this distinction. Claiming focus areas only apply to predictive projects is incorrect; the focus areas apply to all projects, predictive, adaptive, and hybrid. They are independent of the delivery method.","id":"book-process-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A deliverable has repeatedly failed inspection. Rather than just fixing each defect as it appears, the project manager wants to identify the single underlying reason the defects keep occurring so the source can be addressed. Which data analysis technique is the project manager using?","opts":{"A":"Variance analysis","B":"Root cause analysis","C":"Trend analysis","D":"Alternative analysis"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Root cause analysis Explanation: Root cause analysis is used to identify the main underlying reason for a particular event, such as the main cause of defects in a deliverable. Addressing the root cause, rather than repeatedly fixing the symptoms, is exactly what this technique is for. Why the other options are incorrect: Variance analysis determines the cause and degree of difference between the baseline and actual performance, such as comparing planned budget to actual cost. The scenario is about the source of defects, not a baseline variance. Trend analysis looks at data over a period of time to see whether a pattern is forming, such as whether a project is consistently over budget. The project manager is looking for a single underlying cause, not a trend over time. Alternative analysis evaluates different options or ways to accomplish something, such as build versus buy. It is not used to diagnose the cause of defects.","id":"book-process-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"While the team is still executing the current phase of a software project, the project manager is already refining requirements and re-estimating parts of the next phase, and is monitoring performance the entire time. A stakeholder objects, insisting the team must fully finish planning before any execution begins. How should the project manager respond?","opts":{"A":"Explain that the focus areas often overlap and interact; planning can continue while execution is underway, and monitoring happens continuously.","B":"Agree, because the focus areas must be completed strictly in sequence, one at a time.","C":"Stop execution until all planning is complete in order to follow PMI’s required order.","D":"Explain that monitoring only begins after the project is fully executed."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Explain that the focus areas often overlap and interact; planning can continue while execution is underway, and monitoring happens continuously. Explanation: Although PMI presents the focus areas in a sequence, they are not strictly sequential in real life. They often overlap and interact throughout the project life cycle. Planning can happen while execution is underway, and monitoring happens continuously. Assuming the focus areas happen one at a time is a common PMP exam trap. Why the other options are incorrect: Agreeing that the focus areas must be completed strictly in sequence repeats the exact misunderstanding the chapter warns against. They are not rigid, one-at-a-time steps. Stopping execution until all planning is complete is unnecessary; there is no PMI rule requiring all planning to finish before execution. Planning may be done upfront, iteratively, or in rolling waves, and this ongoing refinement is known as progressive elaboration. Saying monitoring only begins after execution is complete is incorrect; monitoring is continuous and happens throughout the project, not only at the end.","id":"book-process-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"Which THREE of the following are Organizational Process Assets (OPAs)? (Choose 3)","opts":{"A":"Templates from previous projects","B":"Government regulations the project must comply with","C":"The organization’s lessons learned repository","D":"Stakeholders’ risk tolerances","E":"Historical information from past projects","F":"The global demand for microchips"},"ans":["A","C","E"],"exp":"A, C, E Explanation: OPAs are the plans, processes, policies, procedures, templates, and knowledge bases an organization has accumulated and can reuse. Templates from previous projects, a lessons learned repository, and historical information from past projects are all stored knowledge and standard ways of working that help a project manager manage a new project. Why the other options are incorrect: Government regulations the project must comply with are an enterprise environmental factor, a contextual constraint the project must operate within rather than a reusable asset. Stakeholders’ risk tolerances are also an enterprise environmental factor; they describe the environment the project manager must plan around. The global demand for microchips is an external enterprise environmental factor, a market condition outside the organization’s control, not stored organizational knowledge.","id":"book-process-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Project Management Process","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive","type":"multi","pick":3},{"n":1,"q":"A company has identified a need for a new software system to streamline its operations. A project manager has been informally assigned to lead the effort and the team is eager to begin planning. What should happen before planning begins?","opts":{"A":"The project manager should develop a high-level project schedule to present to senior management for approval.","B":"The project sponsor should formally authorize the project and the project manager’s authority through a project charter.","C":"The team should identify all stakeholders and document their requirements so planning can begin immediately.","D":"The project manager should conduct a risk assessment to ensure the project is viable before committing resources."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The project sponsor should formally authorize the project and the project manager’s authority through a project charter. Explanation: The project charter formally authorizes the project to begin and establishes the project manager’s authority. Without it, the project manager has no official mandate to use resources or make decisions. Planning and stakeholder identification follow charter approval; they do not precede it. Why the other options are incorrect: Developing a schedule before the project is formally authorized puts the cart before the horse. The charter must exist before any planning activities begin. Identifying stakeholders is an initiating process that occurs after the charter is approved, not before it. Both initiating processes, developing the charter and identifying stakeholders, must be completed before planning starts. Risk assessment is a planning activity. Conducting it before the project is formally authorized skips the initiating process entirely.","id":"book-governance-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A company is evaluating two projects. Project A has an expected return of $150,000 and Project B has an expected return of $400,000. The company has resources to pursue only one project and selects Project B. What is the opportunity cost of this decision?","opts":{"A":"$400,000, because that is the total investment required to complete Project B.","B":"$150,000, because that is the value of Project A that was foregone by selecting Project B.","C":"$250,000, because that is the difference in return between the two projects.","D":"$550,000, because that is the combined value of both projects."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: $150,000, because that is the value of Project A that was foregone by selecting Project B. Explanation: Opportunity cost is the value of what you give up in order to pursue something else. When the company selected Project B, it gave up the entire return of Project A. The opportunity cost is therefore the full value of the rejected option, not the difference between the two, and not the cost of the chosen project. Why the other options are incorrect: Opportunity cost is not the cost or return of the chosen project. It represents the value of the alternative that was not selected. The difference between the two returns represents the net advantage of choosing Project B, not the opportunity cost. Opportunity cost is the total value of the foregone option, not the margin between them. Combining both project values produces a meaningless total. Opportunity cost represents what was sacrificed, not the sum of all options.","id":"book-governance-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"What problem does this RACI chart have? Activity Project Manager Business Analyst Developer Sponsor Define Requirements C R I A Design Solution R A R I Develop Code I C R A Test Solution A A R I Deploy System R C I A R Responsible A Accountable C Consulted I Informed","opts":{"A":"The sponsor is assigned too many accountable roles across the project activities.","B":"The developer is listed as responsible for multiple activities, creating a resource conflict.","C":"The Test Solution activity has two people listed as accountable, which creates ambiguity over ownership.","D":"The project manager is not listed as accountable for any activity, which violates governance standards."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The Test Solution activity has two people listed as accountable, which creates ambiguity over ownership. Explanation: A RACI chart should never have more than one person listed as accountable for a single activity. When two people share accountability, neither has clear ownership, and if something goes wrong each can defer responsibility to the other. The Test Solution activity lists both the Project Manager and Business Analyst as accountable, which is a violation of this principle. Why the other options are incorrect: The sponsor holds the accountable role on three activities, which is not inherently a problem. There is no rule limiting how many activities a person can be accountable for across a chart. Having a team member listed as responsible for multiple activities is normal and expected. Responsible means doing the work, and team members routinely contribute to more than one activity. There is no governance rule requiring the project manager to hold the accountable role on any particular activity. Accountability can be assigned to whichever stakeholder is most appropriate for each task.","id":"book-governance-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager is overseeing a software development project when a vendor submits a claim for additional payment, arguing that recent scope changes resulted in work beyond the original contract. The project manager disagrees with the vendor’s interpretation. No formal dispute resolution process has been attempted yet. What should the project manager do first?","opts":{"A":"Initiate direct negotiations with the vendor to attempt to resolve the disagreement.","B":"Escalate the dispute to the change control board for a binding decision.","C":"Engage a neutral third party to facilitate a resolution between both sides.","D":"Refer the matter to legal counsel to protect the organization’s contractual position."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Initiate direct negotiations with the vendor to attempt to resolve the disagreement. Explanation: When a dispute arises, the first step is always direct negotiation between the parties. This is the least formal, least costly, and least adversarial approach. More formal methods such as mediation, arbitration, or litigation are only appropriate if negotiation fails. Escalating or involving third parties before attempting direct resolution is premature. Why the other options are incorrect: The change control board manages change requests on the project, not contractual disputes between buyer and seller. This is not the appropriate body for resolving vendor claims. Engaging a neutral third party describes mediation, which is the appropriate step only if direct negotiation between the parties has already failed. Referring to legal counsel and initiating litigation is the last resort in dispute resolution. It is inappropriate to escalate to this level before attempting negotiation and other less formal methods first.","id":"book-governance-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project manager is hiring an external vendor to develop a new software application. The requirements are not yet fully defined and are expected to evolve significantly based on user feedback during development. The project manager is considering contract types. Which contract type is most appropriate for this situation?","opts":{"A":"Fixed price","B":"Fixed price with incentive fee","C":"Target cost","D":"Time and materials"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Time and materials Explanation: Time and materials contracts pay for actual time and materials at pre-agreed rates, making them well suited to work where the scope cannot be fully defined upfront. As requirements evolve, the team can adjust the work without renegotiating the contract or triggering disputes over scope boundaries. Why the other options are incorrect: Fixed price requires well-defined scope to be effective. With evolving requirements, the vendor must pad the price to cover uncertainty, and any scope change risks triggering a dispute over what is and is not included in the contract. Fixed price with incentive fee still assumes a defined scope and target to measure performance against. It is not suited to situations where requirements are expected to shift significantly throughout the project. Target cost contracts require a reasonably estimable scope baseline to set the target. With requirements expected to evolve significantly based on user feedback, there is no reliable basis for setting a meaningful cost target.","id":"book-governance-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager is planning a large ERP implementation. The organization has no internal staff with ERP experience, the implementation timeline is aggressive, and the system involves complex integrations that carry significant technical risk. What should the project manager recommend?","opts":{"A":"Insource the implementation using the existing IT team.","B":"Outsource the implementation to an experienced external vendor.","C":"Split the implementation between internal staff and an external vendor equally.","D":"Delay the project until internal staff can be trained on ERP implementation."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Outsource the implementation to an experienced external vendor. Explanation: When an organization lacks the necessary expertise, faces time pressure, and the work carries significant technical risk, outsourcing is the appropriate choice. External vendors bring specialized knowledge and experience that reduce both execution risk and timeline pressure. The make-or-buy decision is driven by factors including expertise, cost, risk, and timeline, not cost alone. Why the other options are incorrect: Assigning the implementation to a team with no ERP experience introduces unnecessary risk and is unlikely to meet the aggressive timeline. Lack of expertise is a key factor that points toward outsourcing. Splitting the work equally assumes internal staff can contribute meaningfully to a complex implementation they have no experience with. This does not address the expertise or risk concerns and may slow the experienced vendor down. Delaying the project to train staff ignores the timeline constraint entirely. Training internal staff for a complex ERP implementation would take considerable time and still may not produce the expertise level an experienced vendor already has.","id":"book-governance-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project is midway through execution when the project sponsor calls the project manager and requests an immediate decision on a scope change that would add a significant new feature to the product. The sponsor is eager to move forward and is asking for approval on the spot. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Approve the change since it is coming from the sponsor and the sponsor has authority over the project.","B":"Deny the change to protect the project schedule and budget from unplanned additions.","C":"Assess the impact of the change on scope, schedule, cost, and risk before making any decision.","D":"Ask the team to begin preliminary work on the feature while the change request is being evaluated, to accelerate the timeline."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Assess the impact of the change on scope, schedule, cost, and risk before making any decision. Explanation: No change request should ever be approved or denied without first assessing its full impact on the project. Even when pressure comes from a senior stakeholder like the sponsor, the project manager’s responsibility is to evaluate the change against scope, schedule, cost, quality, and risk before making a recommendation. On a traditional project this means formal change control. On an agile project, the change would be added to the product backlog and prioritized by the product owner. Either way, skipping assessment leads to uninformed decisions that can derail the project. Why the other options are incorrect: The sponsor’s authority does not bypass the change control process. Even sponsor-initiated changes must go through proper assessment before approval. Denying a change without assessment is just as problematic as approving it without assessment. The change may be entirely justified once its impact is understood. Beginning work before a change is approved violates change control entirely. Work on unapproved changes can introduce unauthorized scope, consume resources, and create rework if the change is ultimately denied.","id":"book-governance-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project manager is explaining the quality assurance activities planned for a software development project to a new team member. The team member asks what quality assurance will look like in practice. Which of the following best describes a quality assurance activity?","opts":{"A":"Testing the completed application to verify it meets all functional requirements before release.","B":"Reviewing the team’s development process to ensure coding standards and best practices are being followed throughout the project.","C":"Inspecting each deliverable for defects before it is presented to the customer for acceptance.","D":"Conducting a final audit of the product to confirm it meets the quality requirements defined in the quality management plan."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Reviewing the team’s development process to ensure coding standards and best practices are being followed throughout the project. Explanation: Quality assurance focuses on the process used to create the product, not the product itself. By ensuring the team follows correct standards and practices throughout development, QA aims to prevent defects before they occur. This is fundamentally different from quality control, which inspects the actual product for defects after the work is done. Why the other options are incorrect: Testing the completed application to verify it meets requirements is quality control. It focuses on the final product and whether it meets defined criteria, not on how the work was performed. Inspecting deliverables for defects before customer acceptance is also quality control. The focus is on the output rather than the process that produced it. Conducting a final audit of the product is quality control. Even though it references the quality management plan, the activity is product-focused and occurs after the work is complete, which is the defining characteristic of QC rather than QA.","id":"book-governance-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is reviewing project status when a team member reports that the primary vendor has been experiencing financial difficulties and there is a chance they may be unable to fulfill their contract obligations next quarter. How should the project manager classify and respond to this situation?","opts":{"A":"Log it as an issue and immediately begin sourcing an alternative vendor.","B":"Log it as an issue and escalate to the sponsor since it threatens the project timeline.","C":"Take no action until the vendor formally notifies the project of a problem.","D":"Log it as a risk, assess its probability and impact, and develop a response plan."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Log it as a risk, assess its probability and impact, and develop a response plan. Explanation: A risk is an uncertain future event that could affect the project. The vendor has not failed to deliver yet; there is only a possibility they may do so. This is a risk that should be logged in the risk register, assessed for probability and potential impact, and responded to proactively. Treating it as an issue when nothing has gone wrong yet would be premature and lead to unnecessary disruption. Why the other options are incorrect: Logging it as an issue and immediately sourcing an alternative vendor treats a potential future problem as if it has already occurred. The vendor is still operational and no contractual failure has taken place. Escalating to the sponsor as an issue has the same problem. The situation warrants proactive risk management, not crisis escalation. Waiting for formal notification is reactive and dangerous. A project manager should act on early warning signs by identifying and planning for risks before they materialize, not wait until the damage is done.","id":"book-governance-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A senior developer is transitioning off a project after completing a critical module. Before leaving, she thoroughly documented her work, including technical specifications, process guides, and step-by-step instructions. Two weeks later, the replacement developer is struggling to maintain the module despite having access to all the documentation. What most likely explains this gap?","opts":{"A":"The documentation was not detailed enough to capture the full scope of the work.","B":"The replacement developer lacks the technical skills needed for this type of work.","C":"The outgoing developer’s knowledge was largely tacit and could not be fully captured through documentation alone.","D":"The project manager failed to ensure the documentation was reviewed and approved before the transition."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The outgoing developer’s knowledge was largely tacit and could not be fully captured through documentation alone. Explanation: Tacit knowledge is experience-based and includes intuition, judgment, and the kind of understanding that comes from years of hands-on experience. Even thorough documentation captures only explicit knowledge. The nuanced understanding of how the module behaves under different conditions, why certain decisions were made, and how to troubleshoot edge cases lives in the developer’s experience and can only be transferred through direct interaction such as paired work, shadowing, or mentoring. Why the other options are incorrect: The scenario states the documentation was thorough. The problem is not the level of detail but the nature of the knowledge itself, which cannot be fully captured in written form regardless of how comprehensive the documentation is. There is no indication the replacement developer lacks the necessary skills. The gap is in transferred knowledge, not capability. Approving documentation before a transition is a process step that would not have addressed the fundamental limitation of trying to capture tacit knowledge through explicit means.","id":"book-governance-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager has been collecting data on task completion rates, hours worked, and costs incurred during project execution. The project sponsor has asked for an update on how the project is performing. What should the project manager provide?","opts":{"A":"A work performance report summarizing the project’s current status and any corrective actions needed.","B":"The task completion rates, hours worked, and cost figures collected during execution.","C":"A comparison of the collected data against the project management plan to show variances.","D":"An updated project schedule showing which tasks have been completed and which are still in progress."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: A work performance report summarizing the project’s current status and any corrective actions needed. Explanation: Work performance data is the information collected during execution. It is analyzed against the project management plan to produce work performance information, which shows the status of the project. That information is then packaged into a work performance report for communication to stakeholders. The sponsor is asking for a status update, which is a work performance report, not unanalyzed data or unformatted analysis. Why the other options are incorrect: Providing work performance data directly to the sponsor skips the analysis and packaging steps. Raw data is not very useful for stakeholders. Comparing data against the plan produces work performance information, in this case a variance analysis. While valuable internally, it still needs to be packaged into a report before being shared with the sponsor. An updated schedule shows task status but does not constitute a full project performance report. The sponsor needs a broader picture of project health including cost, schedule, and any issues or corrective actions, not just a schedule view.","id":"book-governance-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is closing out a software implementation project. The sponsor has formally accepted all deliverables, the product has been transitioned to the operations team, vendor contracts have been closed, and the project team has been released. The project manager is preparing to submit the final report. What should the project manager do before submitting the final report?","opts":{"A":"Conduct a final risk review to confirm all identified risks were resolved or accepted.","B":"Update the project schedule to reflect the actual completion dates of all activities.","C":"Send a closure notification to all stakeholders confirming the project is complete.","D":"Document and archive the lessons learned from the project."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Document and archive the lessons learned from the project. Explanation: Lessons learned must be documented and archived before the project is formally closed. They capture what went well, what did not, and what should be done differently on future projects. This knowledge is stored in the organization’s lessons learned repository so it can benefit future project teams. Submitting the final report without completing this step leaves valuable organizational knowledge uncaptured. Why the other options are incorrect: A final risk review is not a required closing activity. Risks are managed throughout the project and any remaining open risks would have been addressed or accepted before reaching this point. Updating the schedule to reflect actual completion dates is a minor administrative task that would have been done during execution and monitoring, not as a final closing step. A closure notification is a communication activity that typically follows formal closure rather than preceding it. It does not substitute for documenting lessons learned.","id":"book-governance-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager is leading a traditional infrastructure project that has just completed a major phase. The team encountered several unexpected challenges during execution and the project manager wants to ensure the experience is properly examined and the findings captured before the team moves into the next phase. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Conduct a sprint retrospective with the team.","B":"Hold a lessons learned meeting to document findings for the final project report.","C":"Conduct an after-action review with the team.","D":"Commission a quality audit to identify process deficiencies."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Conduct an after-action review with the team. Explanation: An after-action review is a structured reflection that asks four key questions: what was supposed to happen, what actually happened, why were there differences, and what can be learned. It is designed to be conducted during or after a project milestone on any type of project, not just agile. The findings are documented and shared so the knowledge is not lost before the next phase begins. Why the other options are incorrect: A sprint retrospective is an agile ceremony conducted at the end of a sprint. This is a traditional project, so a retrospective is not the appropriate tool. A lessons learned meeting focused on documentation for the final project report is oriented toward end-of-project capture, not the structured mid-project reflection needed here. An after-action review is the more appropriate tool for examining a completed phase before moving forward. A quality audit examines whether processes and standards are being followed. It is not designed to capture experiential learning or examine why planned outcomes differed from actual outcomes.","id":"book-governance-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Agile"},{"n":14,"q":"A senior project manager is mentoring a junior colleague who has just been assigned to lead their first complex stakeholder negotiation. The senior PM has managed dozens of similar negotiations over the years and wants to help prepare them effectively. What is the most effective way for the senior PM to transfer this knowledge?","opts":{"A":"Provide the junior PM with the organization’s stakeholder management templates and guidelines.","B":"Share stories from past negotiations, including what went wrong, how situations were handled, and what was learned.","C":"Share documented negotiation frameworks and best practice guides from the PMO library.","D":"Arrange for the junior PM to attend a formal negotiation training course before the engagement."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Share stories from past negotiations, including what went wrong, how situations were handled, and what was learned. Explanation: Stakeholder negotiation expertise is largely tacit knowledge built through experience and judgment. Storytelling is a knowledge transfer technique specifically suited to sharing this type of knowledge because it conveys context, emotion, and real-world nuance that templates and frameworks cannot capture. Why the other options are incorrect: Templates and guidelines capture explicit knowledge about process steps but cannot convey the judgment and situational awareness developed through experience. They are useful tools but insufficient for preparing someone for the complexity of real negotiations. Documented frameworks from the PMO library have the same limitation. They describe how negotiations should work in theory but cannot replicate the tacit knowledge gained from actually navigating difficult stakeholder dynamics. Formal training provides structured learning but is generally knowledge-based rather than experience-based. It cannot substitute for the transfer of hard-won experiential knowledge that storytelling provides.","id":"book-governance-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A project manager has just been assigned to a new project and is meeting with key stakeholders to gather information before drafting the project charter. The PM wants to visually organize the project’s purpose, objectives, scope, risks, milestones, and team in one place during these early conversations. Which tool is most appropriate for this purpose?","opts":{"A":"Project canvas","B":"Business case","C":"Scope statement","D":"Benefits management plan"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Project canvas Explanation: The project canvas is a visual tool designed to capture and organize key project elements at a high level during initiation. It is particularly useful before the charter is drafted because it helps the PM and stakeholders align on the project’s purpose, objectives, constraints, and risks in a flexible, collaborative format. Unlike formal documents, it can be easily updated as conversations evolve. Why the other options are incorrect: The business case justifies why the project should be done by analyzing costs, benefits, and return on investment. It is a financial and strategic justification document, not a tool for organizing project elements visually. The scope statement formally defines what is and is not included in the project. It is a planning document created after the project is authorized, not a tool for early stakeholder alignment. The benefits management plan describes the benefits the project is expected to deliver and how they will be measured. Like the business case, it is focused on justification and value realization rather than organizing project elements for charter development.","id":"book-governance-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Governance","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A project manager is reviewing the approved project documentation before the team begins execution. The project scope statement, work breakdown structure, and WBS dictionary have all been completed and approved. What does this collection of documents represent?","opts":{"A":"The project management plan","B":"The scope baseline","C":"The requirements documentation","D":"The scope management plan"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The scope baseline Explanation: The scope baseline is made up of exactly three components: the project scope statement, the WBS, and the WBS dictionary. Together they define the approved scope against which all project performance is measured. Any changes to the scope baseline after approval require a formal change request. Why the other options are incorrect: The project management plan encompasses far more than these three documents. It includes subsidiary plans, other baselines, and additional components well beyond the scope statement, WBS, and WBS dictionary. Requirements documentation does not include any of these items. It captures stakeholder requirements gathered during planning and is a separate document from the three described. The scope management plan describes how scope will be managed throughout the project and is a single planning document, not a collection of the three described.","id":"book-scope-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager is leading a website redesign project. During a planning meeting, a stakeholder says the project should include “a faster checkout process, mobile-friendly pages, and a product search feature.” The project manager notes these down but separately documents that the project will require “migrating existing product data, rebuilding the payment gateway integration, and conducting user acceptance testing.” Which of the following correctly categorizes what the stakeholder described versus what the project manager documented?","opts":{"A":"The stakeholder described product scope; the project manager documented project scope.","B":"The stakeholder described project scope; the project manager documented product scope.","C":"Both the stakeholder and the project manager described project scope, just at different levels of detail.","D":"The stakeholder described quality requirements; the project manager documented project scope."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: The stakeholder described product scope; the project manager documented project scope. Explanation: The stakeholder listed features and functions the website will have. These describe what the product does, which is product scope. The project manager documented the work the team must perform to build those features. That is project scope. Why the other options are incorrect: Features and functions like checkout speed and mobile compatibility describe the product’s capabilities, not the work required to build it. The two lists are not the same type of information at different detail levels. One describes product capabilities and the other describes work tasks. They are categorically different. The stakeholder’s list describes functional capabilities of the website, not quality standards like load time thresholds or defect rates, which is what quality requirements would address.","id":"book-scope-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A software development team has just finished building a new customer reporting module. Before sending it to the client for review, the project manager has the internal QA team run it through a series of functional tests to confirm it meets the documented technical specifications and performance standards. Which process is the project manager performing?","opts":{"A":"Validate Scope","B":"Monitor and Control Scope","C":"Define Scope","D":"Manage Quality Assurance"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Monitor and Control Scope Explanation: Monitor and Control Scope is the process in which deliverables are inspected and tested to confirm they meet the quality and scope requirements before they are presented to the customer. The internal QA team testing the module against its technical specifications and performance standards is performing exactly this inspection, and the result is a verified deliverable. That verified deliverable then becomes an input to Validate Scope, where the customer formally accepts it. Why the other options are incorrect: Validate Scope is performed by the customer or sponsor, not the internal team. It is the process of formally accepting completed deliverables, and it occurs after the deliverable has already been verified. Manage Quality Assurance focuses on whether the team is following the correct processes and standards while building the product, not on inspecting a finished deliverable against its specifications. Define Scope is a planning process in which the project scope statement is created. It occurs well before any deliverables are built or tested.","id":"book-scope-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager presents a completed deliverable to the project sponsor for review. The sponsor examines it, confirms it meets all documented requirements and acceptance criteria, and signs off on it. The project manager updates the project records to reflect the formal approval. Which process has just been completed?","opts":{"A":"Manage Quality Assurance","B":"Perform Integrated Change Control","C":"Validate Scope","D":"Monitor and Control Scope"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Validate Scope Explanation: Validate Scope is the process of obtaining formal acceptance of completed deliverables from the customer or sponsor. When the sponsor reviews the deliverable against the documented acceptance criteria and signs off, that is Validate Scope. The key indicator is formal acceptance by an authorized stakeholder, not internal inspection by the team. Why the other options are incorrect: Manage Quality Assurance focuses on ensuring the team follows the correct processes and standards while building the product. It does not involve the formal customer or sponsor signoff that defines Validate Scope. Perform Integrated Change Control is the process of reviewing and approving or rejecting change requests. No change request is involved here; the deliverable is being reviewed and accepted as completed work. Monitor and Control Scope tracks whether the project is staying within its approved scope and manages scope variances. It does not involve formal acceptance of deliverables by a sponsor.","id":"book-scope-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project team completes a new employee onboarding portal and submits it to the HR director for review. The HR director tests several workflows, finds that the document upload feature does not work as specified, and sends it back to the team for corrections. Once the team fixes the issue, the HR director reviews it again, confirms it now meets all requirements, and formally approves it. Which of the following correctly describes the sequence of events?","opts":{"A":"Validate Scope was performed twice, once when the defect was found and once when the deliverable was approved.","B":"Manage Quality Assurance was performed when the defect was found, and Validate Scope was performed when the deliverable was approved.","C":"Validate Scope was performed when the defect was found, and Manage Quality Assurance was performed when the deliverable was approved.","D":"Monitor and Control Scope was performed when the defect was found, and Validate Scope was performed when the deliverable was approved."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Validate Scope was performed twice, once when the defect was found and once when the deliverable was approved. Explanation: Validate Scope is performed whenever an authorized stakeholder formally reviews a deliverable for acceptance. The HR director, as the customer, performed this review both times. The first review resulted in rejection due to a defect, and the second resulted in formal approval. Validate Scope does not always end in acceptance; it can also result in a rejected deliverable and a change request for defect repair. Why the other options are incorrect: Manage Quality Assurance concerns the processes the team follows while building the product; it is not the process used when an authorized stakeholder reviews a finished deliverable for acceptance. The defect here was found by the HR director during a formal acceptance review, which is Validate Scope. Monitor and Control Scope tracks overall scope boundaries and manages scope variances across the project. It is not the process used when a customer reviews a deliverable for acceptance.","id":"book-scope-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager receives an approved change request to add a new integration feature to a software project. The team begins work on the feature immediately. During a later performance review, the project manager notices the project appears to be over scope based on the current measurements. What most likely caused this discrepancy?","opts":{"A":"The change request was not properly authorized before work began.","B":"The scope baseline was not updated to reflect the approved change.","C":"The team added additional features beyond what the approved change request described.","D":"The performance review was conducted too early to account for the new work."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The scope baseline was not updated to reflect the approved change. Explanation: When a change request is approved, the scope baseline must be updated to reflect the new approved scope. If this step is skipped, the project will appear to be over scope because performance is being measured against a baseline that no longer reflects what the project is supposed to deliver. The change was legitimate, but failing to update the baseline creates a false variance. Why the other options are incorrect: The change request was approved before work began, so authorization is not the issue. The problem occurs after approval, in the failure to update the baseline. There is no indication in the scenario that the team exceeded the approved change. The discrepancy is between the approved new scope and the outdated baseline, not between the team’s work and the change request. The timing of the performance review does not cause a scope discrepancy. A baseline that has not been updated will produce inaccurate measurements regardless of when the review is conducted.","id":"book-scope-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is gathering requirements for a new enterprise risk management system. The project involves several senior executives who are known to have strong opinions. The project manager is concerned that participants will tailor their responses to align with the CEO’s known preferences rather than providing their honest expert judgment. Which requirements gathering technique is most appropriate?","opts":{"A":"Facilitated workshop","B":"Focus group","C":"Delphi technique","D":"Interviews"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Delphi technique Explanation: The Delphi technique collects requirements anonymously from subject matter experts, preventing participants from tailoring their responses to organizational hierarchy or the preferences of senior leadership. Anonymity is what separates Delphi from other techniques when objectivity is the primary concern. Why the other options are incorrect: A facilitated workshop brings stakeholders together in the same session, directly exposing participants to the influence of dominant personalities. A focus group has the same problem. Open interaction among participants makes it equally susceptible to social and hierarchical pressure. Interviews gather input individually and avoid group dynamics, but they are not anonymous. Participants can still tailor their responses to perceived expectations in a one-on-one setting.","id":"book-scope-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"Midway through a software development project, a stakeholder claims that a specific reporting feature was never part of the agreed requirements and should not be included in the current sprint. The development team insists the feature was requested during the planning phase. The project manager needs to resolve the dispute quickly. What tool should the project manager consult?","opts":{"A":"The project scope statement","B":"The requirements traceability matrix","C":"The WBS dictionary","D":"The lessons learned register"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The requirements traceability matrix Explanation: The requirements traceability matrix links each requirement to the stakeholder who requested it, the business need it addresses, and the deliverable it maps to. When a dispute arises over whether a requirement was officially requested and agreed upon, the RTM provides a clear, traceable record. Why the other options are incorrect: The project scope statement describes what the project will deliver but does not trace individual requirements back to their origin or the stakeholder who requested them. The WBS dictionary provides detailed descriptions of work packages but focuses on how work will be done rather than where each requirement came from or who requested it. The lessons learned register captures knowledge from past experiences. It is not a tracking tool for current project requirements or their origins.","id":"book-scope-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is building the WBS for a website development project. The team is trying to determine the appropriate level of decomposition for the checkout functionality. Which of the following best represents a work package?","opts":{"A":"E-commerce website","B":"Checkout system","C":"Develop checkout page","D":"Implement payment gateway, configure tax calculation, build order confirmation page, and integrate shipping API"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Develop checkout page Explanation: A work package is a unit of scope that is specific enough to be estimated, assigned, monitored, and controlled as a single piece of work. “Develop checkout page” meets this criteria. It is clearly defined, actionable, and manageable without being so broad that it cannot be tracked or so granular that it becomes a list of tasks. Why the other options are incorrect: “E-commerce website” is the top-level deliverable of the entire project. It is far too broad to estimate, assign, or control as a single unit and requires significant further decomposition. “Checkout system” is a control account or deliverable level item, not a work package. It still needs to be broken down into more specific pieces of work before it can be estimated and assigned. Listing individual technical tasks like configuring tax calculation and integrating APIs represents activity-level decomposition, which is below the work package level. Work packages are decomposed into activities during schedule planning, not during WBS development.","id":"book-scope-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project team is building a new customer portal. They deliver working features to the client every two weeks, gather feedback, and use that input to shape what gets built next. Requirements have been evolving since the project started as the client develops a clearer picture of what they need. Where is the project scope most likely stored?","opts":{"A":"Project scope statement","B":"Requirements traceability matrix","C":"WBS dictionary","D":"Product backlog"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Product backlog Explanation: The scenario describes an adaptive approach: short delivery cycles, continuous customer feedback, and evolving requirements. On agile projects, scope is not locked down in a scope statement upfront. Instead, it is captured and managed in the product backlog, which is continuously refined and reprioritized as the project progresses and more is learned. Why the other options are incorrect: The project scope statement is used on predictive projects where scope is defined in detail before execution begins. The scenario describes a project where requirements are still evolving, which is inconsistent with a fully defined scope statement. The requirements traceability matrix tracks where requirements came from and links them to deliverables. It is a tracking tool, not where scope is stored or managed. The WBS dictionary provides detailed descriptions of work packages. It supports the WBS and scope baseline on traditional projects, not the evolving scope of an agile project.","id":"book-scope-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":11,"q":"At the end of a sprint, the development team declares the user authentication feature complete and moves it to the done column on the Kanban board. During the sprint review, the product owner rejects the feature, stating that it does not include password reset functionality, which she considered an obvious requirement. The development team argues they built exactly what was described in the user story. What most likely was the root cause of this situation?","opts":{"A":"The team and product owner did not have a shared agreement on what criteria the feature needed to meet before it could be considered complete.","B":"The user story was not detailed enough to capture all the required functionality.","C":"The product owner should have been more involved during the sprint to catch the gap earlier.","D":"The sprint review was conducted too late to make corrections within the sprint timeline."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: The team and product owner did not have a shared agreement on what criteria the feature needed to meet before it could be considered complete. Explanation: The definition of done is a shared understanding between the team and stakeholders of what it means for work to be finished. Without it, the team and product owner operate on different assumptions about what complete means. The dispute here is not about effort or communication timing but about the absence of agreed criteria that both sides understood before work began. Why the other options are incorrect: It is partly true that the user story may have lacked detail, but the deeper problem is not just detail. It is that the team and product owner did not align on the conditions for acceptance. More involvement might have helped detect the gap earlier, but it does not identify the primary root cause, lack of alignment on DoD. The timing of the sprint review is not the real issue. The issue existed before the review because expectations were never aligned.","id":"book-scope-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is reviewing progress reports and notices that several deliverables have been completed later than their planned dates. Which analysis should the project manager perform first?","opts":{"A":"Risk analysis","B":"Variance analysis","C":"Trend analysis","D":"Cost-benefit analysis"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Variance analysis Explanation: Variance analysis compares planned work against actual work to identify and quantify deviations from the baseline. When deliverables are finishing late, the first step is to measure how significant the gap is and which activities are affected before deciding on a course of action. This gives the project manager the factual basis needed to determine the root cause and select an appropriate response. Why the other options are incorrect: Risk analysis focuses on uncertainty and possible future events, not first diagnosis of an observed delay. Trend analysis can help later to see whether delays are worsening or improving over time, but first you confirm and measure the current variance. Cost-benefit analysis is used to evaluate response options, not to diagnose schedule slippage.","id":"book-scope-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager reviews the last six weekly status reports and notices that the team has been consistently delivering fewer story points than planned in each successive sprint. The gap between planned and actual output has been gradually widening since the project began. Which analysis should the project manager perform?","opts":{"A":"Risk analysis","B":"Variance analysis","C":"Trend analysis","D":"Cost-benefit analysis"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Trend analysis Explanation: Trend analysis examines project performance over time to determine whether performance is improving, declining, or deviating from the plan. The pattern described, a consistent and widening gap across multiple reporting periods, is exactly what trend analysis is designed to identify. It helps the project manager understand the direction performance is heading so they can act before the situation worsens. Why the other options are incorrect: Risk analysis addresses potential future events and uncertainty. The pattern described is an observed performance trend, not a hypothetical risk. Variance analysis focuses on the difference between planned and actual performance at a given point or reporting period. Here, the scenario emphasizes the direction and pattern across time, which makes trend analysis the better answer. Cost-benefit analysis evaluates corrective options once a problem is understood. It is not a diagnostic tool and is premature before the trend has been fully analyzed.","id":"book-scope-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":14,"q":"What conclusion should the project manager draw from this control chart?","opts":{"A":"The process is out of control because 8 data points exceed the upper control limit.","B":"The process is in control because all data points fall within the upper and lower control limits.","C":"The process requires investigation because of a nonrandom pattern in the data.","D":"The process is performing mostly above the mean, which indicates improvement and requires no action."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The process requires investigation because of a nonrandom pattern in the data. Explanation: The rule of seven states that seven or more consecutive data points on the same side of the mean indicate a nonrandom pattern, even if all points remain within the control limits. Samples 6 through 13 are all above the mean, triggering this rule. While no points have breached the control limits, the consistent pattern signals that something systematic may be affecting the process and warrants investigation. Why the other options are incorrect: No data points exceed the upper control limit. Breaching the control limits is one trigger for investigation, but it is not the only one. The rule of seven applies independently of whether the limits are breached. While all points are within the control limits, a process is not automatically considered in control simply because limits are not breached. The consecutive pattern above the mean indicates the process may not be behaving randomly, which requires investigation. Performing above the mean may seem positive, but the rule of seven does not distinguish between favorable and unfavorable patterns. Any nonrandom consecutive pattern on either side of the mean requires investigation regardless of direction.","id":"book-scope-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A quality review on a software project identifies 200 total defects across five categories: Defect Type Count UI rendering errors Login Data sync issues Notification bugs Report formatting The project manager wants to visualize this data to help the team identify which defect types to address first. Which chart should the project manager use?","opts":{"A":"Burndown chart","B":"Control chart","C":"Scatter diagram","D":"Pareto chart"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Pareto chart Explanation: A Pareto chart displays categories of problems in descending order of frequency, helping the team quickly identify which causes are responsible for the majority of issues. The data is already organized by frequency, which is exactly the input a Pareto chart is designed to visualize. It directly supports the decision of where to focus improvement efforts first. Why the other options are incorrect: A burndown chart tracks remaining work over time in agile projects. A control chart tracks process performance over time against control limits to identify whether a process is stable. A scatter diagram shows the relationship between two variables. It is used to identify correlations, not to rank and prioritize categories of problems.","id":"book-scope-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Scope","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A project has three paths through its network diagram with the following durations: Path 1: 18 days Path 2: 22 days Path 3: 20 days Activity K is on Path 1 only and has a duration of 4 days. The project manager is informed that Activity K will be delayed by 3 days. What is the impact on the project end date?","opts":{"A":"The project end date will be delayed by 3 days","B":"The project end date will not be affected","C":"The project end date will be delayed by 1 day","D":"The project end date may be affected depending on whether Activity K has sufficient float to absorb the delay."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The project end date will not be affected because Activity K is not on the critical path. Explanation: The critical path is the longest path through the network diagram, which is Path 2 at 22 days. Path 1 has a duration of 18 days, giving activities on that path 4 days of float. Activity K is on Path 1 only, meaning a 3-day delay still keeps Path 1 at 21 days, which is shorter than the critical path. The project end date remains 22 days. Why the other options are incorrect: A delay on a non-critical activity does not automatically delay the project. Only delays that exceed available float on a given path will affect the project end date. A 3-day delay to Activity K brings Path 1 to 21 days, which is still shorter than Path 2 at 22 days. Path 1 does not become the critical path. The float on Path 1 is already known from the information given. Path 1 has 4 days of float, and the delay is only 3 days, so the outcome can be determined with certainty.","id":"book-schedule-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project is running two weeks behind schedule. The project manager reviews the schedule and decides to bring in two additional contractors to work alongside the existing team on the most time-critical activities. Which schedule compression technique is being used?","opts":{"A":"Fast-tracking","B":"Resource leveling","C":"Crashing","D":"Resource smoothing"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Crashing Explanation: Crashing involves adding resources to critical path activities to shorten their duration. It typically increases cost but does not inherently change the sequence of activities. Bringing in additional contractors to accelerate time-critical work is a classic example of crashing. Why the other options are incorrect: Fast-tracking involves performing activities in parallel that were originally planned sequentially. No change to the sequence of activities is described in this scenario. Resource leveling adjusts activity dates to resolve resource overallocation or unavailability. It typically extends the schedule rather than compressing it. Resource smoothing adjusts activities within their available float to optimize resource usage without changing the project end date. It is not a compression technique.","id":"book-schedule-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A construction crew is installing drywall while an electrical team runs cables behind the walls simultaneously. The drywall installation cannot be completed until the electrical wiring is finished. Which dependency relationship does this describe?","opts":{"A":"Finish-to-Finish","B":"Finish-to-Start","C":"Start-to-Finish","D":"Start-to-Start"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Finish-to-Finish Explanation: A Finish-to-Finish relationship means the successor activity can run concurrently with the predecessor but cannot finish until the predecessor finishes. Both teams are working at the same time, but drywall installation is constrained by when wiring is complete. Why the other options are incorrect: Finish-to-Start means the successor cannot begin until the predecessor is complete. Both activities are running simultaneously here, which rules this out. Start-to-Finish means the successor cannot finish until the predecessor starts. This is the rarest dependency type and does not describe this relationship. Start-to-Start means the successor cannot start until the predecessor starts. The constraint here is about completion, not initiation.","id":"book-schedule-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager is scheduling a software deployment. After the final code is committed, the team must wait 48 hours for automated security scans to complete before the deployment can begin. What scheduling concept does this best represent?","opts":{"A":"A lead, because the deployment is being accelerated relative to the code commit.","B":"A dependency, because deployment cannot begin until the code commit is complete.","C":"Schedule compression, because the team is optimizing the time between activities.","D":"A lag, because a mandatory waiting period exists between the two activities."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: A lag, because a mandatory waiting period exists between the two activities. Explanation: A lag is a delay between activities. When one activity finishes and the next cannot begin until a defined waiting period has passed, that waiting period is a lag. The 48-hour security scan window is a required delay between code commit and deployment. Why the other options are incorrect: A lead is an overlap between activities, where the successor starts before the predecessor is fully complete. There is no overlap described here, only a delay. While a dependency does exist between the two activities, the specific scheduling concept being described is the 48-hour delay, which is a lag. Dependency is a broader term that does not capture the waiting period specifically. A lag is a delay applied within a logical dependency relationship between two activities. Schedule compression refers to techniques used to shorten the overall project schedule, such as crashing or fast-tracking. A waiting period between activities is the opposite of compression.","id":"book-schedule-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project team has just completed a project. Based on the burndown chart, which of the following best describes the project’s overall performance?","opts":{"A":"The project started ahead of schedule, slowed in the middle, and finished late.","B":"The project started behind schedule, recovered steadily, and finished ahead of schedule.","C":"The project was consistently on schedule throughout all sprints.","D":"The project started behind schedule and never recovered, finishing late."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The project started behind schedule, recovered steadily, and finished ahead of schedule. Explanation: In a burndown chart, when the actual line is above the ideal line, the project is behind schedule because more work remains than planned. When the actual line drops below the ideal line, the project is ahead of schedule. In this chart, the actual line starts above the ideal line indicating early delays, crosses over around sprint 5 as the team recovers, and reaches zero before the ideal line confirming the project finished ahead of schedule. Why the other options are incorrect: The actual line starts above the ideal line in early sprints, meaning the project was behind schedule at the start, not ahead. The actual line is not consistently aligned with the ideal line. It starts above, crosses over, and finishes below, indicating meaningful variation throughout the project. The actual line crosses below the ideal line and reaches zero before it, confirming the project recovered and finished ahead of schedule, not late.","id":"book-schedule-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager notices that a key developer is assigned to three overlapping activities during the same two-week window, making it impossible for her to complete all three simultaneously. The project manager shifts two of the activities to start after the first one finishes, which extends the project end date by four days. Which resource optimization technique is being used?","opts":{"A":"Resource leveling","B":"Resource smoothing","C":"Schedule compression","D":"Fast-tracking"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Resource leveling Explanation: Resource leveling is used when resources are overallocated or unavailable at the required times. The project manager adjusts activity dates to fit actual resource capacity, which often extends the schedule. The four-day extension to the project end date is the defining indicator that leveling, not smoothing, was applied. Why the other options are incorrect: Resource smoothing also adjusts activity timing but only within available float, ensuring the project end date does not change. Since the end date was extended, smoothing was not the technique used. Schedule compression shortens the schedule rather than extending it. Moving activities to run sequentially because of resource constraints is the opposite of compression. Fast-tracking runs activities in parallel to shorten the schedule. The project manager here moved activities from parallel to sequential, which is the opposite of fast-tracking.","id":"book-schedule-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is estimating the duration of a complex software integration activity. The team estimates the optimistic duration at 4 days, the most likely duration at 7 days, and the pessimistic duration at 16 days. What is the PERT beta distribution estimate for this activity?","opts":{"A":"9 days","B":"7 days","C":"6 days","D":"8 days"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: 8 days Explanation: The PERT beta distribution formula is (O + 4M + P) / 6. Applying the values: (4 + 4×7 + 16) / 6 = (4 + 28 + 16) / 6 = 48 / 6 = 8 days. Why the other options are incorrect: 9 days would result from incorrectly averaging the three values without applying the weighted formula, or from a calculation error. 7 days is simply the most likely estimate on its own. While it represents the most probable outcome, the PERT formula accounts for optimistic and pessimistic scenarios by weighting them into the estimate. 6 days would result from a calculation error.","id":"book-schedule-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project manager is building a schedule for a complex IT infrastructure project where several key engineers are shared across multiple activities. Rather than adding time buffers to individual tasks, the project manager removes those buffers, tightens the activity durations, and adds a single buffer at the end of the schedule to protect the project deadline. Which scheduling technique is being used?","opts":{"A":"Critical path method","B":"Resource leveling","C":"Critical chain method","D":"Schedule compression"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Critical chain method Explanation: The critical chain method accounts for resource constraints and uncertainty by removing individual task buffers and replacing them with a single project buffer at the end of the schedule. This approach counteracts Parkinson’s Law, where work expands to fill available time, by keeping individual activities tight while still protecting the overall deadline. Why the other options are incorrect: The critical path method identifies the longest path through the network diagram but does not account for resource availability or replace individual buffers with a project buffer. Resource leveling adjusts activity dates to resolve resource overallocation. It does not involve removing task buffers or adding a project buffer at the end of the schedule. Schedule compression shortens the overall schedule through crashing or fast-tracking. It does not involve buffer management or resource-constrained scheduling.","id":"book-schedule-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is developing the schedule for a two-year infrastructure project. The sponsor asks why the schedule only shows detailed activities for the first three months, with the remaining work represented as high-level milestones. What should the project manager explain?","opts":{"A":"The team does not yet have enough resources assigned to plan the remaining activities.","B":"Work is planned in detail as the project advances and more information becomes available.","C":"The project management plan has not yet been approved, so detailed scheduling cannot be completed.","D":"Detailed scheduling for a two-year project is typically deferred until the execution phase begins."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Work is planned in detail as the project advances and more information becomes available. Explanation: Rolling wave planning is a form of progressive elaboration where near-term work is decomposed into detailed activities while future work remains at a higher level until more information is known. This keeps the schedule realistic without forcing false precision on work that is still too uncertain to plan in detail. Why the other options are incorrect: Resource availability affects scheduling decisions but is not the reason near-term work is detailed while future work is not. Rolling wave planning is a deliberate scheduling approach, not a workaround for missing resources. The project management plan approval status does not prevent rolling wave planning. Nearterm detailed scheduling can and should begin as soon as enough information is available. Detailed scheduling is not universally deferred to execution. Near-term work should already be planned in detail during the planning phase. Only future work that lacks sufficient detail is left at a high level for now.","id":"book-schedule-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"An agile team is preparing for sprint planning and needs to estimate the effort required for a set of new user stories. The team has limited historical data and the stories vary significantly in complexity. The project manager suggests each team member independently select a card representing their effort estimate, reveal all cards simultaneously, and discuss any significant differences until reaching consensus. What estimation technique is being used?","opts":{"A":"Planning poker","B":"T-shirt sizing","C":"Parametric estimating","D":"Bottom-up estimating"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Planning poker Explanation: Planning poker is a collaborative agile estimation technique where team members use cards to independently assign relative estimates to backlog items, reveal them simultaneously, and discuss differences until reaching consensus. Its purpose is not to assign exact durations but to estimate relative effort and complexity, which can then be used with team velocity to support sprint and release planning. Why the other options are incorrect: T-shirt sizing uses size labels such as small, medium, and large to provide quick high-level estimates. It does not involve cards, simultaneous reveal, or the consensus-building discussion that characterizes planning poker. Parametric estimating uses historical data and mathematical relationships to calculate durations. It is a traditional estimating technique, not an agile collaborative method, and requires quantifiable historical data that the team does not have here. Bottom-up estimating breaks work into detailed components and aggregates estimates upward. It requires detailed scope decomposition and produces time-based estimates, not the relative effort estimates that agile teams use for backlog items.","id":"book-schedule-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":11,"q":"An agile team is managing a Kanban board with 60 story points currently in progress. The team completes an average of 15 story points per week. What is the cycle time for this team?","opts":{"A":"6 weeks","B":"45 weeks","C":"900 weeks","D":"4 weeks"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: 4 weeks Explanation: Cycle time is calculated by dividing work in progress by throughput. In this case, 60 points divided by 15 points per week equals 4 weeks. This tells the team how long it takes on average for a unit of work to move through the active workflow from start to completion. Why the other options are incorrect: 6 weeks would result from a calculation error. The correct calculation is 60 / 15 = 4, not 6. 45 weeks would result from subtracting throughput from WIP rather than dividing. 60 - 15 = 45 is not the cycle time formula. 900 weeks would result from multiplying WIP by throughput rather than dividing. 60 x 15 = 900 is the inverse of the correct operation.","id":"book-schedule-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is presenting a schedule update to the executive steering committee. She shows a one-page summary that displays only the major deliverables and key decision points for the project, each represented as a single point in time with no bars or durations shown. Which scheduling tool is being used?","opts":{"A":"Gantt chart","B":"Network diagram","C":"Milestone chart","D":"Activity list"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Milestone chart Explanation: A milestone chart displays only the significant events, deliverables, and decision points on a project. Milestones have zero duration and are represented as points in time rather than bars or ranges. They are commonly used when presenting to senior management because they provide a high-level summary without the detail of a full project schedule. Why the other options are incorrect: A Gantt chart displays all project activities as horizontal bars showing their start dates, end dates, and durations. The tool described shows no bars or durations. A network diagram shows the logical sequencing and dependencies between activities. It is used for scheduling analysis, not for presenting high-level project status to executives. An activity list is a text-based document that describes all project activities and their attributes. It is not a visual scheduling tool and does not display points in time.","id":"book-schedule-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A construction project cannot begin foundation work until the local government issues a building permit. The permit application has been submitted and the team is waiting for approval. Which type of dependency does this describe?","opts":{"A":"Discretionary internal","B":"Mandatory internal","C":"Discretionary external","D":"Mandatory external"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Mandatory external Explanation: This dependency is mandatory because foundation work legally cannot begin without the permit. It is external because the approval process is controlled by a government authority outside the project team’s control. Why the other options are incorrect: A discretionary internal dependency is based on preference or best practice and is within the team’s control. This dependency is neither discretionary nor internal. A mandatory internal dependency is non-optional but is within the project team’s control. The permit approval is outside the team’s control, making it external rather than internal. A discretionary external dependency originates outside the project but is based on preference rather than a hard requirement. Waiting for a government permit is not a matter of preference. Work cannot legally proceed without it.","id":"book-schedule-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"The product owner on an agile project would like to know how much work is remaining to be done on the project. What should the agile project manager use to provide this information?","opts":{"A":"Burndown chart","B":"Burnup chart","C":"Velocity chart","D":"Kanban"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: A burndown chart shows the amount of work remaining to be completed. Why the other options are incorrect: A burnup chart shows the amount of work that has been completed over time. A velocity chart shows the amount of work the team completes in each iteration, which helps estimate how fast the team is progressing. A Kanban board is used to visualize the workflow and show where work items are in the process, such as To Do, In Progress, and Done.","id":"book-schedule-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":15,"q":"Based on the burnup chart, which of the following best describes what happened during this project?","opts":{"A":"Scope was reduced midway through the project, which contributed to the team completing all work ahead of schedule.","B":"The team’s velocity increased significantly after sprint 5, allowing them to finish two sprints early.","C":"Scope was added midway through the project, but the team absorbed it and finished on schedule.","D":"The project was completed on schedule with no changes to the original scope."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Scope was reduced midway through the project, which contributed to the team completing all work ahead of schedule. Explanation: In a burnup chart, the total scope line shows how much work exists at any point in time. A downward step in the total scope line indicates that scope was removed from the project. At sprint 5, the total scope drops from 150 to 120 points. The completed line continues rising and meets the total scope line at sprint 8, two sprints before the planned finish, confirming the project completed early. The scope reduction contributed to this early completion. Why the other options are incorrect: The completed line does not show a sharp acceleration after sprint 5. Progress continues at a similar pace. It is the reduction in total scope, not an increase in velocity, that explains why the lines meet before sprint 10. A scope increase would appear as an upward step in the total scope line. The chart shows a downward step, indicating scope was removed, not added. The total scope line clearly changes at sprint 5, and the project finishes at sprint 8 rather than sprint 10. Neither the scope nor the schedule remained unchanged.","id":"book-schedule-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Schedule","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":1,"q":"A project manager has just completed the project charter and received formal authorization to begin the project. What should the project manager do next?","opts":{"A":"Begin estimating the cost of each project activity.","B":"Develop the project budget and establish the cost baseline.","C":"Compare actual spending against the cost baseline and take corrective action where needed.","D":"Establish how the project’s costs will be planned, estimated, tracked, and controlled."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Establish how the project’s costs will be planned, estimated, tracked, and controlled. Explanation: Before any estimating or budgeting can begin, the project manager should establish the financial management approach for the project. Defining how costs will be measured, what level of accuracy is required, and how variances will be handled describes the “Plan Cost/Financial Management” step which should be done before other cost planning steps. Why the other options are incorrect: Estimating individual activity costs is part of the Estimate Costs process, which comes after the financial management approach has been established. Developing the budget and cost baseline comes after cost estimates have been completed. Both estimating and planning must precede budgeting. Comparing actual spending against the cost baseline is part of controlling costs, which occurs during project execution, not at the start of a project.","id":"book-finance-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager is planning a large construction project. The organization has approved the full project budget but has informed the project manager that only a portion of the funds will be available in the current fiscal year, with the remainder released in the following year. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Reduce the project scope to fit within the funds available in the current fiscal year.","B":"Accelerate the schedule to complete as much work as possible before the funding limit is reached.","C":"Reschedule project activities to align with when funding will be available.","D":"Request a budget exception from senior management to access the full funding immediately."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Reschedule project activities to align with when funding will be available. Explanation: When funds are released in phases, the project manager should adjust when work is performed to match the availability of funds rather than cutting scope or seeking exceptions. This ensures the project remains financially viable without compromising its objectives. Why the other options are incorrect: Reducing scope assumes the funding constraint is permanent, but the full budget has already been approved. The constraint is about timing, not total funding available. Accelerating work to consume available funds before the limit is reached does not address the underlying constraint and may create gaps in work or resource availability later in the project. Requesting an exception to access all funds immediately is not the standard response to a phased funding arrangement. Funding limit reconciliation through rescheduling is the appropriate approach.","id":"book-finance-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A project manager has been asked to provide a cost estimate for a proposed project by end of day so that senior leadership can make a go/no-go decision. The project is similar to one completed two years ago. Detailed scope documentation is not yet available. What estimating technique should the project manager use?","opts":{"A":"Bottom-up estimating, because accuracy is important for a go/no-go decision.","B":"Analogous estimating, because a similar project exists and a quick estimate is needed.","C":"Parametric estimating, because it uses historical data and can produce accurate results.","D":"Expert judgment, because no documentation is available to support other techniques."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Analogous estimating, because a similar project exists and a quick estimate is needed. Explanation: Analogous estimating uses data from previous similar projects to produce a quick high-level estimate. When detailed scope is unavailable and a fast turnaround is required, analogous estimating is the most appropriate technique. It is not the most accurate method, but it is wellsuited for early-stage decisions when speed matters more than precision. Why the other options are incorrect: Bottom-up estimating is the most accurate technique but requires detailed scope documentation and significant time to complete. Neither condition is met in this scenario. Parametric estimating uses mathematical relationships and historical data to produce estimates, but it requires defined units of work to calculate against. Without detailed scope, there is insufficient data to apply this technique reliably. Expert judgment is a valid input to estimating but is not a standalone technique in this context. It is most useful when combined with other methods, and the availability of a similar past project makes analogous estimating the stronger choice.","id":"book-finance-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager has been asked to estimate the cost of a first-of-its-kind artificial intelligence implementation project. The organization has never undertaken anything similar, and there are no comparable industry benchmarks available. Leadership needs a reliable estimate before committing to the project. What estimating technique should the project manager use?","opts":{"A":"Analogous estimating, because it is the fastest way to produce an early-stage estimate.","B":"Parametric estimating, because it uses mathematical relationships to produce reliable results.","C":"Expert judgment, because the project is novel and no comparable historical data exists.","D":"Bottom-up estimating, because it produces the most accurate results."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Expert judgment, because the project is novel and no comparable historical data exists. Explanation: When a project is truly novel and no comparable data exists, analogous and parametric estimating cannot be reliably applied. Expert judgment draws on the knowledge and experience of individuals familiar with similar technologies or domains, making it the most appropriate technique when historical data is unavailable and accuracy matters. Why the other options are incorrect: Analogous estimating relies on data from previous similar projects. With no comparable projects available, there is no valid basis for this technique and applying it would produce an unreliable estimate. Parametric estimating requires defined units of work and historical cost-per-unit data to calculate estimates. Without comparable benchmarks, this technique cannot be applied reliably. Bottom-up estimating requires detailed scope documentation to break work down into estimable components. In an early-stage novel project with limited data, the level of detail needed for bottom-up estimating does not yet exist.","id":"book-finance-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project manager has completed high-level cost estimates and the project scope statement has just been finalized and approved. What should the project manager do next?","opts":{"A":"Break the work down into work packages and develop more detailed cost estimates.","B":"Refine the high-level estimates using analogous data from similar projects.","C":"Submit the high-level estimates to the sponsor for budget approval.","D":"Begin developing the project schedule to determine activity durations before refining costs."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Break the work down into work packages and develop more detailed cost estimates. Explanation: Once the scope statement is finalized, the work is defined clearly enough to develop a WBS and break it down to the work package level. This enables more accurate cost estimation than the high-level estimates produced earlier. Work package estimation allows the team to analyze specific tasks, apply scenario analysis, and produce estimates that are detailed enough to support reliable budgeting. Why the other options are incorrect: Analogous estimating is a high-level technique. You already did high-level estimates; now that scope is firm, you should move toward more detailed, bottom-up style estimating, not just refine at the same level. Submitting high-level estimates for budget approval before detailed work package estimates are available would produce an unreliable cost baseline. The budget should be built from detailed estimates, not early approximations. Schedule development and cost estimating are both planning activities and will iterate together, but in this situation high-level costs are already done and the scope is now finalized. The wording “before refining costs” means this option delays the natural next step implied by the scenario, which is to refine and detail the cost estimates based on the now-approved scope.","id":"book-finance-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"A project is six months into execution when a key assumption made during cost estimating turns out to be incorrect, causing several activity costs to be significantly higher than planned. The project manager attempts to revise the affected estimates but cannot determine what assumptions were made or what method was used to develop the original numbers. What does this scenario most likely indicate?","opts":{"A":"The project manager should have used a more accurate estimating technique during planning.","B":"The risk register was not updated to reflect the assumptions made during estimating.","C":"The basis of estimates was not documented when the cost estimates were developed.","D":"The cost baseline should have included higher contingency reserves to cover incorrect assumptions."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The basis of estimates was not documented when the cost estimates were developed. Explanation: The basis of estimates documents how each estimate was developed, including the methods used, assumptions made, constraints applied, and acceptable ranges. Without this documentation, the project manager has no way to trace back the original logic when estimates need to be revised or when assumptions prove incorrect. Documenting the basis of estimates is what makes cost estimates reviewable, revisable, and defensible. Why the other options are incorrect: The issue is not the accuracy of the estimating technique but the lack of documentation supporting the estimates. Even the most accurate technique produces estimates that are difficult to revise without a record of how they were developed. While assumptions should be tracked in the risk register, the basis of estimates is the specific document that records how estimates were derived. The problem described is a documentation gap, not a risk management failure. Contingency reserves account for known risks, not for incorrectly documented assumptions. Adding more reserves would not have made the estimates easier to trace or revise.","id":"book-finance-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager has just completed detailed cost estimates for all project activities and now needs to determine the total project cost. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Aggregate activity costs into work packages, then roll work package costs up into deliverables to arrive at the total project cost.","B":"Add all individual activity costs together to determine the total project cost.","C":"Average the activity costs across work packages to produce a high-level budget estimate.","D":"Submit the individual activity cost estimates to the sponsor for approval before aggregating them."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Aggregate activity costs into work packages, then roll work package costs up into deliverables to arrive at the total project cost. Explanation: Cost aggregation follows the structure of the WBS. Activity costs are first rolled up into their respective work packages, and work package costs are then aggregated into deliverables. This hierarchical approach ensures that costs are organized consistently with the project’s scope structure and provides clear visibility into where money is being spent at each level. Why the other options are incorrect: Simply adding all activity costs together bypasses the WBS structure and produces a total without the organized breakdown needed to track and control costs at different levels of the project. Averaging activity costs would distort the actual cost picture and is not a recognized approach to cost aggregation. Each activity’s actual estimated cost must be captured, not averaged. Individual activity cost estimates are inputs to the budgeting process, not items requiring standalone sponsor approval. The aggregated cost baseline is what gets formally approved.","id":"book-finance-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project is midway through execution. The project manager wants to determine whether the project is performing within its financial targets. What should the project manager compare actual spending against?","opts":{"A":"The original cost estimates developed during planning.","B":"The approved cost baseline, which includes all approved changes.","C":"The sponsor’s expected budget as communicated at project initiation.","D":"The most recent cost estimates produced by the project team."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The approved cost baseline, which includes all approved changes. Explanation: The cost baseline is the approved, time-phased budget used to measure project financial performance. It represents the original plan plus all approved changes. Comparing actual spending against the cost baseline gives an accurate and current picture of whether the project is performing as expected. Why the other options are incorrect: The original cost estimates were the starting point but may not reflect approved changes made since planning. Using them as the comparison point could produce misleading variance data. The sponsor’s expected budget is not a formal project document and may not reflect approved changes or the time-phased structure needed for accurate performance measurement. The most recent team estimates may reflect current thinking but are not necessarily approved. Only the formally approved cost baseline is the valid reference point for performance measurement.","id":"book-finance-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is explaining the difference between the cost baseline and the total project budget to a new team member. The project has an approved cost baseline of $500,000 and management has set aside an additional $50,000 in management reserves. What is the total project budget?","opts":{"A":"$500,000, because the cost baseline is the total approved budget for the project.","B":"$450,000, because management reserves are not included in the total project budget.","C":"$500,000, because management reserves are tracked separately and are not included in the total project budget.","D":"$550,000, because the total project budget includes the cost baseline plus management reserves."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: $550,000, because the total project budget includes the cost baseline plus management reserves. Explanation: Total project budget = cost baseline + management reserve. The total project budget adds management reserves on top of the cost baseline. Management reserves are held by the organization for unknown unknowns and require a formal change request to access, but they are still part of the total funding allocated to the project. Why the other options are incorrect: The cost baseline alone does not represent the total project budget. It excludes management reserves, which are part of the overall funding but outside the PM’s direct control. Management reserves are added to the cost baseline, not subtracted from it. Subtracting them would understate the total funding available. Management reserves are tracked separately but are still included in the total project budget. The total budget is always the cost baseline plus management reserves.","id":"book-finance-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project manager is executing a software development project when an identified risk materializes, requiring additional testing resources that were not originally planned for. The project manager wants to address the cost impact without submitting a formal change request. What type of reserve should the project manager draw from?","opts":{"A":"Management reserves, because they are set aside specifically for unexpected cost increases.","B":"Contingency reserves, because they are designated for known risks and are within the project manager’s control.","C":"Management reserves, because the cost impact was not included in the original budget.","D":"The project budget, because all cost increases must be funded from the approved budget."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Contingency reserves, because they are designated for known risks and are within the project manager’s control. Explanation: Contingency reserves are set aside to cover known unknowns, i.e., identified risks. Because this risk was previously identified, the contingency reserve is the appropriate funding source. Contingency reserves are included in the cost baseline and are within the project manager’s authority to use without requiring a formal change request. Why the other options are incorrect: Management reserves are for unknown unknowns, risks that were not identified during planning. Since this risk was previously identified, management reserves are not the appropriate source. Additionally, accessing management reserves always requires a formal change request. Management reserves are not appropriate for identified risks regardless of whether the cost impact was in the original budget. All cost increases do not automatically require drawing from the project budget at large. Reserves exist specifically to handle risk-related cost impacts without disrupting the overall budget structure.","id":"book-finance-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager is estimating costs for a complex infrastructure project. Several activities involve work that have never been done before, and the team has identified a number of risks that could increase costs if they occur. The project manager wants to ensure the estimates account for these potential cost impacts. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Increase all activity cost estimates by a fixed percentage to account for the risks","B":"Add a single lump sum to the total project budget to cover all identified risks.","C":"Submit a change request to access management reserves before the project begins.","D":"Add contingency reserves to the affected activity estimates based on the identified risks and their potential cost impact."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Add contingency reserves to the affected activity estimates based on the identified risks and their potential cost impact. Explanation: Reserve analysis involves adding contingency reserves to cost estimates to account for identified risks. These reserves can be a percentage of the activity cost or a fixed amount depending on the nature of the risk. By tying reserves to specific risks identified in the risk register, the project manager ensures that estimates reflect realistic potential costs while keeping reserves purposeful and traceable. Why the other options are incorrect: Applying a blanket percentage increase to all activities does not account for the specific nature of each risk. Reserve analysis should be targeted to activities and risks rather than applied uniformly across the board. Adding a lump sum to the total budget without tying it to specific risks is not reserve analysis. Contingency reserves should be traceable to identified risks, not pooled as a general buffer. Management reserves are for unknown unknowns and are controlled by the organization, not the project manager. They are not accessed before the project begins and cannot substitute for properly planned contingency reserves.","id":"book-finance-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is looking for ways to reduce costs on a software development project that is running slightly over budget. A team member suggests skipping a planned code review process to save time and money, arguing that defects can be fixed after testing if any are found. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Skip the code reviews since they are a discretionary quality activity and the budget needs to be reduced.","B":"Skip the code reviews but document the decision as a known risk in the risk register.","C":"Keep the code reviews in place, recognizing that the cost of fixing defects later is likely to exceed the cost of prevention now.","D":"Submit a change request to remove the code reviews from the project plan to formally document the cost reduction decision."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Keep the code reviews in place, recognizing that the cost of fixing defects later is likely to exceed the cost of prevention now. Explanation: The cost of quality includes both prevention and appraisal activities as well as the cost of non-conformance such as rework and defect correction. Skipping prevention activities to reduce short-term costs typically results in higher total costs when defects surface later. Why the other options are incorrect: Code reviews are a planned quality activity, not a discretionary one. Skipping them to reduce costs ignores the downstream cost impact of defects that go undetected until later in the project. Logging the decision as a risk does not eliminate the cost impact of skipping the reviews. It simply acknowledges the risk without addressing it. Prevention is more cost-effective than risk acknowledgment alone. Going through proper change control does not make the underlying decision sound. Formally removing a quality activity still exposes the project to higher defect-related costs later, regardless of how the removal is processed.","id":"book-finance-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager reviews the latest performance report and finds the following EVM values for an ongoing project: CPI = 0.85 SPI = 1.10 How should the project manager interpret these results?","opts":{"A":"The project is under budget and behind schedule.","B":"The project is over budget and ahead of schedule.","C":"The project is under budget and ahead of schedule.","D":"The project is over budget and behind schedule."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: The project is over budget and ahead of schedule. Explanation: A CPI below 1.0 means the project is spending more than the value it is generating, indicating it is over budget. An SPI above 1.0 means the project is completing more work than planned for this point in time, indicating it is ahead of schedule. In this case, the team is moving faster than planned but at a higher cost than expected. Why the other options are incorrect: A CPI of 0.85 indicates over budget, not under budget. A value below 1.0 means the project is generating less value per dollar spent than planned. While the SPI of 1.10 correctly indicates ahead of schedule, the CPI of 0.85 indicates over budget, not under budget. A CPI of 0.85 indicates over budget. An SPI of 1.10 indicates ahead of schedule, not behind schedule.","id":"book-finance-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"A project manager is reviewing the performance of a construction project. The project has a budget of $200,000. To date, the team has completed 40% of the work and has spent $90,000. What is the Cost Variance (CV) for this project, and what does it indicate?","opts":{"A":"CV = -$10,000; the project is over budget.","B":"CV = $10,000; the project is under budget.","C":"CV = -$10,000; the project is under budget.","D":"CV = $10,000; the project is over budget."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: CV = -$10,000; the project is over budget. Explanation: EV = 40% x $200,000 = $80,000. AC = $90,000. CV = EV - AC = $80,000 - $90,000 = -$10,000. A negative CV means the project has spent more money than the value of work completed, indicating it is over budget by $10,000. Why the other options are incorrect: A CV of $10,000 would indicate the project is under budget, meaning less was spent than the value of work completed. The calculation here produces a negative result, not a positive one. A negative CV always indicates over budget, not under budget. The sign convention for CV is that negative means over budget and positive means under budget. A positive CV of $10,000 would indicate under budget, but the correct calculation produces -$10,000, and a negative CV indicates over budget, not under.","id":"book-finance-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A project manager reviews the latest performance data for an ongoing project and finds the following values: CPI = 0.75 TCPI = 1.35 What do these values indicate about the project?","opts":{"A":"The project is performing efficiently and is on track to finish within budget.","B":"The project is currently under budget and can maintain its current efficiency to finish on time.","C":"The project is currently over budget and would need to perform significantly more efficiently than it has been to finish within the original budget.","D":"The project is slightly over budget but the TCPI indicates it can recover with minimal additional effort."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The project is currently over budget and would need to perform significantly more efficiently than it has been to finish within the original budget. Explanation: A CPI of 0.75 means the project is currently generating only $0.75 of value for every dollar spent, indicating it is 25% over budget. A TCPI of 1.35 means the team would need to perform at 135% efficiency on all remaining work to finish within the original budget. Why the other options are incorrect: A CPI of 0.75 indicates the project is over budget, not performing efficiently. A value below 1.0 means more is being spent than the value being generated. A CPI below 1.0 indicates over budget, not under budget. The project is spending more than planned, not less. The gap between a CPI of 0.75 and a TCPI of 1.35 represents a 60 percentage point difference in required versus actual efficiency. This is not a minor gap that can be closed with minimal effort; it signals a serious financial challenge.","id":"book-finance-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Finance","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A company is migrating its entire enterprise infrastructure to a new cloud platform. The project manager reviews the stakeholder engagement matrix above. Stakeholder Unaware Resistant Neutral Supportive Leading Project Sponsor C D End Users C D IT Director C D Vendor C D Compliance Team C D C = Current engagement level D = Desired engagement level Which stakeholder should the project manager prioritize engaging first?","opts":{"A":"End users, because they have a two-level engagement gap and resistance can slow adoption.","B":"IT Director, because a two-level gap in a stakeholder who controls the technical infrastructure poses the greatest risk to the project.","C":"Project sponsor, because sponsor engagement is always the highest priority.","D":"Compliance team, because regulatory alignment is critical on infrastructure projects."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: IT Director, because a two-level gap in a stakeholder who controls the technical infrastructure poses the greatest risk to the project. Explanation: Both the IT Director and end users have a two-level engagement gap, but the IT Director controls the infrastructure the project depends on. A stakeholder who is neutral when they need to be leading can block decisions, withhold resources, or create obstacles that derail the project. The engagement matrix surfaces this gap visually so the project manager can prioritize where engagement efforts are most critical. Why the other options are incorrect: End users also have a two-level gap and resistance should be addressed, but their ability to block or derail the project is more limited than the IT Director’s. Both gaps matter, but the IT Director’s gap carries greater project risk. The sponsor is already at their desired engagement level. No gap exists and no immediate action is required. The compliance team is also already at their desired level. Regulatory alignment is important but does not require additional engagement effort at this time.","id":"book-stakeholder-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"At the end of a sprint, the development team invites the client and key business stakeholders to view the features that were completed and share their feedback before the next sprint begins. Which agile meeting is this?","opts":{"A":"Sprint review","B":"Sprint retrospective","C":"Daily standup","D":"Sprint planning"},"ans":"A","exp":"A: Sprint review Explanation: The sprint review is the only agile ceremony that includes customers and external stakeholders. Its purpose is to demonstrate completed work and gather feedback that will inform future sprints. It is focused on the product and what was delivered, not on the team’s internal process. Why the other options are incorrect: The sprint retrospective also occurs at the end of the sprint but is an internal team meeting focused on improving how the team works. Customers are not included. The daily standup is a 15-minute daily check-in among team members covering progress and impediments. It does not involve customers or deliverable review. Sprint planning occurs before the sprint begins and is where the team decides what work to pull from the product backlog. It does not involve demonstrating completed work to stakeholders.","id":"book-stakeholder-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":3,"q":"A project manager is working with a senior executive who has been disengaged from the project and whose support will be critical during the upcoming approval phase. The project manager documents a strategy to rebuild the relationship through one-on-one meetings and by involving the executive in key decisions before they are finalized. Which project document does this belong in?","opts":{"A":"Communications management plan","B":"Stakeholder register","C":"Stakeholder engagement plan","D":"Project charter"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Stakeholder engagement plan Explanation: The stakeholder engagement plan describes how and why stakeholders will be engaged based on their needs, expectations, and level of influence. Strategies for building relationships, increasing support, and managing resistance belong here. The communications management plan, by contrast, defines the mechanics of information sharing — what, when, how, and to whom — not the relationship strategy behind it. Why the other options are incorrect: The communications management plan defines how information will be distributed, such as weekly status reports or monthly presentations. It does not capture relationship strategies or engagement approaches tailored to individual stakeholders. The stakeholder register lists stakeholders, their roles, contact information, and influence levels. It is a reference document, not a strategy document. The project charter formally authorizes the project and identifies high-level stakeholders. It does not contain engagement strategies for individual stakeholders.","id":"book-stakeholder-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager needs to notify all project stakeholders that the project completion date has been moved back by three weeks due to a vendor delay. The change has been formally approved and the project manager wants to ensure the information is documented and traceable. What type of communication is most appropriate?","opts":{"A":"Informal verbal","B":"Informal written","C":"Formal verbal","D":"Formal written"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Formal written Explanation: A schedule change that has been formally approved and needs to be documented and traceable calls for formal written communication. This type of communication is structured, official, and creates a record that can be referenced later. Examples include project reports, status updates, and official notifications. The combination of formality and documentation makes it the appropriate choice here. Why the other options are incorrect: Informal verbal communication such as a phone call or hallway conversation is unscheduled and leaves no traceable record. It is not appropriate for communicating a formally approved project change to all stakeholders. Informal written communication such as an email or text message is faster but less structured. While it creates some record, it lacks the formality appropriate for an official project change notification. Formal verbal communication such as a presentation is structured but does not produce a written record on its own. For a change that needs to be documented and traceable, written communication is more appropriate.","id":"book-stakeholder-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project manager maintains a project portal where all status reports, meeting minutes, and technical documents are stored. Stakeholders have been directed to check the portal regularly for project updates rather than receiving individual notifications. Which communication method is being used?","opts":{"A":"Interactive","B":"Push","C":"Pull","D":"Formal verbal"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Pull Explanation: Pull communication involves placing information in a central location where recipients access it on their own schedule. It is best suited for large audiences, non-urgent information, and situations where stakeholders have varying schedules. A project portal where stakeholders retrieve updates themselves is a classic example of pull communication. Why the other options are incorrect: Interactive communication involves real-time two-way exchange such as meetings or instant messaging. No two-way interaction is described here. Push communication involves sending information directly to recipients such as through email or memos. In this scenario, the project manager is not distributing information directly. Stakeholders are retrieving it themselves. Formal verbal is a communication type describing the format of a communication such as a presentation. It is not a distribution method.","id":"book-stakeholder-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager has been managing a remote team through email and instant messaging. During a status update, a key developer confirms via chat that her tasks are on track and she has no concerns. Two weeks later, she reveals she has been struggling with a critical technical blocker for most of the sprint. What does this situation most likely illustrate?","opts":{"A":"The developer should have followed the team’s communication protocols and reported the issue sooner.","B":"Written communication lacks the nonverbal cues that often reveal when someone is uncertain, uncomfortable, or withholding information.","C":"The project manager should have scheduled more frequent status meetings to catch issues earlier.","D":"The developer’s impediment should have been raised during the daily standup."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Written communication lacks nonverbal cues Explanation: Nonverbal communication accounts for a significant portion of how people express themselves, including facial expressions, tone, and body language. These cues are lost in text-based communication. A face-to-face conversation may have revealed the developer’s concern much earlier. Why the other options are incorrect: The issue is not the developer’s compliance with protocols. The communication environment made it harder to surface a sensitive concern. Blaming the developer misses the root cause. More frequent meetings would help but do not address the underlying issue if those meetings are also conducted through text without attention to nonverbal signals. The format of communication matters as much as the frequency. The daily standup is a valid forum for raising impediments, but the scenario does not establish that the team was using daily standups or even operating within a clearly agile framework. The strongest evidence in the scenario points to the limitations of the communication channel, not to failure to use a specific ceremony.","id":"book-stakeholder-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is reviewing an upcoming deliverable when the team realizes the output will process personally identifiable information. The project was initially scoped around anonymized data only. What should the project manager do first?","opts":{"A":"Submit a change request to revise the scope and update the project schedule.","B":"Identify any stakeholders who should be involved given the updated nature of the deliverable and add them to the stakeholder register.","C":"Inform the sponsor since the project scope has changed in a way that may affect the organization.","D":"Document the change in the risk register and assess its potential impact on the project."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Identify any stakeholders who should be involved given the updated nature of the deliverable and add them to the stakeholder register. Explanation: When new information changes the nature of a deliverable, the project manager should reassess who is affected and update the stakeholder register accordingly. Stakeholder identification is a continuous process, and changes in scope or deliverable characteristics can reveal previously unidentified stakeholders. Getting the right people involved is the necessary first step before any other action can be taken effectively. Why the other options are incorrect: A change request may be appropriate once the full impact is understood, but it is premature before the project manager has identified who needs to be involved and assessed what the change means for the project. Informing the sponsor is appropriate but should follow the project manager’s own assessment of who is affected. Escalation before taking any action avoids the PM’s direct responsibility. Documenting the change as a risk is a valid parallel step but does not address the more immediate need to identify and engage the right stakeholders for this type of deliverable.","id":"book-stakeholder-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project manager is implementing a new inventory system. The marketing department has raised concerns that the system’s reporting format will not meet their needs. Which direction of influence does the marketing department represent?","opts":{"A":"Upward","B":"Downward","C":"Outward","D":"Sideward"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Sideward Explanation: The marketing department is a peer department within the same organization. Their relationship to the project is lateral — they are neither above nor below the project in authority, nor are they external to the organization. Why the other options are incorrect: Upward influence comes from senior management, sponsors, or steering bodies who hold authority above the project. Downward influence comes from team members or people working within the project structure. Outward influence comes from parties outside the organization entirely, such as customers, vendors, or regulators.","id":"book-stakeholder-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is leading a large hospital system modernization involving government regulators, insurance providers, clinical staff, hospital executives, patient advocacy groups, and external technology vendors. Several stakeholders have significant influence over the project and the project manager is struggling to determine who to prioritize and how to allocate engagement efforts. Which stakeholder classification model is most appropriate for this situation?","opts":{"A":"Power/interest grid","B":"Direction of influence","C":"Stakeholder cube","D":"Salience model"},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Salience model Explanation: This scenario emphasizes a large, complex stakeholder community with several groups holding different kinds of influence. The salience model is most appropriate because it helps prioritize stakeholders based on power, urgency, and legitimacy, which is especially useful when many stakeholders appear important at the same time. Why the other options are incorrect: Power/interest grid is useful, but it is simpler and less effective when many influential stakeholders compete for attention in a highly complex environment. Direction of influence classifies stakeholders by where they sit relative to the project, not by priority. Stakeholder cube is fairly detailed, but the scenario describes many important stakeholders whose relative priority is hard to distinguish, and salience gives a better mechanism for that distinction.","id":"book-stakeholder-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project manager is leading a large software implementation. During planning, a government regulatory body was heavily involved in reviewing and approving the system architecture. All approvals have since been granted and the project has moved onto the development phase. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Continue following the current stakeholder engagement plan since it was approved during planning.","B":"Remove the regulatory body from the stakeholder register since they are no longer involved in the project.","C":"Update the stakeholder register and adjust the engagement strategy to reflect the regulator’s reduced involvement at this stage.","D":"Flag the reduced involvement as a risk and add it to the risk register for monitoring."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Update the stakeholder register and adjust the engagement strategy to reflect the regulator’s reduced involvement at this stage. Explanation: Stakeholder identification and engagement is a continuous process. As the project progresses, stakeholders’ levels of involvement and relevance naturally shift. The project manager should keep the stakeholder register current and adjust engagement strategies accordingly. Why the other options are incorrect: The stakeholder engagement plan is a living document that should be updated as stakeholder circumstances change. An approved engagement plan is not meant to stay unchanged when stakeholder involvement changes. Removing a stakeholder from the register because they are temporarily less involved is incorrect. The regulator will be relevant again at deployment and should remain in the register with an updated engagement status. A stakeholder temporarily stepping back at an appropriate phase of the project is expected and not inherently a risk. Adding it to the risk register without basis treats a normal project dynamic as a threat.","id":"book-stakeholder-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"An agile team needs to resolve a complex technical disagreement between two developers and a product owner. All three are working in the same office building. The project manager is considering the best way to facilitate the discussion. What communication approach should the project manager recommend?","opts":{"A":"Set up a video call so the discussion can be recorded for future reference.","B":"Create a shared document where each party can outline their position asynchronously.","C":"Send an email thread so everyone has a written record of the discussion.","D":"Bring all three parties together in person for a face-to-face discussion."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Bring all three parties together in person for a face-to-face discussion. Explanation: The presence of a product owner signals this is an agile project, and Agile favors the lowest-tech, highest-touch communication option. Even on a non-Agile project, for a complex disagreement among people in the same office building, face-to-face discussion is generally the most effective approach because it enables immediate feedback, clarification, and nonverbal cues. Why the other options are incorrect: A video call is a reasonable alternative when parties cannot meet in person, but it is a lower-touch option than a face-to-face meeting and loses some of the nonverbal richness of in-person interaction. An asynchronous shared document removes the real-time interaction that is essential for resolving complex disagreements. It is the least effective option for a nuanced discussion that benefits from immediate back-and-forth. An email thread has the same limitations as the shared document and adds the risk of misinterpretation. Written communication lacks the tone, body language, and immediate feedback that make face-to-face discussion so effective.","id":"book-stakeholder-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":12,"q":"During a project status meeting, the project manager is presenting a critical update about a schedule change. All team members have put away their phones, closed their laptops, and are focused entirely on what the project manager is saying. Which communication behavior is this?","opts":{"A":"Active listening","B":"Receptive listening","C":"Effective listening","D":"Paralingual communication"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Effective listening Explanation: Effective listening occurs when the sender has the full and undivided attention of the receiver. The team members are not asking questions or interacting — they are simply giving the project manager their complete focus, which is the defining characteristic of effective listening. Why the other options are incorrect: Active listening involves the listener engaging through questions and seeking clarification. The team members here are attentive but not actively participating in the exchange. Receptive listening is not recognized terminology in project management. The appropriate phrase is effective listening. Paralingual communication refers to how something is said, such as tone, pitch, and vocal patterns. It describes the quality of speech, not the behavior of the listener.","id":"book-stakeholder-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager is planning communications for a project with 8 stakeholders. How many communication channels exist on this project?","opts":{"A":"16","B":"28","C":"56","D":"64"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: 28 Explanation: The formula for calculating communication channels is N (N-1) / 2, where N is the number of stakeholders. Applying the formula: 8 (8-1) / 2 = 8 x 7 / 2 = 56 / 2 = 28 channels. Why the other options are incorrect: 16 would result from simply multiplying the number of stakeholders by 2, which is not the correct formula. 56 is the result of calculating N (N-1) without dividing by 2. The division by 2 is necessary because each channel connects two stakeholders and would otherwise be counted twice. 64 would result from squaring the number of stakeholders (8²), which is not the correct formula.","id":"book-stakeholder-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"A project manager reviews recent meeting minutes, email logs, and status reports and discovers that two key stakeholders have not been receiving the weekly progress updates they were supposed to get according to the communications management plan. Which process is the project manager performing?","opts":{"A":"Monitor Stakeholder Engagement","B":"Manage Communications","C":"Monitor Communications","D":"Plan Communications Management"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Monitor Communications Explanation: Monitor Communications is the process of ensuring the right information is reaching the right stakeholders at the right time according to the communications management plan. Discovering that two stakeholders have not been receiving planned updates is exactly the kind of gap this process is designed to identify. Why the other options are incorrect: Monitor Stakeholder Engagement focuses on evaluating whether stakeholders are satisfied, supportive, or resistant and adjusting engagement strategies accordingly. It is concerned with the quality and effectiveness of stakeholder involvement, not whether specific information deliverables are being distributed as planned. Manage Communications is the process of creating and distributing project information. It involves sending the communications, not checking whether they were received as planned. Plan Communications Management is a planning process that defines how communication will be conducted. It is not a monitoring activity.","id":"book-stakeholder-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A project manager sends a detailed status report to all stakeholders at the end of each week. The reports are clear, well-structured, and consistently delivered on time. During a steering committee meeting, several stakeholders reveal they had misunderstood a key decision communicated three weeks earlier and had been operating under incorrect assumptions ever since. What most likely caused this situation?","opts":{"A":"The stakeholders should have asked clarifying questions when they received the report.","B":"The status reports were too detailed and the key decision was buried in the content.","C":"The project manager did not confirm that stakeholders had understood the information after it was communicated.","D":"The steering committee meeting should have been held sooner to catch the misunderstanding earlier."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: The project manager did not confirm that stakeholders had understood the information after it was communicated. Explanation: It is the sender’s responsibility to ensure the receiver understands the information, not just to deliver it. Sending a well-structured report does not guarantee comprehension. The project manager should have followed up to confirm understanding, particularly for key decisions that could affect stakeholder behavior. Why the other options are incorrect: The responsibility for ensuring understanding lies with the sender, not the receiver. Expecting stakeholders to proactively ask questions shifts accountability in the wrong direction. The scenario describes the reports as clear and well-structured. Assuming the content was too detailed introduces an assumption not supported by the scenario. More frequent meetings might have surfaced the misunderstanding sooner, but they would not address the root cause. The issue is not meeting frequency but the absence of confirmation that the communication was understood.","id":"book-stakeholder-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Stakeholders","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"Two senior developers on a project cannot agree on which technical approach to use for a critical system integration. Both are experienced, both have valid arguments, and the deadline is approaching. The project manager needs to resolve the situation quickly. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Assign one developer’s approach and move forward to avoid further delays.","B":"Ask both developers to set aside their preferences and proceed with whatever the team decides.","C":"Bring both developers together to discuss the options and work toward a solution that addresses both of their concerns.","D":"Escalate the decision to senior management since the technical complexity exceeds the project manager’s authority."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Bring both developers together to discuss the options and work toward a solution that addresses both of their concerns. Explanation: Problem-solving through collaboration is the preferred conflict resolution technique because it seeks a solution that genuinely addresses both sides. When both parties feel heard and the solution meets their core concerns, the conflict is truly resolved rather than suppressed. Why the other options are incorrect: Assigning one developer’s approach is forcing, a win-lose technique where one side gets everything and the other gets nothing. This resolves the immediate impasse but creates resentment and may produce a worse technical outcome by dismissing valid concerns. Asking both developers to defer to the team describes smoothing. It downplays the conflict without resolving it, and the underlying disagreement may resurface later. Escalating to senior management is premature. The project manager has not yet attempted to bring the parties together, which is always the appropriate first step before involving higher authority.","id":"book-resource-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager notices that a senior developer on the team is consistently disengaged and producing below-average work despite receiving competitive pay, good working conditions, and strong job security. When asked, the developer says he feels the work is repetitive and offers no opportunity for growth or recognition. Which motivational theory best explains this situation?","opts":{"A":"Maslow’s hierarchy of needs, because the developer’s lower-level needs have not been fully met.","B":"Herzberg’s two-factor theory, because hygiene factors are satisfied but motivating factors are absent.","C":"McGregor’s Theory X, because the developer is avoiding responsibility and needs closer supervision.","D":"Expectancy theory, because the developer does not believe his effort will lead to meaningful rewards."},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Herzberg’s two-factor theory, because hygiene factors are satisfied but motivating factors are absent. Explanation: Herzberg distinguishes between hygiene factors, which prevent dissatisfaction when present, and motivating factors, which drive genuine engagement. Pay, working conditions, and job security are hygiene factors. The developer’s disengagement stems from the absence of motivating factors such as recognition, growth, and meaningful work. No other theory makes this specific distinction. Why the other options are incorrect: Maslow says people are motivated by progressing through levels of needs, from basic needs up to esteem and self-actualization. You could argue the developer is seeking esteem or growth needs. But the question is more specifically framed around having pay, security, and working conditions already satisfied while lacking growth, meaningful work, and recognition. That pattern maps much more directly to Herzberg, which explicitly separates hygiene factors from motivators. Theory X assumes employees are inherently unmotivated and need close supervision. The scenario describes a capable senior developer whose disengagement has a clear and specific cause. Applying Theory X misdiagnoses the problem. Expectancy theory focuses on whether effort leads to rewards. The developer’s issue is not about the link between effort and reward but about the absence of intrinsically motivating work.","id":"book-resource-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A senior developer on a software project is widely regarded as the most technically skilled person on the team. When disagreements arise about architecture or implementation, team members consistently defer to her judgment. She holds no managerial title and has never used threats or incentives to gain cooperation. Which form of power does this best describe?","opts":{"A":"Reward power","B":"Expert power","C":"Formal power","D":"Referent power"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Expert power Explanation: Expert power comes from knowledge, skill, and technical competence. When team members defer to someone because of what they know rather than their title or ability to reward or punish, that is expert power in action. The team’s consistent deference on technical matters is the defining indicator. Why the other options are incorrect: Reward power is the ability to influence others through tangible or intangible rewards. The scenario describes no reward-giving behavior as the source of her influence. Formal power comes from an official position or title. The scenario explicitly describes someone with no managerial title whose influence comes from competence, not position. Referent power is based on personality, charisma, and admiration. While she may be well-liked, the scenario points specifically to technical expertise as the reason the team defers to her, not personal admiration.","id":"book-resource-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager observes that her team, which struggled with disagreements and competing ideas during the early weeks of the project, has recently begun working more smoothly together. Team members are starting to respect each other’s opinions, agree on processes, and collaborate without the friction that characterized earlier meetings. Which stage of team development is this?","opts":{"A":"Forming","B":"Storming","C":"Norming","D":"Performing"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Norming Explanation: In the norming stage, the team moves past the conflicts of storming and begins to reach consensus on how to work together. Members develop mutual respect, agree on processes and norms, and start collaborating more effectively. The transition from friction to cooperation is the defining characteristic of this stage. Why the other options are incorrect: Forming is the initial stage where the team first comes together. The scenario describes a team that has already been through early conflict, which means forming has passed. Storming is characterized by conflict and competing ideas. The scenario explicitly describes the team moving past that friction, not experiencing it. Performing is when the team is operating at peak efficiency with minimal oversight needed. The team described is still transitioning into collaboration, not yet at the high-performance level of performing.","id":"book-resource-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A software development team is midway through a sprint. During the daily standup, the lead developer mentions that the team has been waiting three days for access to a testing environment in another department. She emailed the department twice with no response. Without the environment, the team cannot complete two of their sprint commitments. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Ask the lead developer to follow up with a phone call since email may not be the most effective channel.","B":"Raise the issue in the next sprint review so the product owner can decide how to handle it.","C":"Escalate the situation to senior management and wait for direction on how to proceed.","D":"Reach out to the other department directly to resolve the access issue on the team’s behalf."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Reach out to the other department directly to resolve the access issue on the team’s behalf. Explanation: Removing obstacles that prevent the team from doing their best work is a core responsibility of a servant leader. Rather than leaving the team to navigate organizational barriers on their own, the project manager uses their access and influence to resolve blockers directly and keep the sprint on track. Why the other options are incorrect: The team has already tried email twice without response. Asking the lead developer to try a different channel keeps the burden on the team for a problem that a good servant leader should resolve on their behalf. The sprint review is a customer-facing ceremony at the end of the sprint, not the place to resolve an active blocker. Waiting would allow the stall to continue for days. Escalating to senior management before attempting to resolve it directly is premature. The project manager has the organizational access to address this without involving higher authority.","id":"book-resource-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager notices that one of her key developers has been missing sprint ceremonies and falling behind on his commitments. When she speaks with him, he explains that his department manager has been pulling him into operational work several times a week. The developer seems uncomfortable and caught in the middle. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Speak with the developer about prioritizing his project commitments over department work.","B":"Escalate the situation to the sponsor since the project timeline is being affected.","C":"Meet with the functional manager to negotiate a schedule that balances both the project and operational needs.","D":"Document the absences and raise them during the next project status meeting with stakeholders."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Meet with the functional manager to negotiate a schedule that balances both the project and operational needs. Explanation: The root cause here is a resource conflict between the project and the functional department. The appropriate response is for the project manager to engage the functional manager directly and negotiate an arrangement that works for both sides. Why the other options are incorrect: Asking the developer to prioritize project work over department work puts him in an impossible position between two competing managers. The conflict needs to be resolved at the management level, not delegated to the team member caught in the middle. Escalating to the sponsor is premature. The project manager has not yet attempted to resolve the conflict directly with the functional manager. Documenting absences and raising them in a status meeting is reactive and does not address the root cause. It also risks embarrassing the developer publicly without solving the underlying resource dispute.","id":"book-resource-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager has been under significant pressure due to a series of missed deadlines. During a team meeting, a developer raises a concern about an upcoming milestone. The project manager says the team needs to find a way to make it work and moves the agenda forward. Over the following two weeks, the team stops volunteering information in meetings and begins only responding when directly asked. How could the project manager have avoided this outcome?","opts":{"A":"By practicing self-regulation and responding to the team’s concerns without letting pressure dictate their reaction.","B":"By updating the project schedule before the meeting to set more realistic expectations with the team.","C":"By establishing a formal process for team members to submit concerns outside of meetings.","D":"By escalating the milestone concern to the sponsor before the meeting took place."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: By practicing self-regulation and responding to the team’s concerns without letting pressure dictate their reaction. Explanation: The team’s withdrawal was a direct response to feeling that raising concerns was unwelcome. A project manager with strong self-regulation manages their emotional responses under pressure rather than letting frustration shape how they respond to the team. Acknowledging the concern and keeping communication open would have prevented the erosion of trust that followed. Why the other options are incorrect: A more realistic schedule might have reduced pressure, but it would not have changed how the project manager responded in the meeting or prevented the team from disengaging afterward. A formal submission process does not address the interpersonal dynamic that caused the team to go silent. If the environment feels unsafe, a process for raising concerns will not be used regardless of how accessible it is. Escalating to the sponsor before the meeting does not address what happened in the meeting itself. The team’s disengagement was caused by the project manager’s in-the-moment response, not by a lack of prior escalation.","id":"book-resource-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"Two developers are assigned very similar tasks. The first is given five days and submits her work on day five. The second is given ten days and submits his work on day ten. Both report the work was straightforward with no significant challenges. What does this scenario best illustrate?","opts":{"A":"Sandbagging","B":"Parkinson’s Law","C":"Student syndrome","D":"Self-protection"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Parkinson’s Law Explanation: Parkinson’s Law states that work expands to fill the time available for its completion. Despite having identical tasks, each developer used their entire allotted time. Neither reported significant difficulty, suggesting the difference in completion time was driven by the deadline itself rather than the actual effort required. Why the other options are incorrect: Sandbagging involves deliberately overestimating how long a task will take to make performance look better later. The scenario describes no manipulation of estimates, only that both developers used all their available time. Student syndrome describes delaying the start of work until the last possible moment. The scenario does not indicate when either developer started, only when they finished. Self-protection involves hiding delays or problems to avoid blame. Both developers finished on time and reported no issues, which does not suggest any concealment of problems.","id":"book-resource-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A software development project has just completed its coding phase and is transitioning into testing. The testing team is ready to begin but the development team is still occupying their workspace and consuming licenses that testing needs. What component of the resource management plan was most likely missing?","opts":{"A":"Equipment management plan","B":"Staff release plan","C":"Resource calendar","D":"Staff acquisition plan"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Staff release plan Explanation: The staff release plan covers not just end-of-project transitions but also the movement of resources between phases. Without it, outgoing teams linger past their phase, blocking incoming teams and creating resource conflicts. Both symptoms in the scenario point to this gap. Why the other options are incorrect: An equipment management plan addresses how physical equipment is tracked and maintained, not how human resources transition between phases. A resource calendar tracks when resources are available and scheduled, but does not govern when or how resources are transitioned from one phase to the next. A staff acquisition plan addresses how to bring resources onto the project. The testing team is already ready, so acquisition is not the issue. The problem is that the outgoing team has not been released.","id":"book-resource-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project manager drafts a team charter over the weekend and presents it to the team on their first day. Three months later, team members regularly make decisions without consulting the people the charter designated as required approvers. What most likely caused this situation?","opts":{"A":"The team charter did not include a decision-making section.","B":"The project manager should have shared the charter with the team before the project began.","C":"The team charter should have been updated when the decision-making issues first appeared.","D":"The team was not involved in creating the charter and is therefore less likely to feel ownership over its rules."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: The team was not involved in creating the charter and is therefore less likely to feel ownership over its rules. Explanation: A team charter is most effective when the team creates it themselves. People are more likely to follow rules they had a say in establishing because they feel personal ownership over them. A charter handed down by the project manager may be technically complete but lacks the buy-in that makes it work in practice. Why the other options are incorrect: The charter did designate required approvers for decisions, so the decision-making process was covered. The issue is not what the charter contained but whether the team felt invested in following it. Sharing the charter with the team before the project is better than presenting it on day one, but it still does not give the team the sense of ownership that comes from creating it themselves. Updating the charter when issues appear is a reactive measure that does not address why the team ignored the existing rules in the first place.","id":"book-resource-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager needs a senior network engineer for a critical phase of an infrastructure upgrade. The engineer’s functional manager says she is unavailable due to department commitments and offers a junior engineer as an alternative. The project manager believes the junior engineer lacks the experience needed for the work. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Accept the junior engineer and adjust the project schedule to account for the additional time the work may take.","B":"Escalate the situation to the sponsor and request that senior management intervene.","C":"Meet with the functional manager to discuss the project’s specific needs and work toward an arrangement that addresses both the project and department requirements.","D":"Document the resource constraint as a risk and proceed with whatever resources are available."},"ans":"C","exp":"C: Meet with the functional manager to discuss the project’s specific needs and work toward an arrangement that addresses both the project and department requirements. Explanation: When the needed resource is controlled by a functional manager, direct negotiation is the appropriate response. Clearly communicating the project’s needs and understanding the department’s constraints gives both sides the opportunity to reach an arrangement that balances the organization’s needs. Why the other options are incorrect: Accepting the junior engineer without attempting to negotiate accepts a resource risk without exploring whether a better arrangement is possible. Schedule adjustments do not address the underlying skill gap. Escalating to senior management is premature. The project manager has not yet attempted to negotiate directly with the functional manager. Documenting the constraint as a risk without attempting to resolve it is passive. Risk documentation is appropriate after negotiation has failed or as a parallel step, not as a substitute for attempting to secure the right resource.","id":"book-resource-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is overseeing a large construction project involving dozens of workers, heavy machinery, specialized software, and raw materials. The finance team has asked for a way to track and report resource costs by category so they can tie spending to the organization’s accounting system. What tool should the project manager use?","opts":{"A":"WBS","B":"OBS","C":"RBS","D":"RAM"},"ans":"C","exp":"C: RBS Explanation: The resource breakdown structure (RBS) is a hierarchical breakdown of resources organized by category and type, such as people, equipment, materials, and software. It is specifically designed to support cost tracking by category and integrates with the organization’s accounting system, making it the right tool for the finance team’s request. Why the other options are incorrect: The work breakdown structure (WBS) organizes project scope into deliverables and work packages. It describes what work needs to be done, not how resources are categorized for cost tracking purposes. The organizational breakdown structure (OBS) shows reporting relationships and roles on the project. It is a people-focused chart, not a resource cost tracking tool. The responsibility assignment matrix (RAM) maps resources to work packages to show who is responsible for what. It does not categorize resources by type or support accounting integration.","id":"book-resource-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"A software development project is repeatedly missing release dates. Over the last three sprints, the development team has met its sprint commitments and coding defects have decreased. Despite this, release dates continue to slip. What should the project manager do next?","opts":{"A":"Reduce scope per release so the team can meet dates more consistently.","B":"Add a release readiness review to ensure completed work is better prepared for deployment.","C":"Reset stakeholder expectations to align release targets with current throughput.","D":"Analyze the workflow to identify the constraint limiting overall delivery."},"ans":"D","exp":"D: Analyze the workflow to identify the constraint limiting overall delivery. Explanation: The Theory of Constraints states that overall performance is limited by the single biggest bottleneck in the system. When development is meeting its commitments but releases are still slipping, the constraint is somewhere else in the workflow. The correct first step is to find where work is accumulating or stalling before addressing it. Improving other areas will not increase throughput until the bottleneck is identified and resolved. Why the other options are incorrect: Reducing scope treats the symptom rather than the cause. If a bottleneck exists, doing less work upstream will not resolve it and may mask the real problem. Adding a release readiness review introduces a new process step without first understanding what is causing the delays. It may even add to the bottleneck depending on the details of the situation. Resetting stakeholder expectations accepts the current performance as fixed. A PM should identify and remove the constraint to improve throughput, not adjust expectations around it.","id":"book-resource-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":14,"q":"A department manager takes over a project team and immediately establishes a policy requiring her approval on every decision, no matter how minor. When asked why, she explains that in her experience, people cut corners when left unsupervised and need clear oversight to stay on track. Which motivational theory best describes this manager’s underlying assumptions?","opts":{"A":"McGregor’s Theory X, because the manager assumes people will avoid responsibility without direct oversight.","B":"Maslow’s hierarchy of needs, because the manager is addressing the team’s need for structure and security.","C":"Herzberg’s two-factor theory, because close supervision is a hygiene factor that prevents dissatisfaction.","D":"McClelland’s theory of needs, because the manager has a high need for power and control over others."},"ans":"A","exp":"A: McGregor’s Theory X, because the manager assumes people will avoid responsibility without direct oversight. Explanation: Theory X describes a management philosophy built on the assumption that people inherently dislike work and will avoid responsibility unless closely controlled. The manager’s policy and her stated reasoning map directly to this belief. Why the other options are incorrect: Maslow’s hierarchy addresses what motivates individuals based on unmet needs, not a manager’s assumptions about employee behavior. Structure and security are needs a team member might seek, not a justification for micromanagement. Herzberg’s two-factor theory distinguishes between hygiene factors that prevent dissatisfaction and motivators that drive engagement. Supervision is a hygiene factor, but Herzberg’s theory describes conditions affecting employee motivation, not a manager’s assumptions about human nature. While the manager may have a high need for power, McClelland’s framework describes personal motivation drivers, not management philosophy assumptions about others.","id":"book-resource-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"A project manager is overseeing a software development team that consistently misses delivery targets despite individual team members completing their assigned work on time. Work appears to move slowly between phases, with features sitting idle for days between design, development, and testing handoffs. What tool should the project manager use to address this?","opts":{"A":"Resource histogram","B":"Value stream mapping","C":"Resource breakdown structure","D":"Kanban board"},"ans":"B","exp":"B: Value stream mapping Explanation: Value stream mapping visualizes the steps in a workflow and identifies where delays, inefficiencies, or waste are occurring. When work is completing individual steps on time but still moving slowly through the overall process, the problem is in the handoffs and flow between steps. VSM gives the team a clear picture of where time is being lost so targeted improvements can be made. Why the other options are incorrect: A resource histogram shows how many resources are allocated over time. It is useful for identifying overallocation or availability gaps but does not visualize workflow steps or handoff delays. A resource breakdown structure categorizes resources by type for cost tracking purposes. It does not address workflow efficiency or identify where work is stalling between phases. A Kanban board visualizes current work in progress and helps teams manage flow, but it shows the current state of tasks rather than mapping the full end-to-end workflow to identify systemic inefficiencies across phases.","id":"book-resource-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Resources","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":1,"q":"A project manager is conducting a weekly status meeting. A team member reports that a critical software module has failed in testing and will require significant rework. A second team member raises a concern that the production server may be unable to handle the expected user load at launch. What should the project manager record each situation as?","opts":{"A":"Both should be added to the risk register because both have the potential to delay the project.","B":"The failed module should be recorded in the issue log; the server capacity concern should be recorded in the risk register.","C":"Both should be recorded in the issue log because both require immediate management attention.","D":"The server capacity concern should be recorded in the issue log; the failed module should be added to the risk register."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. The failed module should be recorded in the issue log; the server capacity concern should be recorded in the risk register. Explanation: A risk is an uncertain event that may or may not occur. An issue is something that has already happened and requires immediate attention. The module failure has already occurred, making it an issue. The server capacity concern is a future uncertainty that has not yet materialized, making it a risk. Why the other options are incorrect: Recording both in the risk register misclassifies the module failure. Something that has already happened is an issue, regardless of its potential impact on the project. Recording both in the issue log misclassifies the server concern. An uncertain future event belongs in the risk register, not the issue log. The server concern belongs in the risk register, not the issue log. Only events that have already occurred are logged as issues.","id":"book-risk-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager has completed a risk identification workshop and the team has identified over thirty potential threats and opportunities. A stakeholder asks to see the document that lists each identified risk, its cause, its potential impact, and the planned response. What should the project manager provide?","opts":{"A":"The risk management plan, since it governs the approach to identifying and responding to risks on the project.","B":"The risk report, since it summarizes the project’s overall risk exposure and risk categories.","C":"The risk register, since it records individual project risks along with their causes, impacts, and planned responses.","D":"The project management plan, since it integrates all project management activities including risk."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. The risk register, since it records individual project risks along with their causes, impacts, and planned responses. Explanation: The risk register is the artifact where individual project risks are documented, including each risk’s cause, the potential event, and its impact. It is updated throughout the risk management processes as analysis and response planning are completed. Why the other options are incorrect: The risk management plan defines how risk management activities will be conducted across the project. It does not list the specific risks themselves. The risk report describes overall project risk, including sources of risk exposure and summary-level information. It does not document individual risks with their causes and responses. The project management plan integrates all subsidiary plans but is not where individual identified risks are listed and tracked.","id":"book-risk-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A program manager asks the project manager for a document that provides a summary of the sources of overall project risk, the distribution of risk by category, and the project’s current exposure across all identified threats and opportunities. What should the project manager provide?","opts":{"A":"The risk report, since it describes overall project risk including sources, categories, and the full exposure picture.","B":"The risk register, since it contains all identified risks along with their probability, impact, and response plans.","C":"The risk management plan, since it outlines how risks are categorized and managed across the project.","D":"The risk breakdown structure, since it organizes risks by category and provides a hierarchical view of the project’s risk landscape."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. The risk report, since it describes overall project risk including sources, categories, and the full exposure picture. Explanation: The risk report covers overall project risk. It includes information such as the sources of overall project risk, risk categories, and the combined picture of the project’s current exposure. The risk register covers individual risks; the risk report covers the project’s risk posture as a whole. Why the other options are incorrect: The risk register records individual project risks. It does not provide the summary-level view of overall project risk exposure that the program manager is asking for. The risk management plan defines how risk processes will be conducted. It does not describe the current risk status or exposure level of the project. The risk breakdown structure is a categorization tool used during planning. It does not describe the project’s current overall risk exposure.","id":"book-risk-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A software development team discovers that one developer has advanced expertise in an automation framework that could eliminate several manual testing processes entirely. The team estimates this could accelerate delivery by three weeks if the developer is assigned to lead the automation work immediately. What risk response strategy should the project manager apply?","opts":{"A":"Enhance the opportunity by providing the developer with additional training to further increase their automation capabilities.","B":"Share the opportunity by recommending the developer also assist other projects across the organization.","C":"Accept the opportunity and monitor whether the automation work delivers the expected efficiency.","D":"Exploit the opportunity by assigning the developer to lead the automation work to ensure the efficiency gain is fully realized."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Exploit the opportunity by assigning the developer to lead the automation work to ensure the efficiency gain is fully realized. Explanation: Exploit is the opportunity response used when the team wants to ensure the opportunity actually occurs. It is the positive counterpart to avoidance. Assigning the developer directly to the automation work locks in the benefit rather than leaving it to chance. Why the other options are incorrect: Enhance is used to increase the probability or magnitude of an opportunity, not to ensure it happens. The opportunity here is already defined and actionable; exploit is the appropriate response. Sharing involves extending the opportunity to benefit other projects or parties. The goal here is to capture the full benefit on this project, not distribute it. Acceptance leaves the opportunity to chance. When a concrete opportunity can be actively seized, accepting it passively forgoes the benefit the team has already identified.","id":"book-risk-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A construction project team identifies that favorable weather in the coming weeks could allow outdoor concrete to cure faster than planned, potentially reducing the schedule by two days. The team cannot guarantee the weather will hold but wants to increase the likelihood of capturing the benefit. What risk response strategy should the project manager apply?","opts":{"A":"Exploit the opportunity by scheduling all outdoor concrete work to begin immediately to lock in the schedule reduction.","B":"Enhance the opportunity by researching whether weather-optimized concrete additives could further accelerate curing if conditions remain favorable.","C":"Share the opportunity by coordinating with other construction projects to benefit from the same favorable conditions.","D":"Accept the opportunity and adjust the schedule if the faster curing actually occurs."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Enhance the opportunity by researching whether weather-optimized concrete additives could further accelerate curing if conditions remain favorable. Explanation: Enhance is the opportunity response used to increase the probability or impact of a positive risk. The team cannot guarantee the weather will cooperate, so they cannot fully exploit it. Instead, they are taking steps to make the opportunity more likely to occur and to amplify its benefit if it does. Why the other options are incorrect: Exploit is used to ensure an opportunity happens. The team cannot guarantee favorable weather, so they cannot exploit it by making certain it occurs. Sharing involves extending the benefit to other parties or projects. The goal here is to increase the likelihood and impact for this project, not to distribute it elsewhere. Acceptance leaves the outcome entirely to chance. When the team can take actions to improve the probability or impact of a positive risk, acceptance is a lower-value response than enhancement.","id":"book-risk-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"A project manager is leading one of seven product development projects in a large portfolio. During risk identification, the team identifies that a potential change in international trade policy could disrupt supply chains across all seven projects simultaneously. The risk falls entirely outside the project manager’s authority and scope to address. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Escalate the risk to the portfolio or program level, since it affects multiple projects and is outside this project’s authority to manage.","B":"Mitigate the risk by identifying alternative suppliers who are less dependent on international trade routes.","C":"Accept the risk passively and document that no response is available at the project level.","D":"Transfer the risk by arranging supply chain insurance to protect this project from the financial impact of a trade disruption."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Escalate the risk to the portfolio or program level, since it affects multiple projects and is outside this project’s authority to manage. Explanation: Escalation is the appropriate response when a risk is outside the project’s scope and may affect multiple projects or the broader organization. Once escalated, the project team is no longer responsible for monitoring or responding to that risk. The risk is managed at the level with authority over it. Why the other options are incorrect: Mitigation attempts to reduce the risk at the project level, but a risk that spans multiple projects and exceeds the project’s authority is more appropriately handled at the portfolio level where decisions can be coordinated. Passive acceptance is appropriate for low-impact risks. A risk affecting seven projects simultaneously at an organizational level warrants escalation, not inaction. Transfer shifts financial consequences to a third party but does not address the underlying risk or the coordination needed across all seven affected projects. Escalation allows the appropriate organizational level to determine the right response.","id":"book-risk-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager is reviewing the risk register and encounters two risks. For the first, the team considers a vendor price increase likely enough that they have set aside a financial buffer specifically to cover the additional cost if it occurs. For the second, the probability of a major seismic event is so low that the team has decided to take no action and simply monitor the situation. Which statements correctly describe the acceptance strategy applied to each?","opts":{"A":"Both risks are being passively accepted because neither involves attempting to prevent the event.","B":"The price increase risk is being passively accepted; the seismic risk is being actively accepted.","C":"The price increase risk is being actively accepted; the seismic risk is being passively accepted.","D":"Both risks are being actively accepted because both have been formally identified and included in the risk register."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. The price increase risk is being actively accepted; the seismic risk is being passively accepted. Explanation: Active acceptance involves setting aside contingency reserves, time, or resources specifically to use if the risk occurs. The financial buffer for the price increase is an active acceptance response. Passive acceptance means monitoring with no action planned. The decision to simply watch the seismic risk and do nothing is passive acceptance. Why the other options are incorrect: Both cannot be passive acceptance. The team has set aside a financial buffer for the price increase, which constitutes an active response even though the event itself is not being prevented. The classifications are reversed. The financial buffer indicates active acceptance of the price risk; monitoring without action indicates passive acceptance of the seismic risk. Being in the risk register does not make an acceptance response active. Active acceptance is specifically defined by the allocation of contingency reserves or other resources in anticipation of the risk.","id":"book-risk-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project team identifies a risk that a third-party data integration service may become unavailable. Rather than immediately building a backup, the team agrees to activate an internal processing workflow only if the third-party service experiences downtime lasting more than four hours. What type of risk response strategy is the team applying?","opts":{"A":"Mitigation, because the team is reducing the impact of a service outage by having an internal alternative available.","B":"Active acceptance, because the team has defined a contingency plan that will be used if the risk occurs.","C":"Transfer, because the team is shifting processing responsibility to the internal team under defined conditions.","D":"Contingent response, because the team has defined a specific trigger condition that activates the response only when needed."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Contingent response, because the team has defined a specific trigger condition that activates the response only when needed. Explanation: A contingent response strategy is one that is pre-planned but only activated when a specific trigger condition is met. The team has identified a clear trigger (four hours of downtime) and a specific action to take when that condition is reached. This is distinct from responses that are implemented immediately. Why the other options are incorrect: Mitigation involves taking action to reduce the probability or impact of a risk before it occurs. The team is not currently doing anything to reduce the likelihood of the outage; they are waiting for a specific condition. Active acceptance involves setting aside reserves to absorb the impact. Here the team has pre-planned a specific response action tied to a trigger, which is the defining characteristic of a contingent response. Transfer would shift the risk to another party. Keeping the response internal and conditional on a trigger does not transfer the risk; it establishes a conditional action plan.","id":"book-risk-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager is discussing risk planning with the team. The team has documented a risk that a key supplier may miss a delivery deadline, based on a pattern seen in prior projects. A second concern arises: the team acknowledges there may be entirely unforeseen technical failures they have never encountered and cannot anticipate. How should the project manager address each concern?","opts":{"A":"Both should be added to the risk register since any potential impact on the project should be formally tracked.","B":"The supplier delay risk should be planned for in the risk register; the unforeseen technical failures should be addressed through project resilience measures such as contingency reserves and adaptive processes.","C":"The supplier delay risk requires no response since it is a commonly known issue; the unforeseen failures should be escalated to senior management.","D":"Both require contingency reserves since the team cannot control the occurrence of either."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. The supplier delay risk should be planned for in the risk register; the unforeseen technical failures should be addressed through project resilience measures such as contingency reserves and adaptive processes. Explanation: The supplier delay is a known-unknown: a risk the team is aware could happen but does not know if it will. Known-unknowns belong in the risk register with planned responses. The unforeseen technical failures represent unknown-unknowns: things the team is not even aware they do not know. Because these cannot be identified or planned for in advance, the appropriate response is project resilience rather than a specific risk plan. Why the other options are incorrect: Unknown-unknowns cannot be added to the risk register because they have not been identified. Only known risks can be documented and tracked. The supplier delay is a known risk that warrants a documented response, not inaction. Escalating unknown-unknowns to senior management does not address them; resilience measures are the appropriate tool. Contingency reserves address the financial impact of risks but do not substitute for a planned response to a known risk. The supplier delay warrants a specific planned response in addition to any reserve allocation.","id":"book-risk-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"A project manager presents a status update to the project sponsor. The sponsor asks two questions: first, whether the specific risk of a regulatory approval delay is still being actively tracked; and second, what the total level of uncertainty affecting the project looks like across all risk areas combined. What should the project manager reference to answer each question?","opts":{"A":"The risk register to address the regulatory approval delay; the risk report to address total project uncertainty.","B":"The risk report to address the regulatory approval delay; the risk register to address total project uncertainty.","C":"The risk management plan for both, since it governs how both individual and overall risks are handled.","D":"The issue log for the regulatory approval delay; the risk register to address total project uncertainty."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. The risk register to address the regulatory approval delay; the risk report to address total project uncertainty. Explanation: The risk register tracks individual project risks, including specific threats like a regulatory approval delay. The risk report addresses overall project risk, including the sources and categories of uncertainty affecting the project as a whole. Each document serves a distinct purpose in risk communication. Why the other options are incorrect: The risk register tracks individual risks; the risk report tracks overall project risk. Reversing this misidentifies what each document contains. The risk management plan defines how risk processes will be conducted. It does not describe the current status of specific risks or the overall risk exposure. The issue log tracks problems that have already occurred. A regulatory approval delay that has not yet happened is a risk, not an issue, and belongs in the risk register.","id":"book-risk-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager needs to communicate schedule uncertainty to senior leadership. Several activities have uncertain durations. Rather than presenting a single completion date, the project manager wants to show the range of possible outcomes and the probability of finishing by the target date. What technique should the project manager use?","opts":{"A":"Sensitivity analysis, to identify which activities have the greatest individual influence on the overall schedule.","B":"Expected monetary value analysis, to calculate the weighted financial impact of schedule delays.","C":"Monte Carlo simulation, to model a range of possible completion dates based on the probability distributions of individual activity durations.","D":"A probability and impact matrix, to classify each schedule risk as high, medium, or low priority."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Monte Carlo simulation, to model a range of possible completion dates based on the probability distributions of individual activity durations. Explanation: Monte Carlo simulation uses a computer to model many possible outcomes by running the schedule through thousands of scenarios based on the probability distributions of individual activity durations. The result is a range of possible completion dates and the likelihood of achieving the target, which is precisely what leadership needs to understand schedule uncertainty. Why the other options are incorrect: Sensitivity analysis identifies which variables have the most influence on a specific outcome. It does not produce a probability distribution of possible completion dates. Expected monetary value is used to assess the financial impact of risks or compare decision alternatives. It does not model a distribution of possible schedule outcomes. A probability and impact matrix prioritizes individual risks based on their likelihood and severity. It does not produce a range of possible schedule outcomes or completion date probabilities.","id":"book-risk-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":12,"q":"A project manager is evaluating two approaches for a data security upgrade. Option 1 is to build an internal solution at a base cost of $180,000, with a 40% probability of requiring $150,000 in additional rework if compliance testing fails. Option 2 is to purchase a vendor solution at a base cost of $220,000, with a 5% probability of $50,000 in integration costs. Based on expected monetary value, which option should the project manager select?","opts":{"A":"Option 1, because its base cost is $40,000 lower than Option 2.","B":"Option 2, because its risk probability is significantly lower than Option 1’s.","C":"Option 1, because its total expected cost including risk is lower than Option 2’s base cost alone.","D":"Option 2, because its total expected cost including risk is $222,500, compared to Option 1’s total of $240,000."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Option 2, because its total expected cost including risk is $222,500, compared to Option 1’s total of $240,000. Explanation: EMV equals probability multiplied by risk cost. For Option 1: 40% x $150,000 = $60,000. Total = $180,000 + $60,000 = $240,000. For Option 2: 5% x $50,000 = $2,500. Total = $220,000 + $2,500 = $222,500. Despite Option 2’s higher base cost, its lower risk exposure produces a lower total expected cost. Why the other options are incorrect: Comparing base costs alone ignores risk. The lower base cost of Option 1 does not account for the significant expected cost of the rework risk. A lower risk probability does not by itself determine the better option. Probability must be combined with the potential cost to calculate the actual financial exposure. Option 1’s total expected cost of $240,000 is higher than Option 2’s, not lower. The calculation must include the EMV of the risk, not just the base cost.","id":"book-risk-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":13,"q":"An agile project team is preparing for the next sprint cycle. The product owner has prioritized the backlog based on business value. During a risk review, the team identifies that one lowerpriority backlog item, if left unaddressed, could invalidate the architectural decisions being made in current sprints and require significant rework later. What should the product owner do, and what concept does this represent?","opts":{"A":"Conduct a technical spike on the architectural risk; this is risk mitigation.","B":"Move the risk-associated item higher in the backlog because the risk of leaving it unaddressed outweighs its relative business value; this is a risk-adjusted backlog.","C":"Accept the risk and continue with value-based prioritization to protect sprint velocity; this is passive acceptance.","D":"Escalate the architectural risk to the project sponsor before making any changes to the sprint plan; this is risk escalation."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Move the risk-associated item higher in the backlog because the risk of leaving it unaddressed outweighs its relative business value; this is a risk-adjusted backlog. Explanation: A risk-adjusted backlog is the product backlog reprioritized to account for risk. Items that are lower in business value but carry high risk may be moved up to ensure risks are addressed before they cause downstream harm. In agile, backlog order is not based only on business value in isolation. The product owner also considers risk, dependencies, and cost of delay/rework. Why the other options are incorrect: A technical spike investigates a specific unknown before committing to a solution. Moving an item up in the backlog because of its risk profile is a prioritization decision, not a spike. Passive acceptance means monitoring without action. Recognizing that a lower-priority item carries a risk significant enough to affect the project and choosing not to reprioritize is not the appropriate agile response. Escalation is for risks outside the project’s authority. An architectural risk within the team’s scope should be addressed through backlog reprioritization, not sponsor escalation.","id":"book-risk-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":14,"q":"An agile development team is concerned that their planned integration approach for a new payment gateway may not handle the required transaction volume. Before committing to full integration work in the next sprint, the team proposes building a minimal test implementation to verify whether the gateway can sustain the expected load under simulated conditions. What is the team proposing?","opts":{"A":"A risk-based spike, to test whether the planned approach would actually solve the identified performance risk before full implementation begins.","B":"A risk-adjusted backlog change, because the payment gateway integration should be reprioritized based on the performance risk.","C":"A mitigation response, because the test implementation will reduce the probability of a performance failure in production.","D":"A contingent response, because the team will only build the full integration if the test confirms it will perform adequately."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. A risk-based spike, to test whether the planned approach would actually solve the identified performance risk before full implementation begins. Explanation: A risk-based spike is a short proof of concept designed to test whether a particular response to a risk would actually work. The team is investigating the viability of their integration approach before committing to it, which is precisely what a risk-based spike is designed to do. It enables fast failure if the approach is inadequate, allowing the team to course-correct early. Why the other options are incorrect: A risk-adjusted backlog change involves reprioritizing existing backlog items based on risk. The team here is proposing a new investigative activity to test a solution, not reordering existing work. Mitigation involves taking action to reduce probability or impact. The test itself does not reduce the risk; it determines whether the planned response would be effective. A contingent response is pre-planned and activated by a trigger. The spike is an investigation to determine feasibility, not a response that waits for a trigger condition.","id":"book-risk-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Agile"},{"n":15,"q":"During execution of a construction project, an unexpected underground utility line is discovered that was not identified in any pre-project surveys. The discovery blocks a critical foundation excavation. With no pre-planned response available, the project manager immediately redirects the excavation path and coordinates an emergency rerouting of the utility line. What is the project manager applying?","opts":{"A":"A contingent response, because the utility discovery triggered a pre-planned action the team had prepared for.","B":"Risk mitigation, because the rerouting reduces the long-term impact of the utility line on the project.","C":"A workaround, because the project manager is developing an unplanned response to an unanticipated risk event.","D":"Risk avoidance, because the excavation is being redirected away from the obstacle entirely."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. A workaround, because the project manager is developing an unplanned response to an unanticipated risk event. Explanation: A workaround is an unplanned response developed in the moment to address a risk that was not anticipated or did not have a planned response. Because the utility line was unknown and no response was prepared, the improvised solution qualifies as a workaround. Workarounds are documented in the Monitor Risks process. Why the other options are incorrect: A contingent response is pre-planned and activated by a known trigger. Because no response was planned for this event, activating an improvised solution is not a contingent response. Risk mitigation involves reducing probability or impact before a risk occurs. The project manager is responding to an event already in progress, not reducing a future risk. Risk avoidance eliminates a risk before it occurs, typically by changing project scope or approach during planning. Responding to an unexpected discovery during execution is reactive, not preventive.","id":"book-risk-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"Risk","domain":"Process","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A project manager on a software development project asks an AI tool to generate a draft risk register for an upcoming release. Within minutes, the AI produces a detailed list of risks, probabilities, and suggested responses. The sponsor is pressuring the team to finalize planning quickly, and the AI output appears thorough and well organized. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Review and validate the AI-generated risks with the team before incorporating them into the project’s risk register.","B":"Submit the AI-generated risk register as the final version to save time, since the output is detailed and the sponsor wants speed.","C":"Discard the AI output entirely and have the team build the risk register manually to avoid relying on AI.","D":"Forward the AI-generated risk register directly to stakeholders for approval without team review."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Review and validate the AI-generated risks with the team before incorporating them into the project’s risk register. Reviewing and validating the output is correct because AI outputs must always be reviewed and validated by the project manager. AI acts as a support tool that can speed up the work, but the project manager remains accountable for the final result. The other options are incorrect. Submitting the register as final is incorrect because it blindly trusts AI output without verification. Discarding the output entirely is incorrect because it rejects a useful tool rather than applying judgment; AI assistance is appropriate once validated. Forwarding the register directly to stakeholders is incorrect because it skips human review and oversight.","id":"book-ai-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"While preparing project deliverables, a project manager uses a generative AI tool to create marketing copy and graphics for a new product website. A team member notes that the AI may have generated the content using material from outside authors and companies. The sponsor wants to publish the website immediately. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Publish the content immediately because AI-generated material is automatically free to use.","B":"Remove all AI-generated content and require the team to recreate everything manually.","C":"Ask the AI tool to confirm whether the content is copyrighted and rely on its response.","D":"Verify ownership and ensure the organization has the proper rights to use the AI-generated content before publishing."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Verify ownership and ensure the organization has the proper rights to use the AI-generated content before publishing. Verifying ownership is correct because AI-generated content may raise ownership and legal questions. The project manager must confirm proper permission and compliance with copyright before the content is used. The other options are incorrect. Publishing immediately is incorrect because it uses content without confirming permission, which may violate copyright. Removing all AI content is incorrect because it overcorrects; verifying compliance is sufficient and AI can still be used responsibly. Relying on the AI tool to confirm copyright is incorrect because it trusts AI without human validation, and AI cannot reliably confirm legal ownership.","id":"book-ai-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"An AI system analyzes several years of historical project data and recommends assigning the most critical tasks to a specific group of team members while excluding others. The project manager notices the recommendation consistently favors the same individuals and may reflect patterns in past data rather than current capability. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Implement the AI recommendation as written, since it is based on objective historical data.","B":"Review the recommendation for potential bias and validate it against current team capabilities before deciding.","C":"Stop using AI for all resource decisions because the data cannot be trusted.","D":"Ask the AI to re-run the analysis repeatedly until it produces a more balanced result."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Review the recommendation for potential bias and validate it against current team capabilities before deciding. Reviewing for bias is correct because AI systems learn from data, and if that data is biased, the results will also be biased. The project manager must validate the output and apply human judgment before acting. The other options are incorrect. Implementing the recommendation as written is incorrect because it assumes AI output is objective and ignores the risk of bias. Stopping all AI use is incorrect because it abandons a useful tool rather than applying oversight. Re-running the analysis until it looks balanced is incorrect because it manipulates the output instead of addressing the underlying bias with human judgment.","id":"book-ai-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project manager is evaluating several delivery strategies for a complex digital transformation program with significant trade-offs in cost, risk, and long-term value. The project manager wants to explore multiple scenarios and forecast potential outcomes before presenting a recommendation to the steering committee. How should the project manager best use AI in this situation?","opts":{"A":"Use AI to automatically select and approve the final delivery strategy without further analysis.","B":"Avoid using AI because strategic decisions are too complex for any AI involvement.","C":"Use AI as a thought partner to model scenarios and analyze trade-offs, then apply judgment to form the recommendation.","D":"Use AI only to generate the meeting agenda for the steering committee."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Use AI as a thought partner to model scenarios and analyze trade-offs, then apply judgment to form the recommendation. Using AI as a thought partner is correct because this is augmentation, the most advanced level of AI use, where AI enhances the project manager’s ability to handle complex and strategic decisions by exploring scenarios and analyzing trade-offs. The project manager still makes the final decision. The other options are incorrect. Letting AI select the strategy is incorrect because it allows AI to make an unchecked strategic decision without human oversight. Avoiding AI entirely is incorrect because AI can meaningfully support complex decision-making. Using AI only for the agenda is incorrect because it underuses AI’s analytical capability for a highvalue strategic decision.","id":"book-ai-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"A project team wants to use an AI tool to analyze customer communications and predict satisfaction trends. The data set includes personally identifiable and regulated customer information. The marketing lead is eager to begin the analysis immediately to inform an upcoming release. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Ensure the data is properly protected and used in compliance with privacy and data protection requirements before proceeding.","B":"Begin the analysis right away because faster insights will benefit the release.","C":"Use the data but remove the AI tool from the workflow to avoid responsibility.","D":"Allow the marketing lead to proceed independently since they own the customer relationship."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Ensure the data is properly protected and used in compliance with privacy and data protection requirements before proceeding. Ensuring data protection and compliance is correct because AI often uses large amounts of data that may include sensitive or regulated information. The project manager must ensure data is protected and used ethically before proceeding. The other options are incorrect. Beginning immediately is incorrect because it ignores privacy and data protection rules. Removing the AI tool while still using the data is incorrect because it does not address the privacy concern at all. Allowing the marketing lead to proceed independently is incorrect because the project manager remains accountable for ensuring compliant and ethical data use.","id":"book-ai-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":6,"q":"During development of a financial web application, an AI optimization tool recommends removing several security verification steps to accelerate the release schedule. The change would shorten the timeline, and the sponsor is focused on an aggressive launch date. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Implement the AI recommendation to meet the aggressive launch date requested by the sponsor.","B":"Implement the change but document that the AI made the decision in order to transfer accountability.","C":"Ask the AI to guarantee that removing the security steps will not cause any harm.","D":"Override the AI recommendation and retain the security features, prioritizing safety and ethical responsibility."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Override the AI recommendation and retain the security features, prioritizing safety and ethical responsibility. Overriding the recommendation is correct because an action may be efficient yet still unethical or unsafe. The project manager must override AI when its suggestion conflicts with safety and professional responsibility, and a human remains accountable for the outcome. The other options are incorrect. Implementing the recommendation is incorrect because it blindly trusts AI at the expense of safety. Documenting that AI made the decision is incorrect because accountability always remains with a human, not the AI. Asking the AI to guarantee safety is incorrect because AI cannot provide such assurance and this still relies on AI in place of human judgment.","id":"book-ai-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":7,"q":"A project manager learns that a new website will consume significant server energy because of inefficient code. Several options exist to address the environmental impact, and leadership has asked the project manager to take the most responsible approach. What should the project manager do first?","opts":{"A":"Purchase renewable energy credits to offset the energy the inefficient site will consume.","B":"Optimize the code to avoid the excessive energy consumption in the first place.","C":"Plan to restore affected systems later by upgrading the infrastructure after launch.","D":"Accept the energy impact now and compensate for it after the project closes."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Optimize the code to avoid the excessive energy consumption in the first place. Optimizing the code is correct because the Sustainability Pyramid directs the project manager to aim for the top first, which is to avoid the negative impact altogether by designing the project to prevent it. Avoiding is the most desirable approach. The other options are incorrect. Purchasing energy credits to offset is incorrect because compensate/offset sits at the bottom of the pyramid and is only a last resort. Restoring systems later is incorrect because restore falls below avoid and minimize on the pyramid. Accepting the impact and compensating afterward is incorrect because it relies on the least desirable level of the pyramid.","id":"book-ai-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":8,"q":"A project manager is initiating a new construction project. Sustainability requirements were not explicitly included in the project charter or business case, and stakeholders have not raised the topic. However, the organization’s enterprise environmental factors emphasize long-term environmental responsibility. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Wait until stakeholders or regulators raise sustainability concerns before addressing them.","B":"Address sustainability only during project closure, when final impacts can be measured.","C":"Proactively include sustainability goals in project documents and discuss them with stakeholders early.","D":"Exclude sustainability since it was not listed as a formal requirement."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Proactively include sustainability goals in project documents and discuss them with stakeholders early. Proactively including sustainability goals is correct because sustainability is something the project manager is proactive about, not reactive. It should be considered from the very beginning and built into key documents and stakeholder discussions even when it is not initially required. The other options are incorrect. Waiting for others to raise the concern is incorrect because it is a reactive approach, while sustainability should be planned early. Addressing it only at closure is incorrect because sustainability must be applied throughout the entire project life cycle. Excluding sustainability is incorrect because it ignores the organization’s enterprise environmental factors and PMI’s expectation to integrate sustainability holistically.","id":"book-ai-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":9,"q":"A project manager must select materials for a manufacturing project. The cheapest option would reduce short-term costs but could harm the environment and the surrounding community. A slightly more expensive eco-friendly option would reduce waste and support responsible sourcing. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Evaluate environmental and social impact alongside cost and choose the option that balances long-term value.","B":"Select the cheapest materials to minimize cost and maximize short-term return on investment.","C":"Delegate the materials decision entirely to the procurement department to avoid responsibility.","D":"Select materials based only on which option delivers the fastest schedule."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Evaluate environmental and social impact alongside cost and choose the option that balances long-term value. Balancing impact and cost is correct because sustainability aligns with the triple bottom line, balancing environmental, social, and economic factors. The project manager should consider long-term value, not only short-term cost. The other options are incorrect. Selecting the cheapest materials is incorrect because it focuses only on cost and short-term ROI while ignoring environmental and social impact. Delegating the decision entirely is incorrect because it avoids the project manager’s accountability for a sustainability-relevant decision. Choosing based only on schedule is incorrect because it prioritizes speed over balanced, responsible decision-making.","id":"book-ai-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":10,"q":"On a website project, key stakeholders request accessibility features so the product can serve a broader audience, including users with disabilities. Adding these features will slightly increase effort but aligns with the organization’s social responsibility goals. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Decline the request because accessibility features are outside the original scope and add effort.","B":"Defer the accessibility features to a future project to protect the current schedule.","C":"Implement the features only if they can be added with no additional effort.","D":"Incorporate the accessibility features to align the project with stakeholder values and social responsibility goals."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Incorporate the accessibility features to align the project with stakeholder values and social responsibility goals. Incorporating the features is correct because sustainability improves stakeholder engagement by aligning the project with stakeholder values, and it supports social responsibility across the performance domains. The other options are incorrect. Declining the request is incorrect because it ignores stakeholder values and social responsibility. Deferring to a future project is incorrect because it delays responsible outcomes, while sustainability should be integrated rather than postponed. Implementing only at no additional effort is incorrect because it conditions a responsible decision purely on zero cost, overlooking long-term value.","id":"book-ai-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"AI and Sustainability","domain":"Business","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":1,"q":"A project is four weeks from a hard deadline when the project manager discovers the current pace of work will result in a two-week delay. The project sponsor tells the project manager to require the team to work overtime on weekends for the remaining four weeks to close the gap. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Require the team to work overtime as the sponsor directs since meeting the deadline is the project’s primary obligation.","B":"Discuss the schedule gap with the team, explore recovery options such as scope reduction or task reprioritization, and present a revised plan to the sponsor.","C":"Accept the delay and update the project schedule baseline to reflect the new completion date.","D":"Ask team members to volunteer for overtime and reassign critical tasks to those who agree."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Discuss the schedule gap with the team, explore recovery options such as scope reduction or task reprioritization, and present a revised plan to the sponsor. Explanation: Mandatory overtime is not a standard project management response to schedule pressure. The sponsor has authority to set direction but the project manager is responsible for finding the most appropriate recovery strategy. Exploring alternatives such as scope adjustment, task reprioritization, or resource reallocation with the team produces more sustainable solutions. Why the other options are incorrect: Requiring mandatory overtime treats a directive as absolute without analyzing whether better alternatives exist. Protecting the team from unnecessary overload is a core project management responsibility. Accepting the delay and re-baselining without attempting recovery skips the analysis phase entirely. Re-baselining is appropriate only after recovery options have been evaluated and exhausted. Volunteering for overtime is still overtime. Structuring it as voluntary does not change the underlying problem and does not involve the team in identifying better alternatives.","id":"book-mindset-1","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":2,"q":"A project manager discovers that a colleague preparing the monthly project status report has been consistently overstating progress to make the project appear on track. Senior leadership has been making resourcing decisions based on these reports. The project manager’s colleague is a close friend. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Speak to the colleague privately and encourage them to correct future reports going forward.","B":"Continue with existing reporting practices to avoid creating conflict that could affect the team.","C":"Review the reports and make corrections before they are distributed without informing the colleague.","D":"Report the misrepresentation to the appropriate person in the organization, regardless of the personal relationship."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Report the misrepresentation to the appropriate person in the organization, regardless of the personal relationship. Explanation: Misrepresenting project status to leadership is an ethical violation that is actively harming the organization through misinformed resourcing decisions. A project manager must act with honesty and transparency even when it is personally difficult. The professional obligation to report unethical behavior takes precedence over personal loyalty. Why the other options are incorrect: Encouraging the colleague to self-correct preserves the relationship but delays accountability and does not address the decisions already made on false data. The PM cannot simply defer this to the colleague’s conscience. Continuing to allow misreporting is complicity in the deception. Avoiding conflict does not justify allowing leadership to be misled. Quietly correcting the reports without disclosure is itself deceptive and does not address the root behavior. The colleague’s misrepresentation and its consequences must be surfaced through the proper channel.","id":"book-mindset-2","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":3,"q":"A project manager receives an email from a stakeholder raising a concern that recent deliverables do not appear to align with the original project vision. The project manager reviews the work and believes the concern is unfounded. No other stakeholders have raised the issue. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Acknowledge the stakeholder’s concern, review the deliverables against the documented vision, and respond with the findings.","B":"Update the risk register to document the stakeholder’s perception as a potential alignment risk and monitor whether similar concerns emerge from other parties.","C":"Forward the email to the sponsor and ask them to review whether the project vision needs to be recommunicated to the broader stakeholder group.","D":"Acknowledge receipt of the concern in a brief reply and revisit it at the next scheduled stakeholder review meeting."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Acknowledge the stakeholder’s concern, review the deliverables against the documented vision, and respond with the findings. Explanation: Every stakeholder concern requires a substantive response, even when the project manager believes it is unfounded. Acknowledging the concern, verifying against documentation, and communicating findings closes the loop with the stakeholder and ensures the PM’s confidence in the work is grounded in documented evidence rather than assumption. Why the other options are incorrect: Logging the concern as a risk and monitoring for recurrence treats a direct stakeholder communication as a passive observation exercise. It generates a paper trail without actually engaging the stakeholder who raised the issue. Forwarding to the sponsor is premature escalation. Responding to a stakeholder concern about vision alignment is within the project manager’s authority and responsibility. The sponsor does not need to be involved before the PM has investigated and replied directly. Acknowledging receipt without investigating and responding is a soft form of deferral. The stakeholder asked a substantive question that requires a substantive answer, not a scheduling placeholder.","id":"book-mindset-3","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":4,"q":"A project team is executing a renovation of a manufacturing facility. Normal protocol requires phased analysis before each work step. Midway through execution, a team member identifies that a critical safety mechanism has been bypassed in a section of the facility where workers are currently present. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Log the safety concern in the issue log and schedule a review meeting with stakeholders to determine the appropriate response.","B":"Consult the risk management plan and implement the approved risk response before taking any other action.","C":"Stop work in the affected area immediately and address the safety issue before any further activity continues.","D":"Notify the project sponsor and wait for direction before making any changes to the work already in progress."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Stop work in the affected area immediately and address the safety issue before any further activity continues. Explanation: When human safety is at immediate risk, standard process steps such as analysis, stakeholder review, or sponsor consultation are overridden. This is the most significant exception to the normal principle of investigating before acting. A known safety hazard where workers are present requires immediate intervention, not scheduled review. Why the other options are incorrect: Logging the concern and scheduling a meeting defers action while workers remain in a known hazardous area. Documentation follows intervention; it does not precede it when physical safety is at immediate risk. Consulting the risk management plan is appropriate for anticipated risks, but an active safety hazard with workers present is not a planned risk event. Response must be immediate, not process-dependent. Notifying the sponsor and waiting for direction introduces delay that is not acceptable when safety is actively compromised. The project manager has the authority and responsibility to stop hazardous work without waiting for executive approval.","id":"book-mindset-4","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":5,"q":"An agile project is in its third sprint. The product owner approaches the project manager and says they are overwhelmed by the complexity of the backlog and asks the project manager to take over prioritizing items for the next sprint. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Take over backlog prioritization for the next sprint to keep the project moving and avoid delays.","B":"Coach the product owner on prioritization techniques and work alongside them to prioritize the backlog rather than doing it on their behalf.","C":"Escalate the product owner’s capacity issue to the project sponsor and request that a product owner capable of their prioritization responsibilities be assigned.","D":"Facilitate a team session where all members vote on backlog priority to distribute the decision-making burden."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Coach the product owner on prioritization techniques and work alongside them to prioritize the backlog rather than doing it on their behalf. Explanation: The product owner is accountable for defining, documenting, and prioritizing the backlog. The project manager’s role is to support, coach, and ensure the process runs effectively, not to take over scope decisions. When the product owner struggles, the correct response is coaching and facilitation, not substitution. Why the other options are incorrect: Taking over prioritization removes accountability from the person responsible for it and gives the project manager authority over scope decisions that do not belong to them. This creates confusion about ownership and undermines agile role clarity. Escalating to the sponsor is premature before the project manager has attempted coaching and direct support. The product owner’s overwhelm is a manageable situation, not one that requires executive reassignment. Team voting on backlog items distributes priority decisions to people who may lack the business context to make them correctly. Backlog ownership belongs to the product owner, not the broader team.","id":"book-mindset-5","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":6,"q":"An agile development team is halfway through a sprint when it becomes clear that a complex feature will take longer than estimated. The team cannot complete all committed work before the sprint ends. A stakeholder asks the project manager to extend the sprint to accommodate the full feature. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Protect the sprint timebox, defer the incomplete feature to the product backlog, and plan it for a future sprint.","B":"Extend the sprint by two to three days to allow the team to complete the committed work.","C":"Reduce the quality requirements for the feature so the team can deliver it within the current sprint.","D":"Replace the team member responsible for the delay and reassign the feature to a faster developer."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Protect the sprint timebox, defer the incomplete feature to the product backlog, and plan it for a future sprint. Explanation: Sprints are fixed timeboxes. In agile, when scope cannot be completed within the committed time, the appropriate response is to adjust scope, not extend the timebox. Incomplete work returns to the product backlog for reprioritization and inclusion in a future sprint. This protects the reliability of sprint planning and maintains the team’s delivery cadence. Why the other options are incorrect: Extending the sprint undermines the timebox principle that makes agile planning predictable. Sprints are not extended simply because work takes longer than estimated; that pattern produces increasingly unreliable sprint planning over time. Reducing quality requirements to fit the timebox violates the Definition of Done and delivers work that has not actually met the team’s completion standard, which is not acceptable regardless of time pressure. Replacing a team member for a single estimation miss is a disproportionate response that damages psychological safety and does not reflect servant leadership.","id":"book-mindset-6","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":7,"q":"A large financial services organization wants to transition all seventeen of its active projects from traditional delivery to agile. The PMO director wants to implement the change across all projects simultaneously over the next month. What should the project manager recommend?","opts":{"A":"Proceed with the simultaneous transition since a coordinated rollout ensures consistency across all projects.","B":"Begin with the highest-priority projects first since they will deliver the most value from the transition fastest.","C":"Delay the transition until all project teams have completed certified agile training.","D":"Recommend piloting the agile approach on one or two selected projects before scaling it across the organization."},"ans":"D","exp":"D. Recommend piloting the agile approach on one or two selected projects before scaling it across the organization. Explanation: Large-scale organizational change should be introduced gradually through pilots. Testing on a small number of projects allows the organization to learn, adjust processes, and identify obstacles before committing the full portfolio. Simultaneous transition across seventeen projects multiplies the risk of failure and leaves no room for learning before the problems become organization-wide. Why the other options are incorrect: Simultaneous transition creates the maximum possible risk exposure. If the change produces problems, they affect every project at the same time with no functioning reference points for comparison or recovery. Starting with high-priority projects is better than a full simultaneous rollout but still skips the learning opportunity that a deliberate pilot provides. High-priority projects carry more risk to the organization if the transition struggles. Delaying until training is complete treats certification as a prerequisite rather than an enabler. Hands-on experience through a pilot is typically more effective at building agile capability than training alone.","id":"book-mindset-7","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":8,"q":"During a project status meeting attended by the full team and two senior stakeholders, the project manager notices that a developer has missed several recent deadlines. To address the pattern, the project manager raises the issue in the meeting and directly asks the developer to explain the delays in front of the group. What should the project manager have done instead?","opts":{"A":"Raised the performance pattern at the end-of-sprint retrospective as a team process issue so the group could collectively identify solutions.","B":"Delegated the performance conversation to the team’s technical lead since direct feedback on development work is typically handled within the development chain.","C":"Spoken with the developer privately before the meeting to understand the cause and agree on a path forward.","D":"Addressed the concern with the developer after the meeting via email, copying the project sponsor to ensure the issue was formally documented."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Spoken with the developer privately before the meeting to understand the cause and agree on a path forward. Explanation: A one-on-one conversation before the meeting allows the PM to understand the root cause without putting the developer in a defensive position, and it preserves the trust and dignity that public confrontation destroys. Speaking privately first also ensures the PM has the full picture before deciding whether further action is needed. Why the other options are incorrect: Raising the concern in a retrospective as a team process issue reframes an individual performance matter as a collective one. It still exposes the developer in a group setting without a prior private conversation, and it may not accurately represent what is actually causing the delays. Delegating to the technical lead removes the project manager’s direct accountability for the situation. Passing the conversation to someone else before attempting direct engagement is avoidance framed as proper process. Emailing after the meeting with the sponsor copied turns a performance concern into a formal documented escalation before any direct conversation has taken place. Copying the sponsor is disproportionate at this stage and signals distrust rather than a genuine attempt to understand and resolve the situation.","id":"book-mindset-8","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":9,"q":"An agile software development team is midway through a sprint when a key customer representative requests a modification to the data export function. The modification was not part of the original backlog. A team member argues the request falls outside the current sprint commitment and recommends the team stay focused on the agreed sprint backlog. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Ask the customer to bring the request to the next sprint planning meeting where the team can formally consider incorporating it.","B":"Add the request to the current sprint to demonstrate responsiveness to the customer and protect the relationship.","C":"Route the request to the product owner to evaluate its urgency and either add it to the backlog or determine whether the sprint goal warrants adjustment.","D":"Evaluate the request and, if it appears high value, notify the team that the sprint backlog will be updated to include it."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Route the request to the product owner to evaluate its urgency and either add it to the backlog or determine whether the sprint goal warrants adjustment. Explanation: The product owner is the appropriate person to evaluate the request, determine its urgency and business value, and decide whether it goes to the backlog for a future sprint or whether it is significant enough that the team and product owner should agree to adjust the sprint goal. Why the other options are incorrect: Asking the customer to raise it at the next sprint planning session defers the request without any evaluation. Sprint planning is for planning committed work, not for receiving new requests for the first time. The product owner should assess urgency now, not at the next ceremony. Adding the request to the current sprint without product owner involvement and team agreement disrupts the sprint commitment. New work should not be inserted mid-sprint without a deliberate decision that the sprint goal warrants adjustment. The project manager does not own backlog decisions. Independently evaluating the request and updating the sprint backlog bypasses the product owner’s authority over scope prioritization entirely.","id":"book-mindset-9","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":10,"q":"A project manager on a commercial construction project receives a change request to substitute the originally specified structural material with an alternative that a team member has calculated costs $20,000 less. The project sponsor suggests approving it immediately given the cost savings. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Analyze the impact of the material change across scope, schedule, cost, risk, and resources before presenting a recommendation.","B":"Approve the change since the team member’s cost analysis confirms it is within budget and the sponsor endorses it.","C":"Refer the change to the technical team and allow them to make the feasibility determination independently.","D":"Reject the change since substituting structural materials mid-project introduces unacceptable risk regardless of cost savings."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Analyze the impact of the material change across scope, schedule, cost, risk, and resources before presenting a recommendation. Explanation: A change to structural materials is not just a cost decision. It can affect construction sequencing, procurement timelines, labor requirements, quality standards, regulatory compliance, and project risk. The project manager’s role as integrator requires analyzing how the change affects all project domains, not just the financial one, before any approval is made. Why the other options are incorrect: Approving based on a single team member’s cost calculation and sponsor preference bypasses the integrative analysis that change control requires. A cost saving that introduces schedule or compliance risk may not be a net benefit. Referring the decision to the technical team alone removes the PM from the integration role. Technical feasibility is one input; the PM must coordinate the full impact across all project areas. Rejecting outright without analysis treats risk avoidance as an absolute rule. The change may be entirely appropriate after a thorough review; dismissal without assessment is not a sound decision.","id":"book-mindset-10","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":11,"q":"A project manager is overseeing two simultaneous projects. One is a traditional infrastructure replacement and the other is an agile software development effort. Both are experiencing schedule pressure. The operations director asks how the project manager monitors schedule performance on each. What should the project manager explain?","opts":{"A":"Both projects use the same approach since schedule performance is measured consistently regardless of delivery method.","B":"On the infrastructure project, schedule is monitored through critical path analysis; on the software project, it is monitored through sprint velocity and team commitment.","C":"On the infrastructure project, velocity tracking identifies which activities are at risk; on the software project, critical path analysis identifies the completion date.","D":"Both projects are monitored through schedule variance analysis since it provides an objective, quantitative measure of performance regardless of the delivery approach used."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. On the infrastructure project, schedule is monitored through critical path analysis; on the software project, it is monitored through sprint velocity and team commitment. Explanation: Schedule management must match the delivery approach. Traditional projects use critical path analysis to identify which activities directly affect the project end date. Agile projects manage schedule through fixed sprint timeboxes, tracking how much work the team completes per iteration through velocity. Why the other options are incorrect: Using the same approach for both projects ignores fundamental differences in how traditional and agile projects plan and control schedule. What works for a sequential dependency-driven plan does not apply to an iterative delivery model. The options are reversed. Velocity tracking requires an iterative backlog structure and does not apply to a dependency-driven infrastructure schedule. Critical path analysis requires defined sequential activities and does not map to agile sprint execution. Schedule variance is an earned value metric designed for predictive projects where planned value can be calculated against a time-phased baseline. Applying it uniformly to an agile project misses the sprint-based mechanisms through which agile teams actually track and manage schedule performance.","id":"book-mindset-11","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":12,"q":"A development team presents their completed work at a sprint review. The customer examines the delivered features and says the functionality does not reflect what they had in mind when the project began. The team is surprised, believing they built exactly what was described in the user stories. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Document the gap between what was delivered and what the customer expected as a defect in the issue log and prioritize rework in the next sprint.","B":"Schedule a requirements workshop with the customer to thoroughly document their expectations before the next sprint begins.","C":"Facilitate a session with the team and customer to clarify the project vision and ensure it is actively referenced during backlog refinement and sprint planning going forward.","D":"Escalate the misalignment to the project sponsor since the customer’s expectations were not adequately captured before development began."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Facilitate a session with the team and customer to clarify the project vision and ensure it is actively referenced during backlog refinement and sprint planning going forward. Explanation: The sprint review exists precisely to surface this kind of misalignment. The team built what was written, but what was written was not grounded in a shared understanding of what the project is trying to achieve. The project manager must use this moment to clarify the vision and establish it as an active reference in every subsequent ceremony, not just a document from kickoff. Why the other options are incorrect: Logging the gap as a defect and scheduling rework treats the symptom without addressing the cause. If the vision is not actively reinforced going forward, the next sprint is likely to produce the same misalignment on different features. A requirements workshop sounds thorough but the issue is not missing documentation; it is a vision misalignment. Escalating to the sponsor bypasses the project manager’s responsibility to facilitate vision alignment. The sprint review is the appropriate mechanism for surfacing exactly this kind of disconnect, and the PM should act on it directly.","id":"book-mindset-12","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"},{"n":13,"q":"A project manager observes that during technical design sessions, the team consistently avoids challenging each other’s ideas. Engineers are reluctant to voice disagreements and suboptimal decisions are being made as a result. The project manager wants to change this dynamic before it further affects the quality of technical outcomes. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Establish explicit norms that encourage team members to respectfully challenge ideas and express disagreement, framing productive conflict as a tool for reaching better decisions.","B":"Assign a rotating devil’s advocate role to one team member per session so opposing views are always present.","C":"Raise the pattern with the team lead in a one-on-one conversation and ask them to encourage more honest discussion going forward.","D":"Accept the current dynamic since the team appears cohesive and introducing conflict could damage working relationships."},"ans":"A","exp":"A. Establish explicit norms Explanation: Conflict avoidance that prevents honest technical debate is not team cohesion. It is a symptom that people do not feel safe to disagree. The project manager must create an environment with clear, shared norms where disagreement is not just permitted but recognized as essential to producing good decisions. This creates the conditions for productive conflict rather than suppressed frustration. Why the other options are incorrect: A devil’s advocate role artificially assigns the job of disagreement to one person rather than building a culture where everyone feels safe to contribute honestly. It treats the symptom rather than the underlying cause. Addressing the pattern only with the team lead narrows the intervention to one channel and leaves the rest of the team without clear permission or norms to challenge ideas. The project manager should establish team-wide expectations directly. Accepting the dynamic because the team appears cohesive misreads conflict avoidance as harmony. When suboptimal decisions are the outcome of suppressed disagreement, the PM has an obligation to address it.","id":"book-mindset-13","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":14,"q":"A project manager is overseeing the replacement of an enterprise data system. During execution, a vendor submits an invoice $1.2 million above the contracted ceiling, citing scope additions they claim were verbally authorized. The project manager has no record of these authorizations, and the disputed amount is well beyond the project manager’s spending authority. What should the project manager do?","opts":{"A":"Reject the invoice outright, notify the vendor the disputed additions will not be paid, and continue with the next project milestone.","B":"Verify the contract terms, document the discrepancy, and escalate to the appropriate governance authority for formal review and decision.","C":"Approve the portions of the invoice supported by documented scope and ask the vendor to resubmit the balance through a formal change request.","D":"Ask the project team and vendor to jointly reconstruct what was verbally discussed before any escalation takes place."},"ans":"B","exp":"B. Explanation: Verbal claims are not sufficient authorization for scope additions, and an amount this far above the contracted ceiling and the PM’s spending authority cannot be resolved at the execution level. The project manager must verify the contract terms, document the discrepancy, and route it to the authority with power to approve or contest it. Unauthorized scope and cost changes must not be approved, paid, or normalized without that formal review. Why the other options are incorrect: Unilaterally rejecting the invoice commits the organization to a contractual position without involving the people who have the authority to make that determination. Approving the documented portions and routing the rest through a change request partially normalizes the unauthorized additions before any formal review has occurred. The entire invoice needs to go through the governance path, not just the contested portion. Asking the team and vendor to reconstruct verbal conversations delays escalation and implicitly treats verbal authorization as a legitimate basis for payment. The absence of documented approvals is itself the issue, and investigating informally before escalating misses the point of formal contract governance.","id":"book-mindset-14","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Predictive"},{"n":15,"q":"An agile project team is planning the next sprint. The product backlog includes a highcomplexity feature from marketing that is expected to produce significant revenue, alongside several smaller usability improvements users have been requesting. The product owner asks the project manager for guidance on what to prioritize. What should the project manager advise?","opts":{"A":"Prioritize the smaller usability improvements since they carry less complexity and will demonstrate more consistent delivery velocity.","B":"Prioritize the high-complexity feature since it was explicitly requested by marketing and represents a high-visibility stakeholder need.","C":"Advise the product owner that prioritization is their decision to make based on an assessment of business value, and offer to facilitate the discussion rather than direct the outcome.","D":"Recommend splitting sprint capacity evenly between the high-complexity feature and the usability improvements to balance competing stakeholder needs."},"ans":"C","exp":"C. Advise the product owner that prioritization is their decision to make based on an assessment of business value, and offer to facilitate the discussion rather than direct the outcome. Explanation: Backlog prioritization belongs to the product owner. The project manager’s role is to support and facilitate the decision-making process, not to make the decision or steer it toward a preferred outcome. When asked for guidance, the PM reinforces the product owner’s ownership of the decision and offers facilitation rather than direction. Why the other options are incorrect: Recommending the smaller items based on velocity implications inserts the PM’s judgment about delivery efficiency into a business prioritization decision that belongs to the product owner. Velocity is a planning input, not the driver of backlog priority. Recommending the marketing feature based on stakeholder visibility also substitutes the PM’s assessment for the product owner’s business analysis. The PM does not have the organizational context to decide which items deliver the most value. Splitting capacity evenly is a compromise that may not reflect actual business value. Backlog prioritization should be driven by value, not by stakeholder equity or capacity distribution formulas.","id":"book-mindset-15","source":"PMP Exam Prep Simplified (2026)","chapter":"PMP Mindset Review","domain":"People","approach":"Agile"}];
